2026-09-29T04:12:44.493152Z [] {'source_reference_id': 'RFT-12634696798', 'source_id': 'buynsw', 'source_url': 'https://buy.nsw.gov.au/prcOpportunity/4F6C8C44-55F7-4292-B82094419A56A56D', 'title': 'Vivid Sydney Technical Support Services', 'issuing_agency': 'Destination NSW (DNSW)', 'category': 'Request for Tender', 'status': 'open', 'publish_date': '2026-09-16', 'closing_date': '2026-10-12', 'value_amount': None, 'value_currency': None, 'value_notes': 'Fixed fee for all requirements, to be provided by tenderer in Part C2 - Price Schedule for 2027, 2028, 2029, 2030 and 2031.', 'location': 'Sydney, New South Wales', 'description': 'Destination NSW (DNSW) is seeking a single contractor to provide comprehensive technical support services for the Vivid Sydney festival for 2027 and 2028, with three optional one-year extensions. The scope includes the management, maintenance, transportation, installation, programming, operation, and removal of approximately 40 annual Light Walk installations, projections, ambient lighting treatments, and DNSW-owned digital assets such as up to 70 Digital Lightboxes, 50 LED Screens, 33 HD VMS Boards, 115m LED Pool Fence, and 14 Digital Kiosks. Services span pre-event preparation (January-May), event delivery (May-June), and post-event assessment (June-July) across multiple Sydney precincts including Circular Quay, The Rocks, Barangaroo, and Darling Harbour.\n\nThe selected contractor will be responsible for providing all necessary suitably qualified, accredited, and licensed technical personnel, including a Technical Project Administrator, two Specialist Technical Staff, two to eight Technical Staff, and a minimum of two Asset and Event Technologies Technical Staff. The contractor must also supply two vehicles (one 1-tonne van, one 2-3 tonne flatbed truck) and all required equipment. Key qualifications include NSW Construction Industry White Card, High Risk Work Licences (Forklift, EWP), and Electrical Test and Tag. The contractor will manage all personnel logistics, WHS compliance, and reporting. Power generation and distribution services are explicitly excluded from this tender and will be procured separately by DNSW.\n\nThe Request for Tender (RFT-12634696798) was issued on 16 September 2026 and closes on 12 October 2026 at 3:00pm AEST. A non-mandatory supplier briefing was held on 23 September 2026, and the deadline for clarification questions was 28 September 2026. Contract award is targeted for mid-November 2026, with commencement mid-to-late November 2026. Vivid Sydney 2027 live event dates are 28 May to 19 June, with bump-in starting around 10 May and bump-out concluding around 27 June. Tenderers must acknowledge Addendum 1, which includes an updated draft agreement, and provide an SME and Local Participation Plan. Non-price criteria account for 70% of the evaluation, with a 50% pass threshold for each criterion. All communication must be directed to Melanie Chong.', 'summary_headline': 'Destination NSW seeks technical support services for Vivid Sydney light installations and digital assets.', 'contact_name': 'Melanie Chong', 'contact_email': 'melanie.chong@dnsw.com.au', 'contact_phone': None, 'lodgment_address': 'www.buy.nsw.gov.au', 'documents': [{'file_name': 'buy-nsw-tenders-terms-and-conditions-2024.pdf', 'file_type': 'pdf', 'storage_uri': 'gs://tenderai-dev-documents/tenders/buynsw/RFT-12634696798/bd537c4cc2756444-buy-nsw-tenders-terms-and-conditions-2024.pdf', 'extracted_text': ' \n \n1 \n \n \nbuy NSW Tenders module \nGlobal terms and conditions \n \n \nJuly 2024 \n \nGeneral \n1. In using the buy NSW Tenders module you:  \na. agree to terms and conditions contained herein and on buy.nsw.gov.au. \nb. agree that any information you provide is true, accurate, complete, current and not \nmisleading to the best of your knowledge and belief. \nc. agree to act in a way that is ethical, reasonable, with integrity, and complies with \napplicable laws when using the site. \nLodging a response \n2. Responses must be submitted electronically on the buy NSW Tenders module by a registered \nuser of buy NSW. \na. Responses (including all supporting information, if any) must be fully received by the \nclosing date and time. Note: The server date and time will be considered the correct date \nand time for the purposes of tenders closing. All information provided in the Response \nmust be in writing and in English. \nb. Incomplete or corrupted responses that cannot be effectively evaluated may be declined \nor not accepted. \nChanges to an opportunity \n3. An Opportunity may be amended before the closing date and time by issuing an amendment \nnotice. The amendment notice becomes part of the Opportunity.  \n4. It is the responsibility of all respondents: \na. to verify if an amendment notice has been issued prior to the closing date and time by \nchecking buy.nsw.gov.au, \nb. if necessary, respond to the amendment notice even if a Response has already been \nsubmitted by the Respondent.  \nQuestion and answers \n5. The buy nsw Tenders module includes question and answer functionality. You can submit a \nquestion to clarify tender requirements. Unless an amendment is issued the answer provided by \nthe Buyer is not considered part of the Opportunity. \n Use of responses \n6. In lodging a Response, you agree that the information provided: \n\na. can be used, shared and copied (including providing access to any third parties) for \nany purpose related to this Opportunity, and for internal procurement purposes. \nb. can be made available, on request, to any NSW government agency. This includes \ninformation dealing with the Respondent?s performance on any prior contract that has \nbeen awarded. Such information may be used by the recipient NSW Government \nagency for reasons including, but not limited to, the assessment of the suitability for \npre-qualification, selective tender lists, expressions of interest or the award of a \ncontract. \nc. is communication falling within section 30 of the Defamation Act 2005 (NSW). You shall \nhave no claim against the Principal and the State of New South Wales in respect of \nany matter arising out of the provision or receipt of such information, including any \nclaim for loss arising out of the communication. \nData security \n7. We maintain appropriate physical, technical, and administrative security to help prevent the loss, \nmisuse, unauthorised access, disclosure or modification of data on\u202fthe site. We do not accept any \nliability for the loss, misuse, unauthorised access, disclosure or modification of your data. \n8. To protect your data all Responses are encrypted at rest and in transit. Responses cannot be \ndecrypted until the close date and time. Responses can only be decrypted by the tender owner \nthrough the tender box opening process.  \nCorruption or unethical conduct \n9. A response may be declined or not considered if a Respondent, or any of its officers, employees, \nagents or sub-contractors is found to have: \na. offered any inducement, rebate, uncommercial/inflated pricing or reward to any \npublic servant or employee, agent or subcontractor of the Principal or the NSW \nGovernment in connection with this RFQ or the submitted Quotation; or \nb. committed corrupt conduct within the meaning of the Independent Commission \nAgainst Corruption Act 1988; or \nc. a record or alleged record of unethical behaviour. \n10. The Principal may, in its discretion, invite a relevant Respondent to provide written comments \nwithin a specified time before the Principal excludes the Respondent from the RFQ under clause \n10. \nNSW Government Procurement Policy Framework \n11. In submitting a response, the Respondent agrees to comply with the NSW Government \nProcurement Policy Framework (available at www.buy.nsw.gov.au).  \n12. If any Respondent fails to comply with these requirements, the Principal may take such failure \ninto account when considering the response and any subsequent response submitted by the \nRespondent and may result in the response being rejected. \nComplaints procedure \n13. It is the NSW Government?s objective to ensure that industry is given every opportunity to win \nGovernment contracts. Should any entity feel that it has been unfairly excluded from quotation \nor unfairly disadvantaged by the Agreement or the Statement of Requirements, it is invited to \nwrite to the Principal \n14. If a complaint is received concerning an alleged breach of an enforceable procurement provision \nunder Division 5 of Part 11 of the Public Works and Procurement Act 1912, this opportunity will be \nsuspended in accordance with the statutory requirements and invited Respondents will be \nnotified accordingly. \n\nBreaches to terms \n15. Respondents and users who breach the terms will be warned by the site?s administrator. \nSubsequent breaches will result in the user being blocked from accessing the site and legal \naction may be taken. \n16. If deemed necessary, breaches to terms may result in the termination of user accounts \n(including Supplier profiles), without liability for any loss. Once we end your user account, all \nrights granted to you in these terms will cease. \nJurisdiction \n17. The laws of New South Wales, Australia govern these terms. Any dispute must be heard in the \ncourts of New South Wales. \n18. If any of these terms are found to be invalid, illegal or unenforceable or for any reason by the \ncourts of New South Wales, the remaining terms will continue to apply in full. \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'Part-A---Vivid-Technical-Support-Services---Conditions-of-Tendering.pdf', 'file_type': 'pdf', 'storage_uri': 'gs://tenderai-dev-documents/tenders/buynsw/RFT-12634696798/4edb9ea3aa8a9189-Part-A---Vivid-Technical-Support-Services---Conditions-of-Tendering.pdf', 'extracted_text': " \n \nRequest for Tender Part A \na \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n \n \nDestination NSW (DNSW) \nRequest for Tender Part A \nConditions of Tendering (Goods & Services) \nVivid Sydney Technical Support Services \n(RFT-12634696798)   \nIssue Date: Wednesday 16 September 2026 \nClosing Date: Monday 12 October 2026 \nClosing Time: 3:00pm AEST \nLodgement: www.buy.nsw.gov.au \n \n \n\n \nRequest for Tender Part A \n2 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nCONTACT OFFICER \nFor the purposes of this RFT, all inquiries should be directed to the Contact Officer. \nContact Name:  \nMelanie Chong \nContact Email:  \nmelanie.chong@dnsw.com.au \nAny information given to a respondent to clarify any aspect of this RFT will also be given to all other \nrespondents if in the opinion of DNSW it would be unfair not to do so. \n \nSUPPLIER BRIEFING  \nA briefing will be held on the date, time, and place below. The Contact Officer and Subject Matter Experts will \nbe available at that time to answer any queries regarding this RFT and the RFT procurement process in \ngeneral.  \nAttendance at the briefing is not mandatory but strongly encouraged.  \nNo more than two persons from each Respondent organisation is permitted to attend the briefing.  Names of \npersons attending the briefing must be emailed to the Contact Officer at least one working day prior. The \nlink to the briefing will be shared to the attendees closer to the briefing time. \nLocation: \nMicrosoft Teams \nDate:  \nWednesday 23 September 2026 \n \nTime:  \n11am AEST  \n  \n \n\n \n \nRequest for Tender Part A \n3 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n1 \nIntroduction \n1.1 \nThis Tender is issued by The Crown in right of the State of New South Wales, acting through \nDestination NSW (DNSW). \n1.2 \nDNSW seeks responses from suppliers who wish to supply the Goods/Services as summarised \nbelow: \nBrief Description of Services Required \nVivid Sydney is a large-scale, multi-day festival \ndelivered across multiple locations, and involves \nthe coordinated installation, commissioning, \noperation, maintenance and removal of event \ninfrastructure, installations and supporting \nservices during bump-in, live event and bump-out. \nDestination NSW is seeking a suitably qualified \nand experienced Technical Support Services \nContractor to support the safe delivery of Vivid \nSydney?s Light Installations, Digital Assets and \nEvent Technologies to provide the services for \nVivid Sydney 2027 and 2028, with three one-year \nextension options for Vivid Sydney 2029, 2030 \nand 2031.  \nThe scope of services includes management, \nmaintenance, transportation, installation, \nprogramming, operation, and removal of Light \nWalk installations, projections, ambient lighting \ntreatments, DNSW-owned digital assets and other \nevent technologies. The Contractor is responsible \nfor provision and management of suitably \nexperienced and qualified / accredited / licenced \ntechnical personnel, vehicles and equipment for \nthe safe and successful delivery of the services. \nProposed No. of Suppliers \n \nThe intention of DNSW is to appoint a single \nContractor. \nProposed Agreement \nDNSW Contract of Agreement. \nProposed Agreement Term \n \n2-years plus three 1-year options exercisable at \nthe sole discretion of DNSW. \n1.3 \nFurther details of the Goods/Services required are provided in Part B ? Statement of Requirements. \n1.4 \nThis procurement is covered by the Enforceable Procurement Provisions Directions 2019 (EPP \nDirections). A copy of the EPP Directions is available at https://arp.nsw.gov.au/pbd-2019-05-\nenforceable-procurement-provisions. \n2 \nRFT Structure \n2.1 \nRFT Documents \n2.1.1 \nThis RFT comprises four Parts as follows:  \nPart A - Conditions of Tendering  \n\n \n \nRequest for Tender Part A \n4 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nThis provides the terms, conditions and processes governing the sourcing phase of the RFT. \nPart B - Statement of Requirements  \nA detailed description of the Goods/Services to be provided to the Respondents including \nspecification, service levels and performance framework. Taken together with the accepted parts of \nthe Response, it will form part of the Contract. \nPart C - Response Documents \nThis is the responses prepared by the Supplier to enable DNSW to evaluate the Responses. The \nresponse may be in a form prescribed by DNSW. \nPart D ? Proposed Agreement  \nThe Contract will be based on the terms and conditions set out in the Proposed Agreement issued \nwith this RFT. \n3 \nEligibility of Response \n3.1 \nLegal Entity of Respondent \n3.1.1 \nResponses must be submitted by a legal entity or, if a joint response, by legal entities, with the \ncapacity to enter into a binding agreement. DNSW will only enter into an Agreement with such legal \nentity or entities. \n3.1.2 \nDNSW may ask a respondent to provide evidence of its legal status or capacity to enter into a binding \nAgreement. If responses from entities propose to contract in their capacity as trustees, such evidence \nmay include copies of the relevant trust deeds. Any evidence requested is to be provided within 5 \nworking days of the request. \n3.2 \nFinancial Capability of the Respondent \n3.2.1 \nDNSW reserves the right to reject any response if DNSW judges the respondent does not have the \nappropriate financial or management capability or capacity.  \n3.2.2 \nWhere DNSW forms the view that the respondent does not have the appropriate management or \nfinancial capability, DNSW reserves the right to make acceptance of any response conditional upon \nthe respondent entering into a bank, parent company or personal guarantee, or an unconditional \nperformance bond in a form satisfactory to DNSW. \n3.3 \nABN Requirements \n3.3.1 \nDNSW will not enter into an Agreement with a company that does not have an Australian Business \nNumber and is not registered for GST. Normally, respondents must be registered for GST and state \ntheir ABN in their response. \n3.3.2 \nRespondents that do not have an ABN and/or are not registered for GST, such as respondents that \nare commencing business in Australia, may be considered at DNSW?s discretion if the respondent \ndemonstrates that it will obtain an ABN and GST registration before entering into an Agreement with \nDNSW. Such respondents must state how and when they intend to obtain an ABN and register for \nGST in their response. \n4 \nResponse Preparation \n4.1 \nRespondent to inform itself \n4.1.1 \nBefore submitting its response, a respondent must: \n\n \n \nRequest for Tender Part A \n5 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \na) \nExamine all information relevant to the risks and contingencies and other circumstances \nhaving an effect on its Response; and \nb) \nSatisfy itself: \ni) that the response is accurate, true and correct; and \nii) that the response is financially and practically viable for the respondent. \n5 \nResponse Process \n5.1 \nProvisional Program  \n5.1.1 \nBelow is DNSW?s provisional RFT program. DNSW may, at its absolute discretion, amend the \nprovisional RFT program. \nPublished \n16 September 2026   \nClosed \n12 October 2026 at 3pm ADST  \nBriefing (optional) \nWednesday 23 September 2026 (11am AEST) \nClarification Question Deadline (optional) \n28 September 2025 3pm (AEST)  \nSupplier Interview / Presentations (optional) w/c 19 October 2026 ? if required \nEvaluation Complete \nw/c 26 October 2026 \nContract Award \nMid-November 2026 \nContract Commencement Date \nMid-late November 2026 \n5.2 \nContact Officer \n5.2.1 \nRespondents should refer all requests for information or advice regarding this RFT to the Contact \nOfficer as per PART A of the RFT. \n5.2.2 \nAny information given to a respondent to clarify any aspect of this RFT will also be given to all other \nrespondents via the issuing of an addenda, if in DNSW?s opinion the information would unfairly \nfavour the inquiring respondent over other respondents. \n5.3 \nConformity of Responses \n5.3.1 \nDNSW seeks Conforming responses. \n5.3.2 \nNon-Conforming responses may be excluded from the RFT process without further consideration at \nDNSW?s discretion. \n5.4 \nAlternative Responses \n5.4.1 \nRespondents may, if they choose, submit an Alternative Response. Alternative Responses will only be \nconsidered if submitted in conjunction with a Conforming Response. An Alternative Response must \nbe clearly marked ?Alternative Response?. \n5.5 \nPrescribed Form of Response \n5.5.1 \nThe Response, including any Alternative Response, must comprise completed PART C1 and C2 and \nany attachments to PART C, as may be necessary. Any attachments should be labelled to identify \nthose sections of the RFT to which they relate. \n\n \n \nRequest for Tender Part A \n6 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n5.6 \nSubmission of Responses \n5.6.1 \nResponses and other information provided in the response are to be in writing and in English. \nReferences to costs or prices must be in Australian dollars. \n5.6.2 \nRespondents must complete all of PART C1 and C2 of the RFT, as directed and must not amend any of \nthe questions provided. \n5.6.3 \nRespondents should notify the Contact Officer in writing on or before the closing date and closing \ntime if they find any discrepancy, error or omission in this RFT. \n5.6.4 \nAll responses must be provided electronically, in either Microsoft Word or PDF forms. \n5.6.5 \nWhen submitting an electronic response with supporting items: \na) \nThe complete response, including the supporting items must be submitted by the closing \ndate and closing time, and \nb) \nSupporting items provided by the respondent in support of its response but excluding \nprinted material, should be clearly designated as supporting items to the RFT to which they \nrelate. \n5.6.6 \nRespondents must ensure that all Excel or Word attachments can be opened and viewed by \nMicrosoft Excel 2010 or Microsoft Word 2010.  \n5.7 \nResponse Lodgement \n5.7.1 \nResponses must be fully received by the Closing Date and Closing Time as per this PART A. \n5.7.2 \nResponses must be lodged via the lodgement location listed on the cover page of this PART A.  \n5.7.3 \nResponses should be submitted in the original format that has been provided. i.e. Word document or \nExcel format \n5.7.4 \nRespondents that choose to submit additional attachments to the Request should keep any price and \nnon-price components in separate documents/files \n5.8 \nElectronic Responses \n5.8.1 \nA response submitted electronically will be treated in accordance with the Electronic Transactions \nAct 2000 (NSW), and given the appropriate level of confidentiality, probity and attention. \n5.8.2 \nSignatures are not required for an electronic response. A respondent must ensure that a response is \nauthorised by the person or persons who may do so on behalf of the respondent and appropriately \nidentify the person and indicate the person?s approval of the information communicated. \n5.8.3 \nElectronically submitted responses may be made corrupt or incomplete, for example by computer \nviruses. DNSW may decline to consider for acceptance a response that cannot be effectively \nevaluated because it is incomplete or corrupt. Respondents must note that: \na) \nTo reduce the likelihood of viruses, a respondent must not include any macros, applets, or \nexecutable code or files in a response, and  \nb) \nElectronically submitted files are free from viruses by checking the files with an up to date \nvirus-checking program before submission. \n5.8.4 \nDNSW will not be responsible in any way for any loss, damage or corruption of electronically \nsubmitted responses. \n5.8.5 \nRespondents may break down the lodgement of large responses into smaller packages if clearly \nidentified e.g. package 1 of 3; 2 of 3; 3 of 3. \n5.8.6 \nIf a respondent provides multiple lodgements, the latest response received will be the response to be \nevaluated unless the respondent provides clear directions to whether the lodgement is: \na) \nan alternative response; \nb) \nsupporting information;  \n\n \n \nRequest for Tender Part A \n7 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nc) \na further part of a response that has had previous lodgement. \n5.9 \nLate Responses \n5.9.1 \nLate responses will not be considered, except where DNSW is satisfied that the integrity and \ncompetitiveness of the process is not compromised. \n5.9.2 \nDNSW shall not penalise any interested party whose response is received late if the delay is due \nsolely to mishandling by DNSW. \n5.10 Extension of Closing Date and Closing Time \n5.10.1 DNSW may, in its discretion, extend the Closing Date and Closing Time. \n5.11 Corruption or Unethical Conduct \n5.11.1 Respondents must comply with the requirements of DNSW Statement of Business Ethics, and must \ndisclose any conflicts of interests in PART C1. \n5.11.2 If a respondent, or any of its officers, employees, agents or sub-contractors is found to have: \na) \nOffered any inducement or reward to any public servant or employee, agent or subcontractor \nof DNSW or the NSW Government in connection with this RFT or the submitted Response; \nb) \nCommitted corrupt conduct in the meaning of the Independent Commission Against \nCorruption Act 1988; or \nc) \nA record or alleged record of unethical behaviour; or has not complied with the requirements \nof DNSW Statement of Business Ethics \nThis may result in the response not receiving further consideration. \n5.11.3 DNSW may, in its discretion, invite a respondent to provide written comments within a specified \ntime before DNSW excludes the respondent on this basis. \n5.11.4 If DNSW becomes aware of improper conflict of interest by a successful respondent after the \nAgreement has been executed, then DNSW reserves the right to terminate the Agreement. \n5.12 Supplier Code of Conduct  \n5.12.1 In submitting its response, the respondent signifies agreement to comply with the Supplier Code of \nConduct. \n5.12.2 Failure to comply with the Supplier Code of Conduct may be taken into account by DNSW when \nconsidering the respondents response or any subsequent response and may result in the response \nbeing passed over. \n5.13 Goods and Services Procurement Policy Framework \n5.13.1 In submitting its tender, the respondent signifies agreement to comply with the Goods and Services \nProcurement Policy Framework available at:  https://buy.nsw.gov.au/policy-library/policies/procurement-\npolicy-framework. \n5.13.2  Failure to comply with the Procurement Policy Framework may be taken into account by DNSW \nwhen considering the respondent?s tender or any subsequent tender and may result in the tender \nbeing passed over. \n5.14 Addenda to RFT \n5.14.1 If, for any reason DNSW, at its sole discretion, requires the RFT to be amended before the Closing \nDate and Closing Time, an addendum will be issued. \n5.14.2 In each case, an Addendum becomes part of the RFT. \n\n \n \nRequest for Tender Part A \n8 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n5.14.3 During the response period DNSW may issue addenda altering the RFT In such cases, it is the \nobligation of the respondent to verify if any addenda were issued prior to closing date, even if a \nresponse has already been submitted. \n5.15 Respondents Costs \n5.15.1 The respondent acknowledges that DNSW will not be liable to it for any expenses or costs incurred \nby it as a result of its participation in this RFT, including where the RFT has been discontinued. \n5.16 Ownership of Responses \n5.16.1 All responses become the property of DNSW on submission. \n5.16.2 DNSW may make copies of the responses for any purpose related to this RFT. \n5.17 Discontinuance of Response Process \n5.17.1 Where DNSW determines that proceeding with the RFT would not be in the public interest, DNSW \nreserves the right to discontinue the RFT process at any point, without making a determination \nregarding acceptance or rejection of responses. \n5.18 Variations to Responses \n5.18.1 At any time after the Closing Date of responses and before any response received in response to this \nRFT is accepted, a respondent may, subject to clause 5.17.2, vary its response: \na) \nby providing DNSW with further information by way of explanation or clarification; \nb) \nby correcting a mistake or anomaly; or \n5.18.2 Such a variation may be made either: \na) \nat the request of DNSW, or \nb) \nwith the consent of DNSW at the request of the respondent; but only if it appears to DNSW \nreasonable in the circumstances to allow the respondent to provide the information or \ncorrect the mistake or anomaly \n5.18.3 If a response is varied in accordance with clause 5.17.1, DNSW will provide all other respondents \nwhose responses have similar characteristics with the opportunity to vary their responses in a \nsimilar way. \n5.18.4 A variation of a response under clause 5.17.1 will not be permitted if in DNSW?s view: \na) \nit would substantially alter the original response; or \nb) \nit would result in the revising or expanding of a response in a way that would give a \nrespondent an unfair advantage over other respondents. \n6 \nEvaluation Process \n6.1  \nEvaluation Criteria \n6.1.1 \nResponses will be assessed against the evaluation criteria listed in the table below. \nPass / Fail Criteria \n \na. Compliance with insurance requirements  \nb. Tender is lodged by the Closing Time. \nc. Respondent is a Legal Entity (with ABN and Registered for GST).  \nd. Respondent acknowledges and will comply with applicable NSW \nProcurement Board Directions as defined. \n\n \n \nRequest for Tender Part A \n9 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \ne. Respondent?s compliance with Modern Slavery Laws. \nf. \nRespondent acknowledges and will comply with the NSW Supplier Code \nof Conduct. \ng. Respondent acknowledges and accepts Part A ? Conditions of Tendering.  \nh. Respondent acknowledges and accepts Part B ? Statement of \nRequirements. \ni. \nRespondent acknowledges and accepts Part D ? Contract of Engagement \nj. \nPart C1 and C2 - Tender Response submitted as per the prescribed forms. \nk. There is Authorisation of the Tender Response. \nPrice Criteria \na. Price. \nNon-Price Criteria \n \na. Demonstrated experience and successful performance in providing \nsimilar services especially with large-scale, multi-day major events \nacross multiple sites \nb. Demonstrated experience delivering technical services for large-scale \noutdoor events and festivals, including lighting, power distribution, \nrigging, electrical installations, digital assets, LED screen technologies, \nelectrical safety compliance, and event infrastructure installation and \nmaintenance.   \nc. Human and infrastructure resource capacity including qualifications, \nskills, experience and proposed extent of the respondent?s key personnel \ninvolvement including sub-contractor/s.  \nd. Demonstrated strong communication and project management skills, \nexcellent attention to detail, and experience developing \nand maintaining internal and external relationships. \ne. SME participation. \nf. \nSocial procurement objectives inclusive of NSW Government's economic, \nethical, environmental, social priorities.   \n6.1.2 \nInformation supplied by the respondent in PART C1 and C2 will contribute to the assessment against \neach criterion. Respondents are advised to respond clearly to all the evaluation criteria listed in this \nRFT. DNSW may seek to clarify any information provided by a respondent in PART C1 or C2. \n6.1.3 \nResponses that do not include a fully completed PART C1 and C2, in particular those responses \nwhich do not contain sufficient information to permit a proper evaluation to be conducted, or \nelectronic responses that cannot be effectively evaluated because the file has become corrupt, may \nbe excluded from the response process without further consideration at DNSW?s discretion. \n6.1.4 \nDNSW may assess an Alternative Response against the evaluation criteria where submitted with a \nConforming Response. \n6.15 \nDNSW may cull, set aside, or exclude a Response from further evaluation in which DNSW has \nevaluated and documented the response as:  \na) non-compliant, non-conforming or has not met the mandatory requirements of the Request;  \nb) failing to satisfy one or more of the evaluation criteria or criterions by scoring below a pass \npercentage of 50%;  \nc) not ranked amongst the top-ranked Responses or is ranked as not being an economically \nadvantageous Response to which the DNSW?s evaluation team decide not to shortlist for further due \ndiligence exercises, or include in a shortlisting;  \n\n \n \nRequest for Tender Part A \n10 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nd) an operational risk or financial risk or reputational risk or legal risk or work health and safety risk to \nDNSW as part of its evaluation, and/or as noted as part of due diligence exercises or accepting the \nResponse.  \n6.16 \nPrices are requested in Australian Dollars and exclusive of GST and, where one or more of  \n \nthe following apply, in the foreign currency of the original supplier / manufacturer where  \n \ngoods or services are sourced or produced offshore: \na)  Where the vendor is a supplier / manufacturer based outside of Australia \nb) Where the vendor is a subsidiary of a supplier / manufacturer based outside of Australia \nc) Where the vendor is a reseller of a supplier / manufacturer based outside of Australia \n  \nThe vendor is required to highlight any submission where the ultimate price is dependent on  \n \nor subject to an adjustment e.g. relating to foreign exchange rates, commodities, interest  \n \nrates or inflation. \n6.2 \nPresentations and Site Inspections \n6.2.1 \nDNSW may in its discretion, and as part of the evaluation process, invite any or some of the \nrespondents to an interview process or ask them to make personal presentations or conduct site \ninspections regarding its response. The respondent shall undertake this at its own cost. \n6.2.2 \nReceiving an interview, presentation or site inspection from a respondent in no way represents a \ncommitment by DNSW to accept any aspect of their response. \n6.2.3 \nAll information obtained during the course of an interview, presentation or site inspection may be \ntaken into consideration in the evaluation of responses. \n6.3 \nDue Diligence \n6.3.1 \nDNSW may in its discretion, and as part of the evaluation process, undertake any due diligence in \naddition to the Evaluation Criteria listed that it considers appropriate including, but not limited to, \nreference checks, financial capacity checks through a third party, clarifications, risk assessments, \nlegal checks etc. \n6.4 \nAcceptance or Rejection of Responses \n6.4.1 \nResponses will remain open for acceptance by DNSW for a period of six months from the Closing \nDate and Closing Time. \n6.4.2 \nIf DNSW rejects all the responses received it may invite fresh responses based on the same or \ndifferent criteria (specifications and details contained in Alternative Responses will not be used as \nthe basis for the calling of new responses). \n6.4.3 \nAny unsuccessful respondent may request a formal debriefing. Any request for a debrief be \nrequested within two weeks of the respondent being notified of their response being unsuccessful. \nThe debrief will be an opportunity for the respondent to understand how their response performed \nagainst the evaluation criteria. The debrief will not be an opportunity for the respondent to debate \nthe outcome of the evaluation or discuss other respondent?s submission. \n6.5 \nPost Tender Negotiations \n6.5.1 \nBefore making any determination as to acceptance or rejection of respondents, DNSW may, at its \ndiscretion, elect to conduct limited negotiations with preferred respondents, including those who \nhave submitted Alternative Tenders or who have submitted Conforming Tenders, to mutually \nimprove outcomes.  \n6.5.2 \nIf DNSW considers that none of the tenders are fully acceptable either because of non-conformance \nor because they do not represent sufficient value for money, but considers that full conformity is \nachievable, negotiations may be conducted with the respondent that submitted the most conforming \n\n \n \nRequest for Tender Part A \n11 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \ntender based on the evaluation criteria. The purpose of the negotiations will be advised by DNSW \nand made clear to the participants before the commencement of negotiation. \n6.5.3 \nDNSW may at its absolute discretion elect to conduct post tender negotiations with more than one \n(1) respondent in the event that it decides that the closeness of the tenders or timing constraints \nwarrants doing so. \n6.6 \nExchange of Information between Government Agencies \n6.5.1 \nLodgement of a response will itself be an authorisation by the respondent to DNSW to make \navailable, on request, to any NSW government agency information, including but not limited to, \ninformation dealing with the respondent?s performance on any contract/agreement that may be \nawarded. Such information may be used by the recipient NSW Government agency for assessment of \nsuitability for pre-qualification, selective response lists, expressions of interest or the award of a \nfacility use agreement or termination of a facility use agreement. \n6.6.2 \nThe provision of the information by DNSW to any other NSW Government agency is agreed by the \nrespondent to be a communication falling within section 30 of the Defamation Act 2005 (NSW), and \nthe respondent shall have no claim against DNSW and the State of New South Wales in respect of any \nmatter arising out of the provision or receipt of such information, including any claim for loss to the \nrespondent arising out of the communication \n6.6.3 \nIn the avoidance of doubt, information that may be collected, exchanged and used in accordance with \nthis provision includes ?personal information? about the respondent for the purposes of the Privacy \nand Personal Information Protection Act 1998.  Lodgement of a response will be an authorisation by \nthe respondent to DNSW to collect such information from third parties, and to use and exchange \nsuch information in accordance with this clause.  \n7 \nNotification of Short-listing \n7.1 \nNo respondents shall be deemed to have been short-listed until the respondent has been notified of \nsuch by DNSW in writing. \n8 \nDisclosure Information \n8.1 \nFollowing DNSW?s decision, all respondents will be notified in writing of the outcome of their RFT \nResponses. \n9 \nComplaints Procedure \n9.1 \nIt is the NSW Government?s objective to ensure that industry is given every opportunity to win \nGovernment contracts. Should any entity feel that it has been unfairly excluded from responding or \nunfairly disadvantaged by the Conditions in the RFT/RFP/RFQ, or the Statement of Requirements in \nPart B, it is invited to write to:  \n \nExecutive Director, Corporate Services, CFO/CPO \nDestination NSW \nLevel 2, 88 Cumberland Street \nThe Rocks, Sydney, NSW, 2000 \n\n \n \nRequest for Tender Part A \n12 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n10  \nModern Slavery  \n10.1 Definitions \n10.1.1 Modern Slavery \n1. \nmeans any conduct that constitutes or would constitute a modern slavery offence, being an \noffense described in Schedule 2 of the Modern Slavery Act 2018 (NSW), or an offence of \nattempting or incitement to commit an offence described in Schedule 2 of the Modern Slavery \nAct 2018 (NSW). \n2. \nincludes any conduct that constitutes or would constitute an offence under any of the Modern \nSlavery Laws as amended from time to time, including an offence of attempting or incitement \nto commit such an offence; and \n3. \nincludes conduct engaged in elsewhere than in New South Wales that, if it occurred in New \nSouth Wales, would constitute a modern slavery offence under paragraphs (a) or (b). \n  \nModern Slavery Laws means: \n? \nthe Modern Slavery Act 2018 (Cth); \n? \nthe Modern Slavery Act 2018 (NSW); \n? \nDivisions 270 and 271 of the Commonwealth Criminal Code; \n? \nsection 176(1A) of the Public Works and Procurement Act 1912 (NSW); \n? \nsection 438ZE of the Local Government Act 1993 (NSW); and \n1. \nany other laws, regulations, codes and international conventions aimed at combatting modern \nslavery, forced labour or human trafficking, from time to time in force in or ratified by Australia \nand, where relevant, in or by other jurisdictions in which the parties operate, each as amended \nfrom time to time. \n  \nreasonable steps means those steps that are reasonable in the circumstances to prevent,  \n \nidentify, mitigate and remedy modern slavery. In assessing whether steps are reasonable, the \n \nparties may refer to the NSW Anti-slavery Commissioner?s Guidance on Reasonable Steps  \n \nand related information and resources published by the Anti-slavery Commissioner.  \n10.2 Modern slavery contract clauses \n10.2.1 By lodging a Tender, the Respondent agrees that, if it is the successful Respondent, it may  be \nrequired to comply with modern slavery contract clauses in the contract entered into with the \nBuyer.   \n10.3 Price \n10.3.1 By submitting a Tender, the Respondent acknowledges and agrees that if it is awarded the Tender \nbased on the pricing or compensation information it has submitted in its tender documents, this \nsubmitted pricing or compensation will allow it to perform the contract without causing or \ncontributing to modern slavery, and to provide or enable an effective remedy to any modern slavery \nit does cause or to which it does contribute. \n10.4 Evaluation  \n10.4.1 Respondents will be evaluated to determine the Response which represents the best value   \nfor money to the Buyer. \n \n \n\n"}, {'file_name': 'Part-B---Vivid-Technical-Support-Services---Statement-of-Requirements.pdf', 'file_type': 'pdf', 'storage_uri': 'gs://tenderai-dev-documents/tenders/buynsw/RFT-12634696798/c1a5165197c716a2-Part-B---Vivid-Technical-Support-Services---Statement-of-Requirements.pdf', 'extracted_text': " \nRequest for Tender ? Part B \na \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nDestination NSW (DNSW) \nRequest for Tender ? Part B \nStatement of Requirements (Goods & Services) \nVivid Sydney Technical Support Services (RFT-12634696798)   \nRFT Closes 3PM Monday 12 October 2026 \n \n\n \nRequest for Tender ? Part B \n2 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n1 \nBackground and Overview of Requirements  \n1.1 \nVivid Sydney is an annual celebration of creativity, innovation and technology, which transforms Sydney \nfor 23 days and nights in May-June.  Vivid Sydney is Australia?s largest event and the Southern \nHemisphere's leading multi-artform festival, with program ?pillars? spanning Light, Music, Minds and Food. \nWith some of Australia and the globe?s most boundary-pushing artists, thought-leaders, musicians, and \nculinary talents, Sydney shined bright as a beacon of world-leading creativity, culture, and community. \n1.2 \nThe event requires specialist technical services for Vivid Sydney?s Light Walk installations, digital assets, \nprojection treatments, large-scale activations and associated event technologies across multiple \nprecincts. The services include the management, maintenance, transportation, installation, programming, \noperation and removal of DNSW-owned digital assets and event technologies. Power generation and \npower distribution services are excluded and will be procured separately.   \n1.3 \nThe services include the provision of appropriately experienced and licenced/qualified/accredited \ntechnical personnel, vehicles and equipment for Vivid Sydney 2027 and 2028 with two optional 1-year \nextensions for Vivid Sydney 2029, 2030 and 2031 at DNSW?s sole discretion.  \n1.4 \nThis procurement is intended to engage an experienced Contractor with the capability to deliver \nsafe, high-quality and seamlessly integrated services that meet the operational, technical and stakeholder \nrequirements of the program.  \n1.5 \nThe services are being sourced externally due to the specialist expertise, operational capability and large-\nscale event delivery experience required to successfully manage and deliver the Vivid Sydney program. \nThe program requires an integrated supplier with demonstrated technical support capability, supported \nby the resources and systems necessary to deliver a high-volume public event within the required \ntimeframe and operational standards.  \n1.6 \nThe Contractor must demonstrate previous experience delivering technical support services for large-\nscale, multi-day outdoor events and festivals across multiple sites. This includes proven capability to \nprovide and manage appropriately skilled, qualified and licensed personnel and to undertake effective \nproject and stakeholder management. \n1.7 \nIt is DNSW?s intention to award the resulting agreement to a single Contractor with the demonstrated \ncapability and capacity to deliver the full scope of services required for Technical Support Services.  \n2 \nScope  \n2.1 \nDNSW procures up to forty (40) light installations annually for Vivid Sydney which may consist of \ntemporary structures, prefabricated works, custom-built activations and conceptual projects requiring \ntechnical installation, programming and operational support. In addition, DNSW owns and manages a \nrange of digital assets, technical equipment and event technologies that are deployed across the event. \n2.2 \nThe scope of work includes the management, transportation, installation, programming, operation, \nmaintenance and removal of Light Walk installations, projections, ambient lighting treatments, \nDestination NSW-owned digital assets and other event technologies. The Contractor will deliver technical \nservices across all phases of event delivery and will ensure that all program items and assets are installed, \nprogrammed, maintained, operated and removed safely, efficiently, on time and within budget. \n2.3 \nThe Contractor is responsible for provision and management of suitably qualified/accredited/licenced and \nexperienced technical personnel and equipment to ensure the services are technically compliant and \ndelivered in accordance with relevant ISO Standards, Australian Standards, NSW legislation, Work Health \nand Safety Act and Regulations, Electrical safety requirements and industry best practice. \n\n \n \nRequest for Tender ? Part B \n3 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n2.4 \nThe Contractor shall provide suitably qualified, accredited, licensed and experienced personnel required \nto undertake the following services: \n? \nInstall, program and remove light installations and projection treatments \n? \nOperate specialized plant and equipment \n? \nDeliver technical support for event technologies \n? \nAssist with power distribution implementation and reticulation support \n? \nMaintain operational readiness throughout the event period. \n2.5 \nThe Contractor shall provide the following vehicles for the Contractor?s own operational use throughout \nthe contract period: \n? \nOne (1) 1-tonne large van \n? \nOne (1) 2-3 tonne flatbed truck (or equivalent) \n2.6 \nServices are divided into three stages and the tasks to be undertaken by the Contractor are described \nbelow: \n2.6.1 \nStage 1 Pre-Event Services are typically carried out from January ? May each year depending on the scope \nof works. The Contractor shall undertake the following services: \n? \nPre-Production and Asset Maintenance \n? \nTesting and commissioning of all digital assets \n? \nReplacement of damaged LED panels (where available) \n? \nSoftware updates and configuration \n? \nInstallation and activation of SIM cards \n? \nConnection to cloud-based management systems \n? \nRefurbishment of external asset surfaces where required \n? \nWarehouse inventory management \n? \nDigital asset reporting \n? \nWarehouse operational support \n? \nAsset preparation and packing for deployment. \n2.6.2 \nFor Stage 2 Event Delivery Services pertaining to DNSW owned assets and event technologies, the \nContractor shall be responsible for: \n? \nDeployment and Operations \n? \nCollection of all assets from DNSW storage facilities in Roseberry \n? \nPreparation for transportation \n? \nDelivery to event sites \n? \nInstallation, programming and commissioning \n? \nOngoing maintenance and fault response \n? \nRelocation of assets where required \n? \nRemoval and return of all assets following the event from a DNSW assets from a DNSW warehouse \nfacility in Rosebery and must be handled and returned in a safe, organised and documented manner.  \n? \nDNSW assets may include Vivid Sydney Asset Inventory, large Vivid Sydney LED Letters, small Vivid \nLED Letters, Digital Lightboxes (up to 70), LED Screens for Local Business Program (up to 50 Round), \n\n \n \nRequest for Tender ? Part B \n4 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nHD Variable Message Sign (VMS) Boards (up to 33), approximately 115m of LED Pool Fence \ninstallation and Digital Kiosks (up to 14). \n2.6.3 \nFor Stage 2 Event Delivery Services pertaining to Light Installations, the Contractor shall provide suitably \nqualified personnel, supervision and technical expertise required for: \n? \nTechnical support with installation and deinstallation of light installations \n? \nAssembly and construction of temporary activation infrastructure \n? \nLighting programming and commissioning \n? \nProjection and digital asset installation support \n? \nFault-finding and maintenance throughout the festival period \n? \nTechnical support and troubleshooting \n? \nInstallation of ambient lighting treatments \n? \nWindow augmentation and projection treatments \n? \nTechnical support for large-scale activations \n? \nEvent bump-in, live event operations and bump-out activities. \n2.6.4 \nFor Stage 3 Post Event Services, the Contractor shall provide the following services and deliverables: \n? \nAsset Assessment and Future Planning \n? \nDetailed assessment of all digital assets \n? \nCondition reporting \n? \nSpare parts reconciliation \n? \nRepair recommendations \n? \nProcurement analysis \n? \nLabour and repair cost estimates \n? \nRecommended maintenance schedules \n? \nAsset lifecycle reporting \n? \nFuture year budgeting estimates. \n2.7 \nTechnical Support Services Personnel Requirement \n2.7.1 \nTechnical Project Administrator  \n2.7.1.1 Provide a dedicated Technical Project Administrator responsible for the coordination, rostering, and \nallocation of work across the Technical Support Services team. The Technical Project Administrator \nmust possess demonstrated experience in administration, workforce scheduling, and rostering within a \ncomparable operational environment. \n2.7.1.2 From the commencement of the contract, the Technical Project Administrator will be required to \nattend regular pre-production planning meetings and act as the primary liaison between DNSW, the \nTechnical Support Services team, and DNSW?s appointed Power supplier. Responsibilities will include \ncoordinating the development, implementation, and ongoing management of the event power \ndistribution and reticulation plan to ensure seamless service delivery and operational integration. \n2.7.1.3 The scope shall also include all associated administrative services and costs relating to personnel \nmanagement, including staff briefings, scheduling and rostering, completion of required inductions \nand relevant training, coordination of personnel, and the completion and submission of the post-\nevent Supplier Sustainability Survey. \n\n \n \nRequest for Tender ? Part B \n5 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n2.7.2 \nSpecialist Technical Staff  \n2.7.2.1 Provide two (2) specialist technical staff/plant operators for bump-in, live event operations and bump-out \nperiods. Personnel must possess the following experience, and qualifications/licences/accreditations: \n? \nDemonstrated event and technical production experience \n? \nNSW Construction Industry White Card \n? \nHigh Risk Work Licence ? Forklift \n? \nHigh Risk Work Licence ? Elevated Work Platform \n? \nElectrical Test and Tag qualification \n2.7.2.2 A rotating roster covering day and night shifts shall be established in consultation with the Vivid Sydney \nTechnical Manager and Production Manager, Vivid Light. \n2.7.3 \nTechnical staff  \n2.7.3.1 Provide between two (2) and eight (8) suitably experienced technical personnel throughout bump-in, live \nevent operations and bump-out. Personnel must collectively demonstrate a range of \nqualifications/accreditations/licences and skills including: \n? \nNSW Construction Industry White Card \n? \nEvent and technical production experience \n? \nLighting systems and DMX programming \n? \nElectrical design and coding experience \n? \nLED screen systems and media server experience \n? \nConstruction and carpentry skills \n? \nElectrical installation knowledge \n? \nSafety compliance and testing protocols \n? \nRigging and working at heights experience \n2.7.4 \nAsset and Event Technologies Technical Staff \n2.7.4.1 Provide a minimum of two (2) specialist technicians for installation, operation and maintenance of \nDestination NSW digital assets throughout the event period. Responsibilities include: \n? \nInstallation and commissioning of digital assets \n? \nMonitoring and maintenance \n? \nProgramming updates \n? \nFault diagnosis and rectification \n? \nTechnical response and call-out services \n2.7.4.2 Personnel must possess qualifications/accreditations/licences and experience in: \n? \nNSW Construction Industry White Card \n? \nLighting systems and DMX programming \n? \nLED screens and associated software \n? \nElectrical design and coding \n? \nRigging and working at heights \n? \nEvent technology deployment \n\n \n \nRequest for Tender ? Part B \n6 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n2.8 \nResourcing Periods required for each role are as follows with technical services and indicative staff \nnumbers set out in Part C2: \n Y= Yes \nTechnical Project \nAdministrator  \nSpecialist \nTechnical Staff \nTechnical Staff  \nAsset and Event \nTechnologies \nTechnical Staff \n \nPre-Event period, November - May, up to 10 days + meetings \nY \n Y \nY \nY \nBump in period (up to 19 days) \nY \nY \nY \nY \nLive event period (23 consecutive nights) \nY \nY \nY \nY \nBump out period (up to 6 days) \nY \nY \nY \nY \nPost Event Services \nY \nY \nY \nY \nPost-Event reporting \nY \n  \n  \n \n2.9 \nThe Contractor shall be fully responsible for all employment, industrial relations, payroll, insurance and \ncontractor management obligations relating to supplied personnel, including: \n? \nRostering technical personnel for shifts of up to twelve (12) hours \n? \nProviding a digital rostering software, inclusive of a time and attendance system \n? \nEnsuring adequate breaks and fatigue management \n? \nProviding all PPE, uniforms and wet-weather gear \n? \nSupplying all required tools and equipment necessary to undertake works \n? \nCoordinating transport, loading and unloading of equipment \n? \nCoordinating storage requirements as required \n? \nCompleting site inductions and documentation \n? \nDeveloping and implementing Risk Assessments and SWMS \n? \nMeeting all project deadlines \n? \nAttending stakeholder meetings as required \n? \nParticipating in daily operational briefings and debriefs \n? \nComplying with electrical testing and tagging requirements \n? \nUndertaking other administrative duties directed by Destination NSW. \n2.10 \nThe Contractor must maintain sufficient qualified personnel, systems and operational resources to ensure \nall services within its scope are fully operational throughout the required event periods and operating \nhours. \n2.11 \nThis requirement is a recurring engagement under a proposed three-year initial contract term covering \nVivid Sydney 2027, 2028 and 2029, with two optional one-year extensions for Vivid Sydney 2030 and 2031 \nexercisable at DNSW?s sole discretion. The contract is intended to provide continuity of service, \noperational consistency and efficiencies across multiple annual event delivery cycles. \n2.12 \nVivid Sydney 2027 Event Dates are as follows (Vivid Sydney 2028 and 2029 timelines will be confirmed in \ndue course but are likely to follow a similar pattern of date): \n? \n10 May 2027 ? Bump in commences (date is approximate and subject to change) \n? \n24 ? 27 May 2027 ? Event previews (exact timings differ across the sites) \n? \n28 May ? 19 June 2027 ? Vivid Sydney  \n\n \n \nRequest for Tender ? Part B \n7 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n? \n27 June 2027 ? Bump out concludes (date is approximate and subject to change).  \n2.13 \nVivid Sydney 2027 Event period and operating hours are as follows: \n? \nThe Event commences nightly, for the 23 nights, with ?Lights On? at 18:00 hours and concludes with \n?Lights Off? at 23:00 hours. All operational plans should ensure workforce, infrastructure, systems and \nresourcing account for any work/s required nightly, before ?Lights On? and after ?Lights Off? each \nevening.  \n? \nThe event period for all years, for which Technical Support Services are required, is typically as \nfollows: \no Up to two weeks and five consecutive days of bump in before the event live period, generally \nworking from 07:00 ? 18:00 onsite. \no 23 nights of live event period, with most site staff working from approximately 14:00 ? 00:00 \neach night. \no Typically, six bump out days, generally working from 07:00 ? 18:00 onsite. \n? \nNoting that the Operational Technical Service period also extends to preparation and planning in the \nmonths prior to, and after the event each year.  \n? \nDates and times can vary outside of the above.  The Contractor agrees to be flexible as required and \nacknowledges that resourcing levels, deployment schedules and operational requirements may \nchange at short notice due to factors including but not limited to: \no Weather and natural disasters \no Unplanned incidents \no Operational requirements of DNSW.  \n? \nDestination NSW and their representatives will take all reasonable steps to communicate any such \nchanges to the Contractor.   \n2.14 \nVivid Sydney typically group parts of the footprint geographically into defined zones, to help manage and \norganise resources and on-site operational management. Whilst zones for 2027 and beyond are not yet \nconfirmed, it is highly likely they will follow the 2026 three-zone grouping (as shown below) again for the \n2027 event:  \n? \nZone 1: Circular Quay & The Rocks (including Campbells Cove and Hickson Rd Reserve) \n? \nZone 2: Barangaroo (including Barangaroo Lawns to Nawi Cove, the Central operational Compound at \nHarbour Park, and Wulugul Walk to the Aquarium) \n? \nZone 3: Darling Harbour (from the Aquarium, around Cockle Bay, Tumbalong Park south to Pier St \nunderpass) \n2.15 \nIn addition to the above areas, technical support teams are sometimes required to respond and manage \nareas outside of this, including North Sydney foreshore area, Glebe Island, Sydney Opera House forecourt, \nand some of the Vivid Sydney pillar event venues around Sydney CBD. \n3 \nApproach and Methodology  \n3.1 \nDNSW intends to adopt a collaborative and operationally focused approach to the provision of goods and \nservices for the 2027, 2028 and 2029 Vivid Sydney festival program. \nThe methodology is expected to include: \n? \nEarly engagement and detailed planning between DNSW, the Contractor and key stakeholders  \n? \nDevelopment of clear project timelines, operational plans and delivery milestones  \n\n \n \nRequest for Tender ? Part B \n8 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n? \nRegular coordination meetings, reporting and progress reviews throughout all project phases  \n? \nIntegration of safety, compliance, accessibility and risk management requirements into all activities  \n? \nEfficient procurement, logistics and resource management processes to support seamless event \ndelivery  \n? \nFlexible and responsive operational management to address evolving event requirements and \nstakeholder needs  \n? \nContinuous communication and collaboration across all contractors, agencies and delivery partners  \n? \nPost-event evaluation and continuous improvement processes to inform future festival delivery \nperiods  \n  \nThe Contractor will be expected to demonstrate proactive project management, strong stakeholder \nengagement and a commitment to delivering high-quality services aligned with DNSW operational \nobjectives and event standards. \n3.2 \nThe Contractor will be required to comply with all applicable New South Wales and local government \nlegislation, regulations, standards, licences and approvals relevant to the provision of services for the \n2027, 2028 and 2029 Vivid Sydney festival periods, and future Vivid Sydney Festival options exercised by \nDNSW. \n3.3 \nThe work will primarily be undertaken at the nominated Vivid Sydney Festival sites and associated \noperational areas within Sydney, New South Wales. This includes onsite event production compounds, \nback-of-house areas, staging and infrastructure zones, and any associated venues or facilities utilised for \nthe planning, installation, operation and pack-down of the Technical Support Services. Certain planning, \ncoordination and administrative activities will also be undertaken remotely or from Contractor premises \nwhere appropriate. \n3.4 \nDNSW may provide the Contractor with access to relevant event sites, operational areas, project \ndocumentation and stakeholder coordination necessary to support delivery of the services. \n4 \nGeneral Requirements \n4.1 \nThe Contractor shall: \n? \nProvide appropriate high-visibility uniform/s, wet weather, PPE and equipment required to deliver \nupon their assigned roles and responsibilities where necessary \n? \nProvide all required administrative and planning support as required \n? \nAlong with their staff, attend any/all safety and risk management briefings and inductions as deemed \nnecessary by Destination NSW and/or their designated representatives prior to works commencing.  \n? \nComply with all relevant legislation, required approvals and laws.  \n? \nComplete all site inductions and compliance documentation, including relevant risk assessments and \nSWMS \n? \nCoordinate transport logistics, and load, transport and unload equipment safely \n? \nComply with electrical testing and tagging requirements \n? \nTake direction from Destination NSW representatives \n? \nProvide sufficient staffing to meet operational timeframes \n? \nSubmit detailed fortnightly timesheets throughout the contract term, at the latest by the Friday \nfollowing each fortnight ending \n? \nIdentify personnel names, roles and hours worked \n\n \n \nRequest for Tender ? Part B \n9 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n? \nInclude shift commencement and completion times \n? \nProvide cost tracking and labour reporting as requested \n? \nAlways endeavour to provide cost efficiencies whenever possible \n? \nEnsure no scheduled shifts exceed twelve (12) consecutive hours and a minimum break of ten (10) \nhours is provided between each shift.   \n4.2 \nReporting obligations are detailed below: \nY= Yes \nTechnical Project \nAdministrator \nSpecialist \nTechnical \nStaff  \nTechnical Staff  \nAsset and Event \nTechnologies \nTechnical Staff \n \nReporting to Vivid Sydney Head of Projects \nY \n  \n  \nY \nReporting to Vivid Sydney Operations Manager, Infrastructure & Logistics  \nY \n \n \nY \nReporting to Vivid Sydney Production Manager, Vivid Light  \nY \n \n \n \nReporting to Vivid Sydney Technical Manager, Vivid Light   \nY \n \n \n \nReporting to Vivid Sydney Event Operations Coordinator, Infrastructure & \nLogistics  \nY \n \n \nY \nReporting to Vivid Sydney Production Coordinator, Vivid Light  \nY \n \n \n \nReporting to Vivid Sydney Operations Command (Event Control Centre) \n Y \nY  \nY \nY \n4.3 \nThe Contractor must perform all other activities reasonably necessary to deliver a safe, effective and \nsuccessful operations consistent with the scope described and objectives of Vivid Sydney. \n5 \nItems to be provided by DNSW \n5.1 \nDNSW will provide the following: \n? \nA copy of all relevant maps, schedules and any other applicable documents to deliver the scope of \nworks, including the Event Command, Control, Communication and Coordination (C4) Structure \n? \nA copy of any relevant, available and requested historical Event information \n? \nRadio communication equipment for all technical support services team members. \n? \nVivid Sydney outdoor jackets for all technical support services team members to be worn during the \nlive event period  \n? \nVivid Sydney hi-vis vests if required \n? \nTechnical Support Services site sheds, equipped with appropriate resources \n? \nPrinting and supply of all reasonable Vehicle Access Passes, noting that onsite parking is NOT \npermitted in any part of the Vivid Sydney footprint \n6 \nWork Health and Safety Considerations \n6.1 \nThe Contractor must comply with all applicable Commonwealth, State and local laws, regulations, codes \nand standards relevant to the services, including work health and safety, public health, fire safety, \nenvironmental, electrical safety and emergency management requirements. The Contractor must hold \nand maintain all licences, permits, registrations, certifications, qualifications, accreditations and \n\n \n \nRequest for Tender ? Part B \n10 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \ncompetencies required to perform the services and provide evidence of compliance upon request by \nDNSW. \n6.2 \nPrior to commencement of the services, the Contractor must ensure: \n? \nAll personnel possess a current NSW White Card and the requisite qualifications, licences, \naccreditations and expertise. This includes any specific licenses relevant to the services and required \nfor the safe operation of plant and machinery including but not limited to Forklift / National High-Risk \nWork / EWP / Truck licence etc. No work is to be undertaken without a valid licence. \n6.3 \nPrior to commencing any work on-site, the Contractor must ensure: \n? \nSuccessful completion of the Vivid Sydney Risk Management Framework (VSRMF). \n? \nAll Contractor personnel are briefed (including subcontractors) on their responsibilities, safety \nstandards and event-specific policies that are applicable to the services.  \n? \nAll personnel have read, understood and signed all required Contractor-provided SWMS, including \nthe VSRMF. \n? \nAll personnel have completed the Vivid Sydney Induction, and any other inductions that are appropriate \nto the Contractor?s services and location / landowner requirements. \n? \nIt has conducted pre-operational checks on equipment and plant that will be utilised for the services. \n6.4 \nDuring services delivery, the Contractor must ensure: \n? \nAll personnel wear the mandatory PPE, including High Visibility wear for day and night. \n? \nAll personnel are aware of the need to remain alert, identify hazards, and assess risks. This includes \nremaining vigilant for any suspicious activity, behaviour or items on site.  \n? \nAll personnel follow emergency instructions and report incidents, hazards, near misses or injuries \nimmediately. \n? \nDaily Toolbox talks are conducted to address specific safety issues, procedures or hazards, and is \nfacilitated by the Precinct Manager and will provide notes from the toolbox talks upon request.  \n? \nHazards and incidents (potential and actual) are reported to the Precinct Manager and Security. \n6.5 \nThe Contractor is required to t deliver a full plan once the final plan for each year has been developed.  \n7 \nContract Management \n7.1 \nRegular contract review meetings will be required between the Contractor, DNSW and relevant \noperational stakeholders throughout the planning, delivery and close-out phases of services delivery. \nThese meetings may include project status updates, operational coordination meetings, production and \nlogistics reviews, risk and safety discussions, budget and procurement tracking, issue resolution, \nscheduling updates and performance reviews against agreed deliverables and timelines. \n7.2 \nMeeting frequency may vary depending on the project phase, with increased meeting cadence \nanticipated during bump-in, live event operations and bump-out periods. Contractor may also be \nrequired to attend ad hoc meetings, stakeholder briefings and post-event review sessions as reasonably \nrequested by DNSW. \n8 \nPricing / Fees \n8.1 \nDNSW is seeking a fixed fee for all the requirements as described in this Part B. \n\n \n \nRequest for Tender ? Part B \n11 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n8.2 \nUnless otherwise agreed in writing by DNSW, the Contractor will be responsible for all ancillary costs \nassociated with the delivery of the services, including but not limited to travel, airfares, accommodation, \nmeals, local transport, freight, parking and incidental expenses. \n8.3 \nDNSW will seek commercially competitive pricing and value for money outcomes as part of the \nprocurement process. Contractors may propose any applicable efficiencies, bundled service pricing, \nvolume-based discounts or other commercial incentives that support cost effectiveness across the \ndelivery of Technical Support Services. \n9 \nModern Slavery  \n9.1 \nDefinitions  \nModern Slavery   \n9.1.1 \nmeans any conduct that constitutes or would constitute a modern slavery offence, being an offense \ndescribed in Schedule 2 of the Modern Slavery Act 2018 (NSW),or an offence of attempting or incitement \nto commit an offence described in Schedule 2 of the Modern Slavery Act 2018 (NSW).   \n9.1.2 \nincludes any conduct that constitutes or would constitute an offence under any of the Modern Slavery \nLaws as amended from time to time, including an offence of attempting or incitement to commit such an \noffence; and  \n9.1.3 \nincludes conduct engaged in elsewhere than in New South Wales that, if it occurred in New South Wales, \nwould constitute a modern slavery offence under paragraphs (a) or (b).   \nModern Slavery Laws means:  \n? \nthe Modern Slavery Act 2018 (Cth);   \n? \nthe Modern Slavery Act 2018 (NSW);  \n? \nDivisions 270 and 271 of the Commonwealth Criminal Code;  \n? \nsection 176(1A) of the Public Works and Procurement Act 1912 (NSW);   \n? \nsection 438ZE of the Local Government Act 1993 (NSW); and  \n9.1.4 \nany other laws, regulations, codes and international conventions aimed at combatting modern slavery, \nforced labour or human trafficking, from time to time in force in or ratified by Australia and, where \nrelevant, in or by other jurisdictions in which the parties operate,   \neach as amended from time to time.  \n9.1.5 \nreasonable steps means those steps that are reasonable in the circumstances to \nprevent, identify, mitigate and remedy modern slavery. In assessing whether steps are reasonable, the \nparties may refer to the NSW Anti-slavery Commissioner?s Guidance on Reasonable Steps and related \ninformation and resources published by the Anti-slavery Commissioner.  \n9.2 \nModern slavery contract clauses  \n9.2.1 \nThe Contractor agrees it may be required to comply with modern slavery contract clauses in the \ncontract entered into with the Buyer.     \n9.3 \nPrice  \n9.3.1 \nThe Contractor agrees the pricing allows it to perform the contract without causing or contributing to \nmodern slavery, and to provide or enable an effective remedy to any modern slavery it does cause or to \nwhich it does contribute. \n\n"}, {'file_name': 'Part-D---Vivid-Technical-Support-Services---Contract-of-engagement-template-fnsw-3.docx', 'file_type': 'docx', 'storage_uri': 'gs://tenderai-dev-documents/tenders/buynsw/RFT-12634696798/02291cd1e234c4ed-Part-D---Vivid-Technical-Support-Services---Contract-of-engagement-template-fnsw-3.docx', 'extracted_text': 'PARTY DETAILS\nParties\nDestination NSW\nof Level 2, 88 Cumberland Street, The Rocks, NSW, 2000\nABN: 52 890 768 976\n(Destination NSW or DNSW)\nand\n[Supplier name]\nOf [Insert registered address]\nABN/ACN/International Business Number \n(Supplier)\nCONTRACT SCHEDULE \nEXECUTION PAGE:\nCONTRACT STANDARD TERMS AND CONDITIONS\nGoods and Services\nFrom the Commencement Date until the Completion Date, the Supplier is engaged to undertake, supply and provide the goods and/or services, duties and outcomes set out in Item 3 and Item 4 of the Contract Schedule (?Goods and/or Services?). In exchange for the Goods and/or Services Destination NSW will pay the Supplier the Contract Price as set out in Item 6 of the Contract Schedule upon receipt of a valid invoice as set out in Item 7. \nEach party states that:\nit has full power and authority to execute, delivery and perform its obligations under this Contract;\nthis Contract is legally binding on and enforceable against it;\nit will comply with all applicable laws in the performance of its obligations under this Contract;\nits signatories are properly authorised; and\nit can enter into this Contract without breaching any other contract or legal obligation to anyone else.\nDestination NSW will reimburse the Supplier for out-of-pocket expenses in connection with Services, if any have been so identified for reimbursement in the invoice, provided that the Supplier has obtained prior written approval to incur such expenses at Destination NSW?s expense before they are incurred and valid tax invoices and receipts are provided for reconciliation purposes in accordance with Item 7. \nThe Supplier must provide the Goods and/or Services in accordance with any Milestones or Delivery Dates set out in Item 5 of the Contract Schedule. \nWhere the Supplier is supplying Goods to Destination NSW it:\nmust ensure and warrant that title to the Goods passes to Destination NSW on the Delivery Date free from encumbrances and all other adverse interests;\nacknowledges that all risk in the Goods will remain the Supplier\'s until the Goods are installed or delivered to the satisfaction of Destination NSW; and\nwarrants that the goods will be: \nfree from defects in materials or workmanship; \nof merchantable quality; \nfit for purpose; and,\ncarry any applicable manufacturer?s warranty which will pass to Destination NSW.\nThe Supplier and any Key Person/s must not unreasonably refuse a request by Destination NSW to inspect and test the Goods prior to acceptance of delivery of the Goods. Destination NSW is entitled to reject any Goods which are not manufactured or delivered in accordance with the terms of this Contract (which includes a failure to comply with the warranties contained in clause 4(c) of this Contract). Destination NSW is not deemed to have accepted any Goods until the latter of:\nthe expiration of a reasonable time for inspection of the Goods following delivery and installation; or\nany latent defect in the Goods becoming apparent. \nIf any Goods are rejected by Destination NSW under clause 5, title to and risk in those Goods automatically re-vests in the Supplier. At the Supplier\'s sole expense, it is responsible for the collection and removal of the rejected Goods. Destination NSW may, in its sole discretion, require the Supplier to replace any rejected Goods, or reimburse the costs arising from having to acquire replacement Goods from a third party, without prejudice to any of the other rights or remedies Destination NSW may have under this Contract or at law. \nWhere the Supplier is providing Services to Destination NSW, the Supplier and any Key Person/s must:\nperform the Services in accordance with best industry practice. Destination NSW relies on the Supplier, as the Supplier of the Services, to know what the best industry practice is;\ncomply with the laws, standards and policies that apply to the Services;\nperform the Services with the due care, skill and diligence expected of a professional service supplier and in a proper and workmanlike manner; \nperform the Services in a cost-effective manner consistent with the required level of quality of performance; and\nensure that any equipment used in delivering the Services is suitable and in good, safe and serviceable order and continuously maintained or repaired so that it remains in such order.\nThe Supplier must notify Destination NSW immediately if it anticipates that there may be a delay in the provision of the Goods and/or Services. Any request for an extension of time to deliver the Goods and/or Services requires Destination NSW?s approval, which will not be unreasonably withheld. \nThe Supplier agrees that any work performed for Destination NSW will be deemed to fall within the Services for the Contract Price set out in Item 6 of the Contract Schedule. Should the Supplier wish to perform work believed to be outside the scope of the Services, it must seek written approval from Destination NSW before proceeding with any such work. Such approval may be withheld by Destination NSW in its sole discretion. \nTerm\nThis Contract will commence on the Commencement Date identified in Item 1 and will continue until the Completion Date identified in Item 1, unless renewed in accordance with clause 11.   \nThe Parties agree that Destination NSW may elect to renew this Contract for the Option Period(s) set out in Item 1 of the Contract Schedule, subject to the consent of the Supplier. \nIf Destination NSW elects to exercise an option to renew, Destination NSW will provide, within 30 days? of the expiration of the Completion Date, notice in writing to the Supplier and the Contract Price Review mechanism set out in Item 8 of the Contract Schedule will apply. Unless otherwise agreed in writing between the parties (email is sufficient), all other terms and conditions set out in this Contract will remain in full force and effect.  \nInvoice and Tax\nThe Supplier must submit a tax invoice at the timeframes set out in Item 7 of the Contract Schedule. \nDestination NSW may withhold or apply a set-off against any tax invoice submitted by the Supplier if Destination NSW is not satisfied, acting reasonably, that the Goods and/or Services have been provided in accordance with the terms and conditions of this Contract. \nOther than as provided in this Contract, all taxes, duties and charges and taxation obligations imposed or levied in connection to the Contract Price (including, without limitation, making appropriate tax deductions and payments in respect of any Key Person/s as applicable) will be borne by the Supplier. \nIf GST is applicable under or in connection with this Contract, any reference in this clause to a term defined or used in the A New Tax System (Goods and Services Tax) Act 1999 (Cth) (?Act?) is, unless the context indicates otherwise, a reference to that term as defined or used in the Act. \nAny amount referred to in this Contract except as indicated otherwise, is expressed on a GST exclusive basis. The Supplier takes all responsibility for determining whether GST is applicable to it and Destination NSW will not be liable whatsoever, for upholding the Supplier\'s requirements and obligations under the Act. \nIf GST is payable under or in connection with this Contract, the consideration to be provided for the supply (unless it is expressly stated to include GST) is increased by an amount equal to the GST exclusive consideration multiplied by the rate at which GST is imposed in respect of the supply. \nTermination \nDestination NSW may at any time terminate this Contract for convenience by giving the Supplier 30 days? notice in writing, such termination being effective upon expiration of the 30-day period. Destination NSW will not be liable for any payments to the Supplier over and above the value of the payment due and payable for Goods provided or Services performed up to the date of termination under this clause. \nEither party may terminate this Contract if the other commits a material breach of this Contract which is either not capable of being remedied or which the party committing the breach has not remedied within seven (7) (or such larger number as agreed in writing) days of receiving a written notice requiring remedy from the other party. \nDestination NSW may terminate this Contract where the Supplier becomes bankrupt or stops, suspends or threatens to stop payment of all or a class of its debts, or where the Supplier appoints an administrator or liquidator.\nUpon termination of this Contract, any property belonging to Destination NSW which is in the possession of the Supplier and its Key Person/s must be immediately returned to Destination NSW.\nWhere this Contract is terminated by Destination NSW under clauses 20 or 21, Destination NSW may withhold any or all of the fees payable to the Supplier in exchange for the Services (other than fees for Goods or Services provided prior to the date of termination). Any fees prepaid by Destination NSW to the Supplier for Goods or Services not delivered to Destination NSW by the termination date are to be refunded to Destination NSW no later than 10 business days after the termination date.    \nIf there is any actual or threatened conduct which is or may be a breach of this Contract, both parties acknowledge that damages may be inadequate compensation for such a breach. Either party will be entitled to apply to any court of competent jurisdiction for interim or permanent injunctive relief or both, restraining the other party from committing any breach or threatened breach of this Contract. Such rights and remedies will be cumulative and in addition to any other rights or remedies which either party may be entitled to at law or in equity. \nForce Majeure\nA party (?Affected Party?) will not be liable for any failure or delay in the performance of its obligations under this Contract to the extent that such failure or delay is caused, directly or indirectly by any of the following circumstances (?Force Majeure Circumstance?):\nfire, flood, earthquake, or acts of God;\nstrikes or industrial action (but not strikes or industrial action by any employees of Destination NSW or the Supplier); and\nacts of war, pandemic, epidemic, terrorism, riots, civil disorders or rebellions or revolutions in Australia. \nThe Affected Party must:\npromptly advise the other party of the details of the Force Majeure Circumstance, and its likely effect on the Affected Party?s obligations under this Contract; and\ntake all steps reasonably necessary to recommence performance and minimise the delay caused by the Force Majeure Circumstance. \nIf the Services cannot be rearranged so that they are conducted, the Supplier will refund to Destination NSW within 10 business days of receiving notice from Destination NSW any unspent portion of the payments and no further payments will be made. \nIndemnity\nThe Supplier must indemnify Destination NSW against any loss, action, proceedings, demand, prosecution, judgment, damage, cost, expense, fine, penalty or liability claimed against or incurred by Destination NSW in relation to:\npersonal injury or damage to property arising out of:\na wilful misconduct (being an act or omission which was a deliberate and wrongful act or omission or involved reckless disregard or wanton indifference to the likely consequences) or any negligent act or omission by the Supplier and/or its Key Person/s, to provide the Goods or Services; or\nany breach by the Supplier and/or its Key Person/s of this Contract (including but not limited to any loss resulting from the Supplier not being registered for GST and ABN purposes);\nbreach by the Supplier and/or its Key Person/s, of applicable Privacy and Data Protection Laws or confidentiality obligations; or\na material breach of the Contract by the Supplier and/or its Key Person/s, or infringement of Destination NSW?s intellectual property rights by the Supplier or it Key Peron/s; and,\nthe Supplier and/or its Key Person/s claiming that they are Destination NSW\'s employee, including claims for wages, superannuation, public holidays, annual leave, parental leave, personal carer s leave, long service leave, redundancy or termination of employment or any other employment entitlement. \nThe indemnity in this clause 28 will not apply to the extent that the relevant loss, action, proceedings, demand, prosecution, judgment, damage, cost, expense, fine, penalty or liability is caused or contributed to by Destination NSW.  \nKey Persons\nIf any Key Person/s are named at Item 2 of the Contract Schedule, then the Supplier must ensure that those Key Person/s are fully engaged in the Services on behalf of the Supplier. If any Key Person is unavailable, then the Supplier must notify Destination NSW and provide, at the earliest opportunity, replacements with comparable skill and experience, subject to written approval by Destination NSW. The Suppler acknowledges and agrees that Destination NSW may, in its absolute discretion, reject any proposed replacement(s) of one or more Key Person/s and that where this occurs, the Supplier must provide an alternate person with comparable skill and experience approved by Destination NSW of as soon as possible.\nConfidentiality, Personal Information and GIPA\nSubject to the Government Information (Public Access) Act 2009 (NSW) (GIPA Act), the Supplier and its Key Person/s acknowledge the importance of confidentiality in all aspects of Destination NSW?s operations and agree to keep all Confidential Information in absolute confidence and not to disclose any such information to any person without our prior written consent both during and after the term of this Contract. \n"Confidential Information" in this Contract includes any information disclosed by Destination NSW or by its employees, agents and assigns to the Supplier either before or after the commencement of the Term that is confidential by its nature, is designated as confidential, specifically identified as confidential, or the Supplier and/or its Key Person/s know, or ought to know, is confidential. \nBoth parties may disclose Confidential Information to their need-to-know employees and agents and to their legal and professional advisors to the extent required for the performance of this Contract, subject to an ongoing obligation of confidentiality and provided that the disclosing party ensures that the recipient in turn keeps the Confidential Information confidential and does not use the information except for the purposes of this Contract. Your The Supplier\'s intended recipients must be pre-approved in writing by Destination NSW and you the Supplier will ensure that your its permitted recipients of our Confidential Information execute a confidentiality deed, prior to receiving the Confidential Information, to protect Destination NSW, which deed must be in a form approved by Destination NSW. \nBoth parties must collect, use, and store any Personal Information in relation to the Services strictly in compliance with the Privacy and Data Protection Laws and: \nFor the purpose of the clause, Privacy and Data Protection Laws means in respect of a party, any law or regulation relating the protection of personal data including:\nthe General Data Protection Regulation (2016/679);\nthe Privacy and Electronic Communications Directive (2002/58/EC);\nthe Privacy Act 1988 (Cth); and\nthe Privacy and Personal Information Protection Act 1998 (NSW). \nFor the purpose of this clause, "Personal Information" means the definition set out in the relevant Privacy and Data Protection Laws. \nImmediately on termination of this Contract for any reason, both parties must:\ncease the use of all Confidential Information of, or relating to, the other party;\ndeliver to the other party all documents and other materials in its possession or control containing, recording or constituting the other party?s Confidential Information, or, at the other party?s option, destroy, and certify to the other party that it has destroyed, those documents; \nfor other party Confidential Information stored electronically, permanently delete that Confidential Information from all electronic media on which it is stored, so that it cannot be restored.\nNothing in clause 34 above shall prevent Destination NSW from any record storage actions it is required to take pursuant to the State Records Act 1998 (NSW).  \nThe Supplier acknowledges that the restrictions in this clause are reasonable in all the circumstances and necessary to protect the assets and information of Destination NSW (including brand and reputation) and the remedy of damages may be inadequate to protect the interests of Destination NSW and Destination NSW is entitled to seek and obtain injunctive relief, or any other relief.\nRight of Access to Records and Information\nFor the purposes of assisting Destination NSW meet its obligations under the GIPA Act, the Supplier must, within seven (7) days of receiving a written request by Destination NSW, provide Destination NSW with immediate access to the following information contained in records held by the Supplier or its Key Person/s:\ninformation that relates directly to the provision to Destination NSW of the Goods or Services under this Contract;\ninformation collected by the Supplier from members of the public for the purposes of the provision of the Goods or Services under this Contract; and/or\ninformation received by the Supplier from Destination NSW to enable the Supplier to provide the Goods or Services under this Contract. \nFor the purpose of clause 35, information does not include:\ninformation that discloses or would tend to disclose financing arrangements, financial modelling, cost structure or profit margin;\ninformation that the Supplier is prohibited from disclosing to Destination NSW by provision made by or under any act, whether of any State or Territory, or of the Commonwealth; or\ninformation that, if disclosed to Destination NSW, could reasonably be expected to place the Supplier at a substantial commercial disadvantage in relation to Destination NSW, whether at present or in the future. \nThe Supplier will provide copies of any of the information requested by Destination NSW under clause 35 and any associated costs for this may be separately agreed between the parties. \nAny failure by the Supplier to comply with any request under clause 35 above will be considered a breach of an essential term and will allow Destination NSW to terminate this Contract by providing notice in writing of its intention to do so with the termination to take effect seven (7) days after receipt of the notice. If the Supplier fails to remedy the breach within the seven (7) day period to the satisfaction of Destination NSW, then the termination will take effect seven (7) days after receipt of the notice.\nIntellectual Property \nThe Supplier agrees that ownership of all intellectual property rights subsisting in any material created under this Contract, including but not limited to insert specific description of important material under this Contract ? plans, documents and photographs or footage (final and raw, proofs, negatives) shall vest in Destination NSW royalty free, for use worldwide, free of charge and in perpetuity. For the purpose of this clause, ?intellectual property? expressly excludes the Supplier?s pre-existing materials, and the Supplier retains all right, title and interest in and to any and all pre-existing materials. For the purposes of this Contract, the pre-existing materials are deemed to be the Supplier?s confidential information. To the extent any pre-existing materials are used in connection with the Services and/or incorporated or contained in any deliverable provided to Destination NSW pursuant to this contract, the Supplier hereby grants Destination NSW a non-exclusive, irrevocable, perpetual, royalty-free, worldwide license to use the pre-existing materials in accordance with this contract. The Supplier?s pre-existing materials include any software, tools, algorithms, know-how, ideas, techniques, materials, architecture, methodologies, concepts, trade secrets and other proprietary information that were owned or licensed by the Supplier prior to entering into this Contract. The Supplier warrants that Destination NSW?s exploitation of the intellectual property rights referenced in this clause, as contemplated under this Contract, will not infringe the intellectual property rights of any third party. \nDestination NSW intellectual property subsisting in any materials or documents provided by Destination NSW to the Supplier is the sole and exclusive property of Destination NSW or its licensors (as applicable) and this Contract does not confer on the Supplier any proprietary right, ownership, or title to any of the Destination NSW intellectual property. \nThe Supplier consents to and must ensure each of its employee(s) and contractor(s) consent to Destination NSW doing anything in respect of the intellectual property rights assigned or licensed to Destination NSW under this Contract which would otherwise breach the moral rights of the affected party granted to it by the Copyright Act 1968 (Cth). \nDispute Resolution\nIf a dispute under this Contract arises (?Dispute?):\nthe party claiming that a Dispute has arisen must give a written notice to the other party (?Dispute Notice?), setting out reasonable particulars of the Dispute;\nwithin five (5) business days after receipt of a Dispute Notice, a senior representative of each party must meet and attempt to resolve the Dispute within 10 business days of receipt of the Dispute Notice;\nif the parties fail to meet or resolve the Dispute within 10 business days of the receipt of the Dispute Notice, the chief executive officer (or equivalent) of each party (or his or her nominee) must meet (in person or by way of telephone conference) and attempt to resolve the Dispute within 20 business days of receipt of the Dispute Notice; and\nif the parties fail to meet or resolve the Dispute within 20 business days of receipt of the Dispute Notice, the parties may take whatever action they consider necessary to resolve the Dispute. \nCommunications between the parties during the process set out in clause 38, whether oral or in writing, will not be admissible as evidence in any legal process unless in writing and signed by both parties. \nDespite the existence of a Dispute, the parties must continue to perform their respective obligation under this Contract. \nNothing in clauses 44 to 46 prevents a party issuing proceedings from obtaining urgent injunctive or urgent declaratory relief. \nAssignment\nThe Supplier may, with Destination NSW?s approval, assign any rights or duties under this Contract or sub-contract performance of this Contract or any part of it. Provided the individual or entity the Supplier assigns or subcontracts any rights or duties to is qualified to perform the Services, Destination NSW?s approval will not be unreasonably withheld. Engaging sub-contractors to provide any part of the Services will not relieve the Supplier from any of its liabilities or obligations under this Contract. \nGenerative Artificial Intelligence Systems\nNotwithstanding any other provision of this Contract, each party (Party A) is prohibited, without the other party?s (Party B) prior written consent, from reproducing, publishing, communicating, or otherwise using, in whole or in part:\nany intellectual property, confidential information or any other information of Party B; or \nany material created under this contract by either party; or\nor any trademarks of Party B; \n(collectively Party B Material),\nto develop, train or direct, Artificial Intelligence Systems, technology or models, including the mining or scraping of text, images or data from Party B Material that vests in whole or in part in Party B whether undertaken by the Party A or by third parties authorised, licensed or directed by Party A.\nArtificial Intelligence System means a machine-based system that, for explicit or implicit objectives, infers, from the input it receives, how to generate outputs such as predictions, content, recommendations\xa0or decisions that can influence physical or virtual environments.\xa0\nModern Slavery\nFor the purpose of this clause:\nCore Obligations means those obligations set out in clause 53 (Core Obligations);\nEngaged Entity of a party means any first tier (direct) suppliers, subcontractors, consultants and contractors engaged by that party (or that party?s directors, officers and employees) in connection with this Contract. For the avoidance of doubt, ?Engaged Entities? includes independent contractors (whether an individual or body corporate), secondees, consultants and any other workers (however described) who may be engaged for the purposes of this Contract but are not employed by the relevant party;\nManagement Plan means a plan to take reasonable steps to manage risks of Modern Slavery in the Supplier?s operations and supply chains (including in the operations and supply chains of Supplier?s Engaged Entities);\nMaterial Breach has the meaning given in clause 58\nModern Slavery:\nmeans any conduct that constitutes or would constitute any offence listed in Schedule 2 of the Modern Slavery Act 2018 (NSW), including an offence of attempting or incitement to commit such an offence;\nincludes any conduct that constitutes or would constitute an offence under any of the Modern Slavery Laws as amended from time to time, including an offence of attempting or incitement to commit such an offence; and\nincludes conduct engaged in elsewhere than in New South Wales that, if it occurred in New South Wales, would constitute a modern slavery offence under paragraphs (a) or (b);\nModern Slavery Laws means:\nthe Modern Slavery Act 2018 (Cth);\nthe Modern Slavery Act 2018 (NSW);\nDivisions 270 and 271 of the Commonwealth Criminal Code;\nsection 176(1A) of the Public Works and Procurement Act 1912 (NSW);\nsection 438ZE of the Local Government Act 1993 (NSW); and\nany other laws, regulations, codes and international conventions aimed at combatting modern slavery, forced labour or human trafficking, from time to time in force in or ratified by Australia and, where relevant, in or by other jurisdictions in which the parties operate, \neach as amended from time to time;\nreasonable steps means those steps that are reasonable in the circumstances to prevent, identify, mitigate and remedy modern slavery. In assessing whether steps are reasonable, the parties may refer to the NSW Anti-slavery Commissioner?s Guidance on Reasonable Steps and related information and resources published by the Anti-slavery Commissioner;\nRelated Body Corporate has the meaning given to that term in the Corporations Act 2001 (Cth);\nRelated Entity means, in respect of a party, a Related Body Corporate of such party;\nRemediation Plan has the meaning given to it in clause 59. \nThe Supplier must:\nnot engage in Modern Slavery;\ntake reasonable steps to ensure that its directors, officers, employees, Related Entities and Engaged Entities comply with Modern Slavery Laws as applicable;\ntake reasonable steps to ensure that its Engaged Entities include provisions equivalent to the Core Obligations (including this sub-clause) in their contracts with their suppliers; and\ntake reasonable steps to ensure that its Engaged Entities provide their respective directors, officers, employees and suppliers with at least the minimum level of wages and other entitlements required by law. \nThe Supplier acknowledges and agrees that the Contract Price supports the Supplier to comply with its Core Obligations.\nThe Supplier agrees that it will establish, implement, and maintain for the term of this Contract, appropriate systems and policies as required to meet its Core Obligations.\nWithout limiting the Core Obligations and clause 55, and to the extent permitted by law, the Supplier agrees that it will notify Destination NSW immediately with adequate particulars of the Modern Slavery and the actions taken, or being taken, to remedy the Modern Slavery if the Supplier becomes aware of any actual or reasonably suspected Modern Slavery engaged in, or any notices, investigations, proceedings or claims arising in any jurisdiction in relation to any actual or reasonably suspected breach of Modern Slavery Laws by the Supplier, the Supplier\'s directors, officers, employees, Related Entities, or by any of its Engaged Entities, whether or not the Modern Slavery occurs or is suspected to occur in the performance of the Contract.\nThe Supplier represents and warrants to, and for the benefit of, Destination NSW that, as at the Contract Date and on a continuing basis for the duration of the term of this Contract, the Supplier has disclosed, in accordance with clause 56:\nto the extent the Supplier is aware, any:\nactual or reasonably suspected Modern Slavery engaged in; and\nnotices, investigations, proceedings or claims arising in any jurisdiction in relation to any actual or reasonably suspected breach of Modern Slavery Laws,\nby the Supplier, he Supplier\'s directors, officers, employees, or Related Entities, or by any of the Supplier?s Engaged Entities while performing any contract with the Supplier, whether or not the Modern Slavery arises in the performance of the Contract; and\nall actions taken to remedy said Modern Slavery or breach of Modern Slavery Laws. \nWithout limiting the parties? rights under this Contract, the parties agree that:\na breach of the Supplier?s obligations under this clause will be taken to be a Material Breach of this Contract; and\nbefore exercising any termination rights that may arise as a result of this Material Breach, the parties shall consult with relevant stakeholders on whether Modern Slavery may arise from such termination and the reasonable steps to prevent or mitigate such risk of Modern Slavery. \nWhere Destination NSW forms the view that there is a Material Breach that is reasonably capable of being remedied, then the parties shall develop a remediation plan to take reasonable steps to remedy the breach in accordance with this Contract (the Remediation Plan). The Supplier shall take reasonable efforts to implement this Remediation Plan. \nEntire agreement and previous representations\nThis Contract states all the express terms of the Contract between the parties and supersedes all prior discussions and agreements in respect of its subject matter. \nUpon entering this Contract, the Supplier will be taken to have investigated and satisfied itself of the adequacy of all information relied upon by the Supplier to form a position in relation to the Goods or Services to be provided under this Contract. \nTo the maximum extent permitted by law, and other than as expressly specified in this Contract, Destination NSW does not:\nmake any representation or warranty (express or implied) as to the accuracy, completeness, currency, fitness for purpose, quality, reliability, usefulness or timeliness of the content of the information relied upon by the Supplier in entering this Contract to provide the Goods and/or Services or as to any of Destination NSW?s Confidential Information provided to the Supplier; or\nhave any liability whatsoever for any decision made by the Supplier or any other person to engage with Destination NSW to provide the Goods and/or Services. \nGeneral \nThe Supplier must take out and maintain the insurance cover identified at Item 10 of the Schedule with a reputable insurance company for the period of this Contract. Destination NSW may request confirmation of such cover by way of certificates of currency prior to the commencement of the Term. \nThe Supplier is also solely responsible for ensuring that any sub-contractors engaged are also suitably insured and acknowledges that it and its Key Person/s are not entitled to the benefit of any accident, third party, public liability or
