2026-09-29T04:19:18.881919Z [] {'source_reference_id': 'RFI-2017495 - EF26/553', 'source_id': 'buynsw', 'source_url': 'https://buy.nsw.gov.au/prcOpportunity/4B85DCFE-06A4-4CEA-9DB171D82BE94204', 'title': 'BreastScreen NSW trailers and self-drive mobile vans replacement project', 'issuing_agency': 'Cancer Institute NSW', 'category': 'Request for Information', 'status': None, 'publish_date': None, 'closing_date': '2026-10-16', 'value_amount': None, 'value_currency': None, 'value_notes': None, 'location': 'NSW', 'description': 'The Cancer Institute NSW (CINSW) is conducting a Request for Information (RFI) to gather insights for the design, supply, construction, fit out, and delivery of new mobile mammography units for its BreastScreen NSW (BSNSW) program. The current fleet of 2 self-drive trucks and 12 semi-trailers is nearing end-of-life, and CINSW plans a staggered replacement over 2 to 5 years. This RFI specifically seeks information for three self-drive trucks and twelve trailers.\n\nThe new vehicles must be designed to accommodate digital mammography and ultrasound equipment, adhering to detailed technical requirements. These include external dimensions (max 12m long, 2.4m wide, 4.3m high), and an internal layout featuring a radiation-shielded mammography room (minimum 4.2m long), a reception/waiting area, and an amenities room. Key features required are wheelchair access via an automatic loader, robust power systems (mains, backup, UPS, with consideration for solar), water/waste management, and compliance with Australian and NSW Design Rules, including AS3200 and Radiation Guideline 7. BSNSW staff are not expected to drive these vehicles.\n\nThis is an RFI, not a tender, and responses will not be competitively evaluated but will be used as an information source to shape future procurement. Participation is voluntary and does not guarantee any preference in subsequent processes. Submissions are due by 3:00 p.m. AEST on 16 October 2026 via the buy NSW Opportunities Hub; email submissions are not permitted due to probity requirements.', 'summary_headline': 'Cancer Institute NSW seeks information for replacing BreastScreen NSW mobile mammography vans and trailers.', 'contact_name': 'Nathalia Sanchez', 'contact_email': 'Nathalia.sanchez@health.nsw.gov.au', 'contact_phone': None, 'lodgment_address': 'buy NSW Opportunities Hub', 'documents': [{'file_name': 'buy-nsw-tenders-terms-and-conditions-2024.pdf', 'file_type': 'pdf', 'storage_uri': 'gs://tenderai-dev-documents/tenders/buynsw/RFI-2017495 - EF26_553/bd537c4cc2756444-buy-nsw-tenders-terms-and-conditions-2024.pdf', 'extracted_text': ' \n \n1 \n \n \nbuy NSW Tenders module \nGlobal terms and conditions \n \n \nJuly 2024 \n \nGeneral \n1. In using the buy NSW Tenders module you:  \na. agree to terms and conditions contained herein and on buy.nsw.gov.au. \nb. agree that any information you provide is true, accurate, complete, current and not \nmisleading to the best of your knowledge and belief. \nc. agree to act in a way that is ethical, reasonable, with integrity, and complies with \napplicable laws when using the site. \nLodging a response \n2. Responses must be submitted electronically on the buy NSW Tenders module by a registered \nuser of buy NSW. \na. Responses (including all supporting information, if any) must be fully received by the \nclosing date and time. Note: The server date and time will be considered the correct date \nand time for the purposes of tenders closing. All information provided in the Response \nmust be in writing and in English. \nb. Incomplete or corrupted responses that cannot be effectively evaluated may be declined \nor not accepted. \nChanges to an opportunity \n3. An Opportunity may be amended before the closing date and time by issuing an amendment \nnotice. The amendment notice becomes part of the Opportunity.  \n4. It is the responsibility of all respondents: \na. to verify if an amendment notice has been issued prior to the closing date and time by \nchecking buy.nsw.gov.au, \nb. if necessary, respond to the amendment notice even if a Response has already been \nsubmitted by the Respondent.  \nQuestion and answers \n5. The buy nsw Tenders module includes question and answer functionality. You can submit a \nquestion to clarify tender requirements. Unless an amendment is issued the answer provided by \nthe Buyer is not considered part of the Opportunity. \n Use of responses \n6. In lodging a Response, you agree that the information provided: \n\na. can be used, shared and copied (including providing access to any third parties) for \nany purpose related to this Opportunity, and for internal procurement purposes. \nb. can be made available, on request, to any NSW government agency. This includes \ninformation dealing with the Respondent?s performance on any prior contract that has \nbeen awarded. Such information may be used by the recipient NSW Government \nagency for reasons including, but not limited to, the assessment of the suitability for \npre-qualification, selective tender lists, expressions of interest or the award of a \ncontract. \nc. is communication falling within section 30 of the Defamation Act 2005 (NSW). You shall \nhave no claim against the Principal and the State of New South Wales in respect of \nany matter arising out of the provision or receipt of such information, including any \nclaim for loss arising out of the communication. \nData security \n7. We maintain appropriate physical, technical, and administrative security to help prevent the loss, \nmisuse, unauthorised access, disclosure or modification of data on\u202fthe site. We do not accept any \nliability for the loss, misuse, unauthorised access, disclosure or modification of your data. \n8. To protect your data all Responses are encrypted at rest and in transit. Responses cannot be \ndecrypted until the close date and time. Responses can only be decrypted by the tender owner \nthrough the tender box opening process.  \nCorruption or unethical conduct \n9. A response may be declined or not considered if a Respondent, or any of its officers, employees, \nagents or sub-contractors is found to have: \na. offered any inducement, rebate, uncommercial/inflated pricing or reward to any \npublic servant or employee, agent or subcontractor of the Principal or the NSW \nGovernment in connection with this RFQ or the submitted Quotation; or \nb. committed corrupt conduct within the meaning of the Independent Commission \nAgainst Corruption Act 1988; or \nc. a record or alleged record of unethical behaviour. \n10. The Principal may, in its discretion, invite a relevant Respondent to provide written comments \nwithin a specified time before the Principal excludes the Respondent from the RFQ under clause \n10. \nNSW Government Procurement Policy Framework \n11. In submitting a response, the Respondent agrees to comply with the NSW Government \nProcurement Policy Framework (available at www.buy.nsw.gov.au).  \n12. If any Respondent fails to comply with these requirements, the Principal may take such failure \ninto account when considering the response and any subsequent response submitted by the \nRespondent and may result in the response being rejected. \nComplaints procedure \n13. It is the NSW Government?s objective to ensure that industry is given every opportunity to win \nGovernment contracts. Should any entity feel that it has been unfairly excluded from quotation \nor unfairly disadvantaged by the Agreement or the Statement of Requirements, it is invited to \nwrite to the Principal \n14. If a complaint is received concerning an alleged breach of an enforceable procurement provision \nunder Division 5 of Part 11 of the Public Works and Procurement Act 1912, this opportunity will be \nsuspended in accordance with the statutory requirements and invited Respondents will be \nnotified accordingly. \n\nBreaches to terms \n15. Respondents and users who breach the terms will be warned by the site?s administrator. \nSubsequent breaches will result in the user being blocked from accessing the site and legal \naction may be taken. \n16. If deemed necessary, breaches to terms may result in the termination of user accounts \n(including Supplier profiles), without liability for any loss. Once we end your user account, all \nrights granted to you in these terms will cease. \nJurisdiction \n17. The laws of New South Wales, Australia govern these terms. Any dispute must be heard in the \ncourts of New South Wales. \n18. If any of these terms are found to be invalid, illegal or unenforceable or for any reason by the \ncourts of New South Wales, the remaining terms will continue to apply in full. \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'RFI-BSNSW-Trailers-and-Self-drive-vans-replacement-project.pdf', 'file_type': 'pdf', 'storage_uri': 'gs://tenderai-dev-documents/tenders/buynsw/RFI-2017495 - EF26_553/150c81ff41984682-RFI-BSNSW-Trailers-and-Self-drive-vans-replacement-project.pdf', 'extracted_text': ' \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nCancer Institute NSW \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n  \nRequest for Information \n \n \n \n \nRFI No. \nRFI-2017495 - EF26/553 \nRFI Name \nBreastScreen NSW trailers and self-drive mobile vans replacement \n \nproject \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nwww.health.nsw.gov.au \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n\n \n \nCM File: ACI/D24/3394-01 \nAgency for Clinical Innovation \n \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nCONTENTS \n1. \nPublication and Contact Information \n4 \n1.1 \nAgency Details \n4 \n1.2 \nAuthorised Contact Officer \n4 \n1.3 \nClosing Date and Time \n4 \n1.4 \nDocument Standards for Submissions \n4 \n1.5 \nResponsibility for Response Costs \n4 \n1.6 \nAssessment of Responses \n4 \n1.7 \nConfidentiality \n4 \n1.8 \nFurther Process Stages following the RFI \n5 \n2. \nIntroduction \n5 \n2.1 \nAgency \n5 \n3. \nPurpose of the RFI \n5 \n3.1 \nAn opportunity to contribute and shape \n6 \n4. \nBackground \n6 \n4.1 \nScope of the RFI \n6 \n5. \nTECHNICAL REQUIREMENTS \n6 \n5.1 \nStandards \n6 \n5.2 \nGeneral Requirements \n6 \n5.3 \nVehicle Size \n7 \n5.4 \nVehicle Weight \n7 \n5.5 \nBody Layout \n7 \n5.6 \nMammography Room \n7 \n5.7 \nReception /Waiting Area \n8 \n5.8 \nAmenities Room \n8 \n5.9 \nWindows \n8 \n5.10 IT Communications Box And Isolator/Re-Set Switch \n8 \n5.11 Wheelchair Access and Loader \n8 \n5.12 Main Entry \n8 \n5.13 Stairs \n9 \n5.14 Stabilising Legs \n9 \n5.15 Awning \n9 \n5.16 Body Frame, Windows and Work Area \n9 \n5.17 Floor Construction \n9 \n5.18 Lighting \n9 \n\n \nCM File: ACI/D24/3394-01 \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n5.19 Air Conditioning \n10 \n5.20 Power Resources \n10 \n5.21 Power Requirements \n10 \n5.22 Power Board and Supply \n10 \n5.23 Cabling \n10 \n5.24 Vehicle ? Self Drive \n11 \n5.25 Suspension \n11 \n5.26 Water And Waste Management System \n12 \n5.27 Internal Finishing \n12 \n6. \nInformation Requested \n12 \n6.1 \nSupplier Capability and Experience \n12 \n6.2 \nDesign Innovation \n12 \n6.3 \nFleet Replacement Strategy \n12 \n6.4 \nSustainability \n12 \n6.5 \nMaintenance and Support \n12 \n6.6 \nCommercial Information \n12 \n6.7 \nMarket Capacity \n13 \n6.8 \nRisks and Challenges \n13 \n6.9 \nDelivery Model \n13 \n6.10 Delivery Timeframes \n13 \n6.11 Areas for Consideration \n13 \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \nCM File: ACI/D24/3394-01 \nCancer Institute NSW \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n1. \nPublication and Contact Information \n1.1 \nAgency Details \nAgency name \nCancer Institute NSW (The Institute)  \nAgency address \n1 Reserve Road, St Leonards NSW 2065 \n1.2 \nAuthorised Contact Officer \nQuestions regarding this RFI shall be submitted by e-mail to the following contact officer via email. \nQuestions may be answered by publication of an addenda to this RFI via the buy NSW Opportunities \nHub. \nContact Officer \n \nName  \nNathalia Sanchez \nTitle  \nProcurement & Contracts Officer \nEmail \nNathalia.sanchez@health.nsw.gov.au \n1.3 \nClosing Date and Time \nAll responses to this RFI are to be submitted via the buy NSW Opportunities Hub by the closing date \nand time detailed below. \nRespondents should not wait until the nominated closing time to lodge their submission as difficulties \nin uploading responses to the website may be encountered where many users attempt to upload \nresponses at the same time, or where attempting to upload large file sizes or volumes. \nClosing Date \n16 October 2026 \nClosing Time \n3.00 p.m. AEST \nDue to probity requirements, responses submitted by email may not be permitted or accepted. \n1.4 \nDocument Standards for Submissions \nElectronic submissions must be provided in either Microsoft Word (.doc or .docx) or PDF (.pdf). \nSupporting information may be provided in Microsoft Excel (.xls or .xlsx) or PowerPoint (.ppt), however \nthese files should be kept to a minimum. \n1.5 \nResponsibility for Response Costs \nThe respondent?s participation in this RFI process, or in relation to any matter concerning this RFI, is at \nthe respondent?s own risk and cost. The Institute will not be responsible for any costs or expenses \nincurred by any respondent in preparation or lodgement of a response to this RFI. \n1.6 \nAssessment of Responses \nResponses to this RFI will not be competitively evaluated. Responses will be reviewed and considered \nby the Institute as an information source for the purposes described in this RFI. \n1.7 \nConfidentiality \nThe Institute will treat all responses as commercial-in-confidence. \n \n \n\n \nRMS No: E26/20297  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n1.8 \nFurther Process Stages following the RFI \nFollowing the RFI process, the Institute (without limiting other options): \n(a) commence a new procurement process to provide of goods and/or services of the same or similar \nnature to those included in this RFI; \n(b) commence a new procurement process to provide of goods and/or services different to those \nincluded in this RFI; \n(c) decide not to proceed with any other procurement process for the goods and/or services. \nThis Request for Information does not create any representation (either express or implied): \n? that any tender or procurement process will proceed; \n? that if a tender or procurement process does proceed, that the participant?s tender or other form \nof application (if any) will be given any preference or special consideration; \n? does not indicate a commitment to any particular course of action \n2. \nIntroduction \nThe Institute is seeking information from the mobile van vehicle and trailers manufacturer industry on \nthe design, supply, construction, fit out and delivery of BreastScreen NSW (BSNSW) self-drive trucks \nand trailers.  \n2.1 \nAgency  \nThe Cancer Institute NSW (the Institute) is Australia?s first statewide cancer control agency, \nestablished in 2003 to lessen the impact of cancer in NSW.  The Cancer Institute operates as an \nindependent statutory agency within the NSW Health cluster and is governed by the Cancer Act 2003. \nThe Institute is a pillar organisation of NSW Health, providing the strategic direction for cancer control \nin NSW. \nThe BreastScreen New South Wales (BSNSW) Program utilises mobile services to provide equitable \naccess to breast cancer screening services for women who do not live in close proximity to a BSNSW \nfixed sites. \nThe BSNSW program currently comprises of 2 self-drive trucks and 12 semi-trailers attending \napproximately 200 mobile sites across a two-yearly cycle. The vehicles have a single Mammography \nroom, a reception/waiting room, a small change room and an amenities/clinical room. They are staffed \nby up to two radiographers and one administration staff member at any one time.  \nBSNSW staff are not expected to drive these vehicles. Drivers with vehicle licences are arranged to \nrelocate, position, and level the vans. \n3. \nPurpose of the RFI \nThe Institute would like to invite suppliers to submit written responses addressing their experience, \ncapability, capacity to build the mobile vans, recommended innovations and estimated budget. \n \n \n\n \nRMS No: E26/20297  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n3.1 \nAn opportunity to contribute and shape \nThis RFI is an opportunity for parties within the mobile van vehicle and trailers manufacturer industry \nto contribute information based on their knowledge and experience. \nThe Institute encourages participation, acknowledging that participation is completely voluntary. You \nmay choose to answer all or some of the questions. Please note this process is independent from any \nsubsequent procurement process and your participation or otherwise will have no bearing on any \nfuture procurement processes. \n4. \nBackground \nBSNSW mobile vans are reaching end of life and need to be replacement in a staggered approach \nbetween 2 to 5 years. \nAll BSNSW Services, including mobile services, perform digital mammography services.  \nThe build of new self-drive trucks and trailers should include the design, supply, construction, fit out \nand delivery. The vehicles should be able to accommodate digital mammography equipment and \nultrasound equipment.  \n4.1 \nScope of the RFI \nTo improve our knowledge and understanding of current approaches, the Institute seeks information \nand opinions on the building, design, supply, construction, fit out, delivery and estimated budget of \nthree self-drive trucks and twelve trailers for BSNSW. The vehicles should be able to accommodate \ndigital mammography equipment. \n5. \nTECHNICAL REQUIREMENTS  \n5.1 \nStandards \n5.1.1 \nEquipment shall comply with AS3200.1.0 (1998) - Medical electrical equipment Part 1.0: General \nrequirements for safety and AS3200.1.2 (2005) - Medical electrical equipment - General requirements for \nsafety - Collateral standard: Electromagnetic compatibility- Requirements and tests. The mobile breast \nscreen vehicle must comply with Australian and NSW Design Rules. \n5.1.2 \nRadiation Guideline 7: Radiation shielding design assessment and verification requirements. \n5.2 \nGeneral Requirements \n5.2.1 \nThe BSNSW self-drive trucks and trailers shall have the following key design attributes: \na) The capacity to maximize access to and around the mammography equipment, with particular \nattention made to staff limit their twisting, reaching and bending. \nb) Minimized pack up/set up time, allowing staff to operate safely and swiftly. \nc) Custom designed storage for all mammography and office components. \nd) Easy access to front & rear of PC\'s, UPS, Communications equipment using a \'floor or wall mounted\' \ncabinet. \ne) Be uniform in design \nf) \nMeet NSW road conditions \n5.2.2 It should accommodate the following: \na) Main and wheelchair entry awning/s \nb) Reception/waiting area with seating \nc) Functioning mammography room \nd) Internal and external storage \ne) Water and waste services \nf) \nElectrical works \ng) Ergonomic Acrylic Radiation shielding design, manufacture and supply that must comply with \nRadiation Guideline 7: Radiation shielding design assessment and verification requirements.  \nh) Acrylic Radiation shielding installation including mounting plates \ni) \nPlumbing works \nj) \nPower cable with electric wheel 30m \nk) Air conditioning  \n\n \nRMS No: E26/20297  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nl) \nLevelling system \nm) UPS \nn) Wheel Chair Lift \no) Music system \np) TV in the waiting area \n5.3 \nVehicle Size \n5.3.1 \nIn terms of external dimensions, the vehicle gross size shall not exceed the following: \na) Up to 12 metres (nominal) with a wheelbase and turning circle suitable for use within \nmetropolitan/outer metropolitan areas. \nb) No more than 2.4 metres wide \nc) No more than 4.3 metres high (whilst at maximum height of air ride suspensions). \n5.3.1.1 In terms of internal on-board dimensions, the usable on-board space available for BreastScreen staff to \noperate within should: \na) be a minimum of 10.5 metres in length \nb) be a minimum of 2.4 metres in height, and \nc) maximise the usable width. \n5.4 \nVehicle Weight \n5.4.1 \nIn terms of gross weight, the vehicle will be required to travel on sealed roads across regional, rural and \nmetropolitan regions. A limit to the weight \'Tare\' is not stipulated by commercial carriers, but gross \nvehicle mass (GVM) must be within the vehicle manufacturer guidelines for engine capacity and chassis \nfor self-drive trucks and trailers.  \n5.4.2 \nWeight loading on front and rear axles must be within manufacturer\'s guidelines and NSW\'s Transport \nregulations. \n5.5 \nBody Layout \n5.5.1 \nThe work is expected to include floor plan options for BSNSW utilising different lengths of internal space. \n5.5.2 \nConsideration should be given to slide-out options to increase the space within the mammography room \nand potentially reception room. \n5.6 \nMammography Room \n5.6.1 \nThe mammography room shall house all necessary mammography equipment. Circulation (free) space \nshould be sufficient for radiographers to safely and swiftly conduct a mammogram. \n5.6.2 \nThe mammography room should be a minimum of 4.2 metres in length complying with mammography \nequipment specifications. \n5.6.3 \nMust allow for wheelchair/client walker movement around equipment. Space for radiographer to move \nbehind client and wheelchair. \n5.6.4 \nThis room should be shielded for radiation protection purposes and must comply with Radiation Guideline \n7: Radiation shielding design assessment and verification requirements. The internal wall and the internal \ndoor between the mammography room and any other area must be minim 1.2 mm steel sheeting or \nequivalent. \n5.6.5 \nSecuring and fastening of mammography equipment \n? The mammogram gantry weighs approximately 800 kg with the unit rotating to enable images to be \ntaken from various angles. \n? The mammogram Acquisition Workstation weighs approximately 200 kg \n? The mammography equipment is expensive and sensitive to vibration. The mammography machine \nand associated equipment must be protected from mechanical shock, all sensitive equipment must \nalso be securely fastened when ready for travelling so that vibration does not cause deformation of \nload bearing structures and to stop movement. Therefore, the floor of the mammography room shall \nbe fitted with ample securing lockdown points for the attaching/lashing of straps prior to \ntransportation. These lockdown points will be designated by the mammography equipment \nmanufacturer. \n? The mammography equipment must at all times be securely fastened to the floor. \n5.6.6 \nConsole and radiation safety screen \n? The floor of the mammography room shall be fitted with an operator?s console and radiation protection \nscreen. \n\n \nRMS No: E26/20297  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n5.6.7 \nHand basin \n? An appropriately located small hand washing basin shall be installed. This shall be supplied with \nhot/warm and cold water delivered by a central mixer tap. A soap and paper towel dispenser shall be \nfitted close by. \n5.6.8 \nChange area and storage \n? A small area within the mammography room will be used by a client for removing garments and \ngetting dressed. It shall have a fixed seating bench with storage underneath fixed to the wall. \n? This area shall be part of the overall space available within the mammography room and be positioned \nto allow free movement of the radiographer independent to the client. \n5.7 \nReception /Waiting Area \n5.7.1 \nThe reception/waiting area shall be close to the entry and provide the following spaces: \na) Waiting space should allow seating for four people at a time \nb) Reception space should include a reception desk with sufficient room to house a laptop, monitor and \nprinter. \nc) Sufficient power points for 2 monitors, printer, computer, charging station  \nd) sufficient space for a printer/scanner (I so want to go electronic!), two 19in monitors, work space,  \ne) Ensure that the receptionist has visibility of the disabled access. \n5.8 \nAmenities Room \n5.8.1 \nThis room will be used for the purpose of providing 3 staff privacy for scheduled meal breaks. \nSoundproofing should be provided to this room to protect the privacy of staff and individuals. \n5.8.2 \nThis room should be supplied and fitted with the following equipment: \na) Bench and cupboards  \nb) a small bar-size dual/multi powered fridge \nc) a microwave  \nd) table suitable for 3 staff to sit and eat meals \ne) seating for 3 staff using fixed seating  \nf) \none basin sink  \ng) hot water system shall be provided, this can be a smaller <50 litre domestic system or alternative \nh) sufficient power points, to charge phones and have a laptop \ni) \nCleaning cupboard for storage of stick vacuum, broom, mop and bucket. \n5.9 \nWindows \n5.9.1 \nThe reception area and the amenities room shall have the ability to utilise natural light during operation. \nThis can be via 2 small to medium sized high set windows.. The windows should have suitable seals to \nprevent leakage in heavy rain. \n5.9.2 \nThe mammography room should not have any windows or skylight. \n5.9.3 \nDouble glazed windows. \n5.10 \nIT Communications Box And Isolator/Re-Set Switch \n5.10.1 \nThe main communications box shall be installed. The unit will be approximately 1000mm tall and 600 mm \nwide. \n5.11 \nWheelchair Access and Loader \n5.11.1 \nWheelchair access should be located directly into the mammography room. This should be via a fully \nautomatic wheelchair loader with a one-piece platform and a manual backup pump, folding handrails, \nremote control and pendant lead and an automatic outer roll stop that locks up prior to the platform \nleaving the ground. The wheelchair loader must fully comply with AS3856. \n5.12 \nMain Entry \n5.12.1 \nAn entry design which is secure and weatherproof that can be securely held open for long periods of \ntime shall be supplied.  \n5.12.2 There shall be an internal weatherproof door  \n5.12.3 The internal doors shall have a manual button options needs to be considered in the event of electrical \nor other failure. \n\n \nRMS No: E26/20297  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n5.12.4 The entry door/s i.e. both the main entry and the wheelchair entry should be on the near side of the vehicle \nand are to be covered by an awning. \n5.13 \nStairs \n5.13.1 \nThe following shall comply with current ASNZS standards for stairs: \na) Stairs must be stable and meet the needs of screening cohort of 40+ years of age \nb) step treads to be manufactured from a non-slip material or permanently coated with a non-slip \nmaterial \nc) the top step and entrance to the vehicle to be highlighted in a durable yellow non-slip material \nd) the stair pitch to be =/<30 degrees(check with Standards) \ne) the stair tread risers to be consistent in height and at the minimum height possible following \nstandard guidelines (150- 180 mm) \nf) \nthe stair treads to be a consistent depth of approximately 300 mm \ng) the stair tread width to be 900 mm \nh) a hand rail to be incorporated \ni) \nMechanism of stowing stairs that protects it from exposure to the external environment in \nsuboptimal conditions. \n5.13.2 The stairs shall allow single person operation. Automatic operation with manual override should be \nincluded. Stairs should be heavy duty in nature.  \n5.14 \nStabilising Legs \n5.14.1 \nStablising legs should be heavy duty in nature and should include skid feet. \n5.14.2  The stabilising legs deployment shall allow single person operation. Automatic operation with manual \noverride should be included. \n5.14.3 Similar to "SVM Hyleco system 1030"or "V-mux" system or ?Big Foot? levelling system. \n5.15 \nAwning \n5.15.1 \n3.15.1 \nA customised rigid awning/s shall be provided to cover both the main entry door stairs and the \nwheelchair access, thus providing a weather proof entry and exit for both of these locations. \n5.16 \nBody Frame, Windows and Work Area \n5.16.1 \nSub floor areas should be constructed with a rust proof finish during frame construction and a suitable \ncoating shall be immediately applied to the edges of all cuts or welds to restore the corrosion resistance \nof the material. \n5.16.2 Body frame shall have insulation for all weather conditions, with maximum reduction of heat transfer. \nThis shall be made of durable and fire retardant materials and comply with appropriate Australian \nStandards. \n5.16.3 Internal walls shall provide sound dampening and control where possible between work areas and \nreception area and exterior to the vehicle. \n5.16.4 The entire internal area shall be lined with polyester vinyl sheeting or similar material to both walls and \nceiling to facilitate easy cleaning.  \n5.16.5 Floor covering is to be suitable for a high throughput commercial area. \n5.17 \nFloor Construction \n5.17.1 \nThe floor is to be structurally supportive to take the direct weight of mammography equipment which \ncould be up to 800kg for the gantry and up to 200 kg for the Acquisition Workstation. \n5.17.2 The flooring shall be comprised of a composite, moisture resistant material that has anti-static properties \nand be easy to clean. \n5.18 \nLighting \n5.18.1 \nCeiling mounted covered lights to provide interior lighting of 150 Lumens shall be of the type that does \nnot dislodge while the vehicle is travelling. Dimmable recessed down lights shall be provided in the \nMammography room.  \n5.18.2 A dimmer switch is required in the mammography room.  \n5.18.3 External lights should be provided to fully illuminate both the entrance and rear of vehicle.  \n \n\n \nRMS No: E26/20297  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n5.19 \nAir Conditioning \n5.19.1 \nAn air conditioning system or systems shall be provided for all 3 rooms. \n5.19.2 The vehicle should function in all weather conditions throughout NSW  \n5.19.3 The air conditioning of the mammography room is a special case. In this room mammography equipment \nwill be installed that must be kept within a certain temperature range. The operating and non-operating \ntemperature ranges of the following mammography equipment (System 1) is required: \n \n \nSystem 1 \nDescription \nMammography X-ray Gantry \nOperating range \nTemperature (C): 20 - 30 \nHumidity (%): 20- 80 without condensing moisture \nNon-operating \n? \nrange \nTemperature (C): 10 ? 35 for a maximum of 12 hours \n10 ?C to 30 ?C indefinitely \nMaximum Rate of Temperature Change <10 ?C/hr. \nRelative Humidity (%): 10 ? 80 without condensing moisture \n \n5.19.4 The exterior panels of the vehicle and other panels must be thermally designed with sufficient thermal \ninsulation values (R values) to enable the air-conditioning system or systems to maintain the required \ntemperature range(s) of each room within the vehicle with a minimum expenditure of electrical energy. \n5.19.5 An A/C unit in mammography room must be able to run uninterrupted 24/7 (run while not connected to \npower by means of generator or other power source (eg during transit).  \n5.20 \nPower Resources \n5.20.1 The vehicle should have a capability for limited operation during power grid failure. \n5.20.2 The following two options should be provided: \na) Priority operation A - mains power, where the vehicle is connected to the available power grid via a \nlead/s, the mobile medical unit?s voltage requirement is 240/415 volts, three phase at 50 Hz, failure \nof this leads to; \nb) Priority operation B - an alternative power solution for use where sufficient mains power does not \nexist or there is a power failure ? to operate emergency lighting, and AC. Back?up input to the unit \npower HVAC shall be 240V, 50Hz, one phase. \n5.20.3 A UPS should be installed to enable a graceful shutdown of imaging equipment. \n5.21 \nPower Requirements \n5.21.1 \nFor the necessary technical details for the mammography machine and associated equipment refer to \nAttached mammography system ? Mobile Side Planning Guide. \n5.21.2 The Provider shall review expected current draw of all electrical items on board the vehicle, and shall \ntabulate this in the Technical Schedule. \n5.22 \nPower Board and Supply \n5.22.1 The power board must be easily accessible for maintenance and emergency purposes. All wiring within \nthe vehicle shall comply with all relevant Standards including aspects of the following Australian and \nNew Zealand Standards: \na) AS/NZS 3000 Wiring Rules \nb) AS/NZS 3001 Electrical Installation of Relocatable Premises \nc) AS/NZS 3003 Electrical Installation of patient treatment areas \n5.22.2 Further information on appropriate standards can be obtained from Standards Australia. \n5.22.3 Consideration of using solar power as a power source. \n5.23 Cabling \n5.23.1 Efficient operation of digital imaging equipment will depend on the performance of the cabling \ninfrastructure within the vehicle used to supply power and data signals. \n\n \nRMS No: E26/20297  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n5.23.2 Category 6 cabling is required throughout the vehicle for digital data, wireless signals and audio \ndistribution to the devices indicated in the table below: \n \n \nPower Distribution \nData Distribution \nRadio Distribution \nAudio \nDistribution \nDrivers? area \nExtra 12 Volt \nNil \nUHF/AM Radio \nCB Radio \nSat Phone \nReversing \nCamera \nMultipurpose room \nLighting \nComputer equipment \nAir \nconditioning \nSmall appliance \nComputer \nequipment \n \n \nMammography room \nLighting \nComputer \nequipment \nWireless \nData Speakers \n \nComputer equipment \nComputer rack \nSolution \n \n \nComputer rack \nNetwork\n \ncabl\ne \neg Telstra Next G \n \n \nAir conditioning \ntermination points  \n \n \nMammography \nmachine \nand \nWireless \nnetworking \n \n \n \nmonitor \ncomponents \n \n \n \nDedicated \ntemperature \ncontrol \nConsole \nWorkstation \n \n \n \ndevice for\n \nmammography \n \n \n \n \nmachine \n \n \n \n \nConsole/workstation \n \n \n \n \nNetwork cable\n \ntermination \n \n \n \n \npoints \n \n \n \n \nWireless\n \nnetworki\nng \n \n \n \n \ncomponents \n \n \n \n \nKeyboard/mouse \n \n \n \n \nUPS \n \n \n \nLocation of feed \n \nComputer Rack if \nin \nMammography \nRoom \nAntennas \non \nVehicle Roof \n \n \n5.24 \nVehicle ? Self Drive \n5.24.1 The vehicle is likely to travel distances of between 5,000 and 10,000 km annually, utilising both primary \nand secondary roads. It may be based.at a screening site from several days to months. The vehicle should \nbe capable of highway speeds. \n5.24.2 The vehicle shall come under the classification of "rigid chassis / cab chassis vehicle". Providers shall \nstate the approximate or upper limit Gross Vehicle Mass (GVM) of the vehicle being offered. Providers \nare encouraged to determine the best fit in regard to maximising the useable workspace whilst \nminimising the required GVM and not exceeding the manufacturer\'s chassis and front and rear axle GVM \nlimits. The vehicle is required to operate on NSW roads and exemptions or exceptions to compliance to \nallow registration of the vehicle will not be permitted. \n5.25 Suspension \n5.25.1 The suspension shall minimise damaging vibration to the supplied vehicle and its components and to all \nadditional on-board equipment including the mammography machine and associated equipment, along \nwith the electrical and air conditioning systems. Adjustable air ride/bag rear suspension is the minimum \nrequirement on the rear axle. Adjustability of suspension is required particularly to raise rear/tail \noverhang departure angles and front arrival angles when manoeuvring. \n5.25.2 Suspension must be suitable for roads being utilised across NSW and occasional operation on sporting \nfields, etc. \n \n \n\n \nRMS No: E26/20297  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n5.26 \nWater And Waste Management System \n5.26.1 Fresh drinking water and waste water tanks shall be installed \n5.26.2 Water Storage for approximately 300 litres with a volume gauge shall be provided. Grey water storage \nof 375 litres shall be provided with a volume gauge. \n5.26.3 A separate durable energy efficient hot water system shall be installed that is connected to the fresh \nwater tank and supplies hot water to all mixers. This system does not need to store significant volume of \nhot water, and 20 litres would be suitable for hand washing and cleaning purposes. \n5.27 \nInternal Finishing \n5.27.1 Internal finishings must meet infection control standards \n5.27.2 Internal finishings should be weather resistant in the event of water ingress  \n6. \nInformation Requested \nThe overall objective of the program is to obtain information and recommendations for the building, \ndesign, supply, construction, fit out and delivery of three self-drive trucks and twelve trailers for \nBSNSW. \nThe Institute is interested in receiving information and feedback in the following areas: \n6.1 \nSupplier Capability and Experience \n? \nDescribe your organisation\'s experience designing and manufacturing mobile healthcare vehicles. \n? \nProvide examples of similar projects completed within Australia or internationally. \n? \nDescribe your current manufacturing capacity and location of manufacturing facilities. \n? \nWhat is the typical build timeframe for vehicles of this complexity? \n6.2 \nDesign Innovation \n? \nWhat innovations have been incorporated into recent mobile healthcare vehicles? \n? \nWhat opportunities exist to improve patient experience, staff safety and operational efficiency? \n? \nLayouts that would improve functionality or workflow? \n6.3 \nFleet Replacement Strategy \n? \nWould you recommend replacing the fleet progressively or as larger batches? \n? \nWhat benefits or risks are associated with staged replacement? \n? \nWhat fleet standardization opportunities exist? \n6.4 \nSustainability \n? \nWhat sustainable design features would you recommend? \n? \nHybrid, battery-powered or renewable energy solutions viable. \n? \nWhat options exist to reduce fuel consumption and operating costs? \n6.5 \nMaintenance and Support \n? \nRecommended maintenance models. \n? \nAvailability of service support throughout NSW. \n? \nExpected lifecycle of vehicle structures and major components. \n6.6 \nCommercial Information \n? \nHigh-level indicative pricing. \n? \nKey cost drivers. \n\n \nRMS No: E26/20297  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n? \nTypical warranty periods. \n? \nWhole-of-life cost considerations. \n6.7 \nMarket Capacity \n? \nCurrent market conditions. \n? \nSupply chain constraints. \n? \nAvailability of chassis and specialist components. \n? \nAnticipated future market trends. \n6.8 \nRisks and Challenges \n? \nWhat are the major risks associated with a program of this nature? \n? \nAre there technical or regulatory challenges that should be considered? \n? \nWhat lessons learned from previous mobile healthcare vehicle projects should inform the project? \n6.9 \nDelivery Model \n? \nCan suppliers provide both vehicle manufacture and fit-out services? \n? \nWhat subcontracting arrangements are typically used? \n6.10 \nDelivery Timeframes \n? \nCurrent lead times for design and manufacture. \n? \nSupply chain factors likely to impact delivery. \n \nSuppliers are encouraged to provide high-level concept drawings, floorplans, renderings or case \nstudies demonstrating alternative vehicle layouts and design approaches. \nYou may include additional information on matters or issues not raised here. Responses may include as \nmuch or as little detail as you feel is necessary. \n6.11 \nAreas for Consideration \nRespondents may include in their response any or all of the following information: \n(a) \nProposed approach \n(b) \nRecommendation for design and construction \n(c) \nEnergy efficient/sustainability recommendations (noting that the vehicles travel to NSW \nregional and remote areas) \n(d) \nEstimated individual cost for a self-drive truck and trailer based on provided technical \nrequirements. \n\n'}], 'content_hash': None, 'tags': None, 'first_seen_at': None, 'last_scanned_at': '2026-09-29T04:19:13.772705+00:00', 'updated_at': None, 'raw_extra': '{"tags": ["supply of goods", "maintenance"]}', 'embedding': [0.07653948664665222, -0.01621290110051632, 0.017093030735850334, -0.002635485492646694, 0.0006424932507798076, 0.03206046670675278, 0.05208363011479378, 0.027528701350092888, 0.0235738605260849, 0.015821458771824837, 0.0038904044777154922, 0.07676012068986893, 0.03985317051410675, 0.005142617505043745, 0.02288714610040188, -0.005135096609592438, 0.0754854828119278, 0.035145070403814316, -0.05521045997738838, -0.032499995082616806, -0.03951803594827652, -0.02053268626332283, -0.06130971014499664, -0.007404321804642677, 0.00957757979631424, -0.04172539338469505, -0.013372190296649933, -0.01450300868600607, 0.02449946664273739, -0.05514660105109215, -0.015949061140418053, 0.005598988849669695, -0.031867675483226776, 0.009516377002000809, 0.041412774473428726, -0.0016250275075435638, 0.04145408049225807, 0.026209935545921326, 0.050946738570928574, -0.00015384380822069943, -0.0540163591504097, 0.015306815505027771, 0.025270333513617516, 0.009839730337262154, 0.024838976562023163, 0.026982929557561874, -0.02830292470753193, 0.037829600274562836, -0.057067934423685074, 0.03155193477869034, 0.034770410507917404, 0