2026-09-29T04:22:04.139480Z [] {'source_reference_id': 'SR01241', 'source_id': 'buynsw', 'source_url': 'https://buy.nsw.gov.au/prcOpportunity/8BE4B7BA-59FB-4337-A32DF974611A32AF', 'title': 'Training provider for leadership workshops ? Fresh Start program 2026-2029', 'issuing_agency': 'Department of Planning, Housing and Infrastructure', 'category': 'RFx', 'status': None, 'publish_date': None, 'closing_date': '2026-10-02', 'value_amount': None, 'value_currency': None, 'value_notes': None, 'location': 'New South Wales', 'description': 'The Office of Local Government (OLG) NSW, under the Department of Planning, Housing and Infrastructure, is seeking a qualified training provider to design, deliver, and administer a statewide leadership development program for current and emerging leaders within NSW local government councils. The program, part of the Fresh Start initiative, aims to strengthen leadership capability, improve team leadership, foster positive workplace cultures, and enhance competencies such as communication, influencing, collaboration, decision-making, and organizational effectiveness. The program will be delivered through a modular framework, with suppliers encouraged to propose 10-20 modules that councils can select from to create customized 2-3 day workshops. Delivery will primarily be online, with face-to-face options available in metropolitan and regional areas. Approximately 40-50 workshops are anticipated in the first year, targeting 500-1000 participants.\n\nThe scope of work includes designing learning content, developing resources (workshop materials, facilitator guides, participant resources), coordinating delivery schedules, delivering workshops, managing participant registrations and administration, and collecting evaluation data using OLG templates. A mandatory requirement is to partner with or subcontract Aboriginal organizations and/or subject matter experts for the development and/or delivery of the Cultural Capability module, focusing on First Nations cultural safety. OLG will promote the program, provide guidance, and assist with scheduling, but will not provide facilitators, develop content, manage registrations, or arrange travel/accommodation. Suppliers may propose optional complementary services like leadership coaching.\n\nThe initial contract term is 12 months, with two optional 12-month extensions. The tender closing date is 2nd October 2026, at 3 PM. A non-mandatory online briefing was held on 7th September 2026. Program delivery is expected to commence in late 2026 or early 2027, with some workshops potentially before Christmas 2026. Respondents must have a third-party certified quality management framework (e.g., AS/NZS ISO 9001, ASQA Standards, ISO 27001, SOC 2, IRAP assessment) or demonstrate equivalent externally accredited quality assurance. Supplier systems must comply with cybersecurity standards including ASD Essential Eight Maturity Model (Level Three), ISO/IEC 27001, ISO/IEC 27002, IEC 62443, and SOC 2 Type II, with alternative evidence considered during contract establishment. Principal Data must not be stored or accessed outside NSW without consent. The Principal will own new intellectual property created under the contract, while the supplier grants a perpetual, irrevocable, royalty-free license for their existing IP used in the program.', 'summary_headline': 'Office of Local Government NSW seeks training provider for a statewide leadership development program for local government employees.', 'contact_name': 'Nikki Ward', 'contact_email': 'nikki.ward@dphi.nsw.gov.au', 'contact_phone': None, 'lodgment_address': 'buy NSW Tenders module (https://buy.nsw.gov.au/login/signup)', 'documents': [{'file_name': 'buy-nsw-tenders-terms-and-conditions-2024.pdf', 'file_type': 'pdf', 'storage_uri': 'gs://tenderai-dev-documents/tenders/buynsw/SR01241/bd537c4cc2756444-buy-nsw-tenders-terms-and-conditions-2024.pdf', 'extracted_text': ' \n \n1 \n \n \nbuy NSW Tenders module \nGlobal terms and conditions \n \n \nJuly 2024 \n \nGeneral \n1. In using the buy NSW Tenders module you:  \na. agree to terms and conditions contained herein and on buy.nsw.gov.au. \nb. agree that any information you provide is true, accurate, complete, current and not \nmisleading to the best of your knowledge and belief. \nc. agree to act in a way that is ethical, reasonable, with integrity, and complies with \napplicable laws when using the site. \nLodging a response \n2. Responses must be submitted electronically on the buy NSW Tenders module by a registered \nuser of buy NSW. \na. Responses (including all supporting information, if any) must be fully received by the \nclosing date and time. Note: The server date and time will be considered the correct date \nand time for the purposes of tenders closing. All information provided in the Response \nmust be in writing and in English. \nb. Incomplete or corrupted responses that cannot be effectively evaluated may be declined \nor not accepted. \nChanges to an opportunity \n3. An Opportunity may be amended before the closing date and time by issuing an amendment \nnotice. The amendment notice becomes part of the Opportunity.  \n4. It is the responsibility of all respondents: \na. to verify if an amendment notice has been issued prior to the closing date and time by \nchecking buy.nsw.gov.au, \nb. if necessary, respond to the amendment notice even if a Response has already been \nsubmitted by the Respondent.  \nQuestion and answers \n5. The buy nsw Tenders module includes question and answer functionality. You can submit a \nquestion to clarify tender requirements. Unless an amendment is issued the answer provided by \nthe Buyer is not considered part of the Opportunity. \n Use of responses \n6. In lodging a Response, you agree that the information provided: \n\na. can be used, shared and copied (including providing access to any third parties) for \nany purpose related to this Opportunity, and for internal procurement purposes. \nb. can be made available, on request, to any NSW government agency. This includes \ninformation dealing with the Respondent?s performance on any prior contract that has \nbeen awarded. Such information may be used by the recipient NSW Government \nagency for reasons including, but not limited to, the assessment of the suitability for \npre-qualification, selective tender lists, expressions of interest or the award of a \ncontract. \nc. is communication falling within section 30 of the Defamation Act 2005 (NSW). You shall \nhave no claim against the Principal and the State of New South Wales in respect of \nany matter arising out of the provision or receipt of such information, including any \nclaim for loss arising out of the communication. \nData security \n7. We maintain appropriate physical, technical, and administrative security to help prevent the loss, \nmisuse, unauthorised access, disclosure or modification of data on\u202fthe site. We do not accept any \nliability for the loss, misuse, unauthorised access, disclosure or modification of your data. \n8. To protect your data all Responses are encrypted at rest and in transit. Responses cannot be \ndecrypted until the close date and time. Responses can only be decrypted by the tender owner \nthrough the tender box opening process.  \nCorruption or unethical conduct \n9. A response may be declined or not considered if a Respondent, or any of its officers, employees, \nagents or sub-contractors is found to have: \na. offered any inducement, rebate, uncommercial/inflated pricing or reward to any \npublic servant or employee, agent or subcontractor of the Principal or the NSW \nGovernment in connection with this RFQ or the submitted Quotation; or \nb. committed corrupt conduct within the meaning of the Independent Commission \nAgainst Corruption Act 1988; or \nc. a record or alleged record of unethical behaviour. \n10. The Principal may, in its discretion, invite a relevant Respondent to provide written comments \nwithin a specified time before the Principal excludes the Respondent from the RFQ under clause \n10. \nNSW Government Procurement Policy Framework \n11. In submitting a response, the Respondent agrees to comply with the NSW Government \nProcurement Policy Framework (available at www.buy.nsw.gov.au).  \n12. If any Respondent fails to comply with these requirements, the Principal may take such failure \ninto account when considering the response and any subsequent response submitted by the \nRespondent and may result in the response being rejected. \nComplaints procedure \n13. It is the NSW Government?s objective to ensure that industry is given every opportunity to win \nGovernment contracts. Should any entity feel that it has been unfairly excluded from quotation \nor unfairly disadvantaged by the Agreement or the Statement of Requirements, it is invited to \nwrite to the Principal \n14. If a complaint is received concerning an alleged breach of an enforceable procurement provision \nunder Division 5 of Part 11 of the Public Works and Procurement Act 1912, this opportunity will be \nsuspended in accordance with the statutory requirements and invited Respondents will be \nnotified accordingly. \n\nBreaches to terms \n15. Respondents and users who breach the terms will be warned by the site?s administrator. \nSubsequent breaches will result in the user being blocked from accessing the site and legal \naction may be taken. \n16. If deemed necessary, breaches to terms may result in the termination of user accounts \n(including Supplier profiles), without liability for any loss. Once we end your user account, all \nrights granted to you in these terms will cease. \nJurisdiction \n17. The laws of New South Wales, Australia govern these terms. Any dispute must be heard in the \ncourts of New South Wales. \n18. If any of these terms are found to be invalid, illegal or unenforceable or for any reason by the \ncourts of New South Wales, the remaining terms will continue to apply in full. \n\n'}, {'file_name': '20260831_SR01241 Part A - Conditions of Tendering_FINAL.docx', 'file_type': 'docx', 'storage_uri': 'gs://tenderai-dev-documents/tenders/buynsw/SR01241/ec8086dec06813dd-20260831_SR01241 Part A - Conditions of Tendering_FINAL.docx', 'extracted_text': 'GENERAL INFORMATION \n1.1\tPurpose of this document\nThis document invites suppliers to provide a response from a suitably qualified training provider to design, deliver and administer a statewide leadership development program for local government employees. The program will support the development of current and emerging leaders, strengthen workforce capability and contribute to improved organisational performance and leadership capacity across NSW councils. Please note: a RFx is a request for quotations, tenders, proposals or information for a procurement of goods and services.\n1.2\tSummary of requirements (PART D)\nPart D of these  documents defines in detail the scope of the project, the specifications of the goods and services that the Principal requires and the performance standards.\n timeframes\nThe table below highlights the important steps in this  process and the target dates for each one of these steps:\n1.4\tPrincipal?s Contact Person\nRespondents should refer any requests for information to the Principal?s Contact Person nominated on the cover of this document. All requests must be in writing.\nRespondents must not contact any other member of the Principal?s staff directly in relation to this  process.\nRespondents should notify the Principal?s Contact Person in writing on or before the Closing Date and Time if they find any discrepancy, error or omission in any part of this documentation.\nIf the Principal gives information to a Respondent, the Principal may give that same information to all other Respondents if the Principal believes the information would unfairly favour the inquiring Respondent over other Respondents.\n1.5\tBriefing \nIf details of a briefing are included at the beginning of this document, then the Principal will hold a briefing at the location and on the date and time nominated. Respondents should provide the Principal?s Contact Person with the name of the persons who will be attending the briefing at least 2 working days prior to the date of the briefing. No more than 2 people from each Respondent should attend the briefing.\nIf attendance at the briefing is mandatory (as indicated at the beginning of this document), then Respondents must attend the briefing. The Principal has discretion whether to accept Responses from Respondents who fail to attend a mandatory briefing.\n1.6\tAddendum\nThe Principal may change this set of documents by issuing an addendum in writing to all Respondents. The addendum becomes part of the set of documents.\nRespondents must check the web site address that they received the invite from https://buy.nsw.gov.au/opportunity/search and download the Addendum.\n1.7\tThe Principal?s discretion \nThe Principal may, at any time in its absolute discretion: \nchange any details of this , vary the scope of goods and services it requires, or issue an addendum; \nchoose to invite fresh Responses based on the same or different criteria; \naccept Responses that do not comply with all of the requirements of this , or that contain conditions or qualifications; \nrefuse to register or consider Responses which:\nare lodged by any means other than in accordance with the terms of this \nare lodged after the Closing Date and Time; \nuse modified response documents or include pricing information in their Part B non-price response (where applicable); or\ndo not conform with the terms of this ;\nrequest and consider further information from the Respondent or consider information from any other source, which the Principal requires to assess the Responses;\nhave regard to the Principal?s knowledge and previous experience and dealings with the Respondent, either individually or as part of a consortium, and information about its past or current performance from any source;\nenter direct negotiations with one or more preferred Respondents; \ncease negotiations or recommence negotiations with any Respondent; \naccept a Response by any Respondent; \naccept only part of a Response by any Respondent; \naccept Responses from more than one party; and\ndiscontinue the  process at any point for any reason, without making a determination regarding acceptance or rejection of any Responses.\n1.8\tRespondent?s status\nRespondents must be registered for GST and state their ABN in their Response. \nThe Principal may ask a Respondent to provide evidence of its legal status or capacity to contract. The Respondent must provide the evidence within 3 business days of the Principal?s request. If a Respondent is a trustee, the evidence may include a copy of the relevant trust deed. \n1.9\tThe Principal?s Liability \nThe Principal is not liable to any recipient of this  in connection with this  process including:\nany costs incurred in preparing a Response; \nany exercise by the Principal, or failure to exercise, any discretion or right it has under this ; \nany error or omission in this  or any information provided to or received by a Respondent; or\nany decision by the Principal not to proceed with the procurement as contemplated by this  in part or at all.\nTo the maximum extent permitted by law, any obligations and liabilities which may otherwise be implied or imposed on the Principal under contract, in equity, by statute or otherwise are excluded.\nThe Principal may exercise its discretion to not give reasons for any decision.\n 1.10\tConfidentiality and Intellectual Property Rights\n documents\nThe Principal makes available this  and all related documents on a commercial in confidence basis. Any person in receipt of this  must ensure that all information whether written or verbal concerning this  is kept confidential, except any information which is in the public domain (other than as a consequence of a breach of this confidentiality obligation).\nThe Respondent must keep confidential any information concerning the Principal or the State of New South Wales, as a result of or in connection with its submission of a Response, unless otherwise agreed in writing.\nCopies of this document or related documents must not be distributed except with the prior written consent of the Principal.\nThese obligations apply equally to any sub-contractor used by the Respondent.\nResponse documents\nThe Response and any accompanying documents become the property of the Principal. \nAll intellectual property developed by the successful Respondent during the course of providing the services will vest in the Principal, unless expressly stated otherwise in the contract or these  documents.\nThe parties agree that nothing in this document affects or otherwise assigns ownership of any Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property (ICIP) that is supplied or disclosed by the Respondent or the Principal in the course of this procurement activity, as the term ICIP is defined in the Principal?s Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property Protocol (ICIP Protocol) as published (https://www.nsw.gov.au/departments-and-agencies/department-of-planning-housing-and-infrastructure/aboriginal-outcomes) or otherwise made available to the Respondent from time to time. The parties agree to handle any ICIP supplied or disclosed by the Respondent in accordance with the ICIP Protocol.\n1.11\tCode of Conduct \nRespondents must comply with the NSW Supplier Code of Conduct. In submitting Responses, Respondents signify agreement to comply with the Code, available at: https://buy.nsw.gov.au/policy-library/policies/supplier-code-of-conduct \nIf a Respondent fails to comply with the Code, the Principal may take that failure into account when considering Responses and may pass over Responses without prejudice to any other rights of action or remedies available to the Principal.\n1.12\tConflict of interest\nA conflict of interest arises when a Respondent, or a person or organisation associated with the Respondent, is in a position to benefit directly or indirectly from actions of the Respondent through an unfair or unintended imposition or loss on the Principal or other party. A conflict of interest can also arise when a Respondent?s integrity, objectivity or fairness in providing goods or services is at risk due to a personal interest or conflicting business arrangements.\nRespondents must disclose in their Responses any potential or actual conflicts of interest that they may have or may be perceived to have should they be successful.\nIdentification of a conflict of interest or a perceived conflict of interest will not automatically exclude a Respondent from consideration. The Principal reserves the right to assess the potential impact of the conflict or perceived conflict in relation to the Response before a final decision is made. However, the Principal?s decision about exclusion will be final.\n\n1.13\tExchange of information between government agencies\nBy submitting a Response, the Respondent authorises the Principal to gather, assess and communicate to Commonwealth, other State and local government agencies and authorities any information about the Respondent, including its financial position and the Respondent?s performance in respect of any contract awarded as a result of this  process. Such information may be used for assessment of suitability of future response, tender, prequalification or contract opportunities.\nThe information that may be collected, exchanged and used in accordance with this provision includes ?personal information? about a Respondent for the purposes of the Privacy and Personal Information Act 1998.\nThe Respondent agrees that the Principal?s provision of information to any other agency or authority is a communication falling within section 30 of the Defamation Act 2005, and the Respondent has no claim against the Principal or the State of New South Wales in respect of any matter arising out of the provision or receipt of such information, including any claim for loss to the Respondent arising out of the communication.\n1.14\tFinancial Capability of Respondent\nThe Principal reserves the right to reject any Response if the Principal judges the Respondent not to have appropriate financial capability.\nThe Principal will not knowingly accept Responses from or award contracts to Respondents who are subject to exclusion from tendering as a result of a breach of the NSW Supplier Code of Conduct, are bankrupt, are subject to a winding up order, or are corporate entities with persons involved directly or indirectly in the management of the entity who are disqualified by law.\n1.15\tWork Health & Safety \nThe Respondent must conduct its own assessments and investigations regarding the work health and safety of all persons affected by the goods and services sought under this  process. The successful Respondent (if any) will be required to comply with the Work Health & Safety Act 2011, any related regulations and codes of practice, and any directions, policies and guidelines given by the Principal. \n1.16\tUse of Artificial Intelligence (AI) in Responses\nRespondents may use AI-enabled tools in the preparation of their Responses; however, the Respondent remains wholly responsible for the accuracy, completeness, originality, and compliance of the Response. Respondents must not disclose or upload the Principal?s confidential information, RFx documents, or data to publicly available AI systems or tools in preparing their Response.\nThe Principal may exclude a Response from consideration if it reasonably believes that the Respondent?s use of AI-enabled tools has resulted in a breach of confidentiality, misrepresentation, or non-compliance with these Conditions of Tendering.\nWhere the Proforma Contract or Statement of Requirements does not define "AI System" or "AI-enabled tool," the following definition shall apply:\nAI System or AI-enabled tool means a model, or a machine-based system that, for explicit or implicit objectives, infers, from the input it receives, how to generate outputs such as predictions, content, recommendations, or decisions that can influence physical or virtual environments.\nFor clarity, where the Proforma Contract or Statement of Requirements includes definitions for "AI System" or "AI-enabled tool," those definitions shall take precedence.\nLODGEMENT\n2.1\tInstructions for Respondents \nRespondents must read all parts of this document and submit a completed PART B ? Response Form (including any Part B attachments such as pricing schedules).\nBefore submitting a Response, a Respondent must:\nexamine all information relevant to the risks and contingencies and other circumstances having an effect on its Response\nsatisfy itself that the Response, including the price, is correct\nbe satisfied that it is financially and practically viable for the Respondent to enter into and perform the proposed contract if it were chosen to do so by the Principal.\nResponses must:\nbe in plain English and be legible\nexpress measurements in Australian legal units of measurement, unless otherwise required by the scope\nclearly label and cross-reference any attachments\nspecify pricing information:\nin your native currency;\ninclusive of all other charges, expenses, duties and taxes; and\nGST and other expenses (including disbursements) where applicable, must be separately identified in the prices provided.\n2.2\tConformity of responses \nThe Principal seeks conforming Responses. The Principal may exclude non-conforming Responses from the  process without further consideration.\n2.3\tAlternative responses  \nRespondents may submit an alternative Response but only if submitted in conjunction with a conforming response and marked ?Alternative Response?. The Principal may consider an alternative Response in its absolute discretion.\n\n2.4\tClosing Date & Time \nRespondents must complete and submit Part B ? Response Form and any Part B attachments, by the Closing Date and Time stated on the cover of this document. \n2.5\tExtension to Closing Date and Time \nThe Principal may, in its discretion, extend the Closing Date and Time. \n2.6\tLate Responses  \nThe Principal may consider late Responses where the Principal is satisfied that the integrity and competitiveness of the  process has not been compromised. \n2.7\tElectronic Lodgement \nThe Principal will treat a Response submitted electronically in accordance with the Electronic Transactions Act 2000 (NSW), and given no lesser level of confidentiality, probity and attention than Responses lodged by other means.\nThe Respondent will need to have a\xa0buy NSW Supplier Hub account to respond to a tender. If you do not have an account, you can register here at https://buy.nsw.gov.au/login/signup. \nWhen a Respondent submits a Response through the buy.NSW website, the Respondent is taken to have accepted the Terms of use on the buy.NSW website at https://buy.nsw.gov.au/opportunity/search\nIf a Respondent experiences any persistent difficulty with the buy.NSW website in submitting a Response, the Respondent should advise buy.NSW immediately on 1800 679 289. If there is a defect or failure of the buy.NSW website and the Principal is advised, the Principal may extend the Closing Date and Closing Time.\nElectronically submitted Responses may be made corrupt or incomplete, for example by computer viruses. The Principal may decline to consider a Response that the Principal cannot effectively evaluate because it is incomplete or corrupt. \nThe Response:\nfile name should incorporate the Respondent?s company name\nfile name should reflect the various parts of the Response they represent, where the Response comprises multiple files\nfile and folder names must not exceed 35 characters in length\nmust not exceed 10 sub-folder levels \nmust not exceed 1 GB per upload \nFolders within folders are permitted. Zips within zips are not accepted\nmust be completely self-contained. No hyperlinked or other material may be incorporated by reference\n2.8\tMinimum Validity Period   \nAll responses remain valid for a minimum of 90 days from the Closing Date. \n2.9\tFurther information \nThe Principal may request a Respondent to provide further information after the Closing Date to assist the evaluation  process. The Respondent should submit such information in the format and by the time requested.\n2.10\tVariation of responses \nAt any time after the Closing Date and before the Principal accepts a Response, a Respondent may vary its Response: by providing the Principal with further information by way of explanation; to correct a mistake (so long as the original response is not substantially altered, or the Respondent is not given an unfair advantage over other Respondents); or by documenting agreed changes negotiated. The Principal may provide all other Respondents whose Responses have similar characteristics with the opportunity of varying their Responses in a similar way.\nEVALUATION\n3.1\tEvaluation methodology\nThe objective of the evaluation is to identify the Response(s) that best meet the Principal?s requirements and represent best value for money for the Principal.\nThe Principal?s evaluation team will evaluate Responses against the evaluation criteria set out in Part B. \nThe Principal may, during the evaluation process, exclude a Response from further consideration at any stage of the RFx process if it is:\nfound not to comply with Conditions and Requirement of Tendering;\nevaluated as not meeting or addressing a criterion or RFx requirement;\nfound to be unacceptable in terms of effectiveness against the evaluation criteria;\nevaluated as representing a greater than acceptable price;\nfound to be presenting unacceptable risk;\nincomplete.\nThe Principal is not bound to accept the lowest cost Response. \nThe Principal may also take into account any other considerations such as financial viability (including bankruptcy or insolvency) of the Respondent, either individually or as a member of a Respondent consortium, or any other information about the Respondent that the Principal receives from any other source.\nA Response is not accepted unless and until the Respondent and the Principal execute a binding written contract.\n3.2\tMinimum Requirements\nThe Principal may exclude from further consideration any Respondents who do not comply with the following minimum requirements:\nThe requirements in section 2.1 (Instructions for Respondents)\nThe requirements in section 2.7 (Electronic Lodgement)\nThe mandatory requirements listed in Part B and/or Part D.\n3.3\tEvaluation Criteria\nIn evaluating Responses, the Principal may take into consideration factors including, but not limited to, the evaluation criteria which are listed in Part B. \n3.4\tSite inspections and presentations  \nThe Principal may undertake site inspections of the Respondents? or their subcontractors? premises or invite Respondents to make presentations regarding their Responses. A presentation does not represent a commitment by the Principal to accept any aspect of a Response. The Principal may take into account any information obtained during the course of a presentation or site inspection in the evaluation of Responses. \n3.5 Modern Slavery\nModern Slavery: \n(a) means any conduct that constitutes or would constitute a modern slavery offence, being an offense described in Schedule 2 of the Modern Slavery Act 2018 (NSW), or an offence of attempting or incitement to commit an offence described in Schedule 2 of the Modern Slavery Act 2018 (NSW).\n(b) includes any conduct that constitutes or would constitute an offence under any of the Modern Slavery Laws as amended from time to time, including an offence of attempting or incitement to commit such an offence; and\n(c)  includes conduct engaged in elsewhere than in New South Wales that, if it occurred in New South Wales, would constitute a modern slavery offence under paragraphs (a) or (b).\nModern Slavery Laws means: \n(a) the Modern Slavery Act 2018 (Cth);\n(b) the Modern Slavery Act 2018 (NSW); \n(c) Divisions 270 and 271 of the Commonwealth Criminal Code; \n(d) section 176(1A) of the Public Works and Procurement Act 1912 (NSW); \n(e) section 438ZE of the Local Government Act 1993 (NSW); and \n(f) any other laws, regulations, codes and international conventions aimed at combatting modern slavery, forced labour or human trafficking, from time to time in force in or ratified by Australia and, where relevant, in or by other jurisdictions in which the parties operate, as amended from time to time. \nReasonable Steps means measures, steps or actions required by the NSW Anti-slavery Commissioner?s Guidance on Reasonable Steps, as in force from time to time.\nBy lodging a Response, the Respondent agrees that, if it is the successful Respondent, it may be required to comply with modern slavery contract clauses in the contract entered into with the Principal.\nBy submitting a Response, the Respondent acknowledges and agrees that if it is awarded the RFx based on the pricing or compensation information it has submitted in its Response, this submitted pricing or compensation will allow it to perform the contract without causing or contributing to Modern Slavery, and to provide or enable an effective remedy to any Modern Slavery it does cause or to which it does contribute\nOUTCOME\n4.1\tPost Negotiations\nBefore making any determination as to acceptance or rejection of Responses, the Principal may elect to conduct limited negotiation with preferred Respondent(s).\nThe Principal may at its absolute discretion elect to conduct post RFx negotiations and/or seek a Best and Final Offer with more than 1 Respondent in the event that it decides that the closeness of the Responses or timing constraints warrant doing so.\n4.2\tMethod of Acceptance - Contract\nNo Response is accepted unless and until the Principal and successful Respondent sign a contract which will be based on the Part C ? Draft Contract. The Principal will generally not enter into negotiations on Part C ? Draft Contract. The Principal reserves the right to make changes to the draft contract before entering into a final contract with the successful Respondent.\n4.3\tDebriefing for unsuccessful Respondents\nAt the conclusion of the  process, the Principal will inform all Respondents in writing of the outcome of their Response.\nThe Principal may offer unsuccessful Respondents the opportunity for a debriefing.\nDuring the  process the Principal will not accept or respond to request for information on the status and progress of the evaluation.\n4.4\tPublic disclosure\nThe Principal may be required to publicly disclose details of any Responses and of any contract awarded, in accordance with the Government Information (Public Access) Act 2009. \n4.5\tComplaints \nIt is the NSW Government?s objective to ensure that industry is given every opportunity to win Government contracts. Should any person or entity feel that it has been unfairly excluded from quoting or unfairly disadvantaged by the terms of this  process, they are invited to write to: \nDirector, Procurement and Chief Procurement Officer\nDepartment of Planning, Housing and Infrastructure\nEmail: procurement@dpie.nsw.gov.au\n'}, {'file_name': '20260831_SR01241 Part B - Response form_FINAL.docx', 'file_type': 'docx', 'storage_uri': 'gs://tenderai-dev-documents/tenders/buynsw/SR01241/0907fc78b0c7c500-20260831_SR01241 Part B - Response form_FINAL.docx', 'extracted_text': None}, {'file_name': '20260831_SR01241 Part C - Goods and Services Contract_FINAL.docx', 'file_type': 'docx', 'storage_uri': 'gs://tenderai-dev-documents/tenders/buynsw/SR01241/bf2b8776d39401ae-20260831_SR01241 Part C - Goods and Services Contract_FINAL.docx', 'extracted_text': "Goods and Services Contract\nFresh Start Leadership Program \nProcurement Number: SR01241\nThe Crown in the right of the State of New South Wales acting through the Department of Planning, Housing and Infrastructure\nABN 20 770 707 468\n[Insert Supplier?s full legal name]\nABN [Insert Supplier ABN]\nTable of Contents\nSigning Page\nBy executing this Agreement, the parties acknowledge that they have read, and accept and agree to be bound by, the Terms and Conditions attached below.\nExecuted by the parties as an agreement.\n[User Instruction: Please consult with your Legal Team if required to ensure the signature block for the Principal is completed correctly, including, for example, the description of the Principal and whether the Principal executes in its own right or on behalf of the Crown. If an authorised signatory is signing on behalf of the Principal, please confirm the relevant delegations or authorisations being relied upon.]\n[User Instruction: The Supplier?s execution block must reflect the Supplier?s legal structure (i.e. company incorporated in Australia, sole trader, trust etc) and approved signatories. Several different options are provided below. Select the most appropriate option and delete the options that are not applicable. If none of the below options are appropriate, please consult with your Legal Team.]\n[Option 1: Where the Supplier is a company incorporated in Australia by two directors or a director and a secretary.]\n[Option 2: Where the Supplier is a company incorporated in Australia by a sole director and secretary being the same person or where there is no company secretary.]\nSignature of Sole Director [and Sole Company Secretary]\nStrike through if not signed electronically** I acknowledge this Agreement has been electronically signed by me, the Sole Director (and if noted above, the Sole Company Secretary) named below, affixed on the date specified below.\nPrint full name\nDate\n[Option 3: Use of individuals/sole traders is high risk. Where it is unavoidable to deal with an individual/sole trader, additional advice from the relevant legal, finance and HR/WHS team should be sought regarding superannuation, workers compensation and other risk factors.]\n[Option 4: Where the Supplier is a company signing in its capacity as a trustee of a trust. If this execution block is used, you should request a copy of the relevant trust deed and seek guidance from your Legal Team to confirm that the trustee is appropriately authorised to execute this Agreement on behalf of the trust. Additional provisions may be required where the Supplier is acting as trustee of a trust.]\n[Option 5: Where the Supplier is an individual signing in their capacity as a trustee of a trust. If this execution block is used, you should request a copy of the relevant trust deed and seek guidance from your Legal Team to confirm that the trustee is appropriately authorised to execute this Agreement on behalf of the trust. Additional provisions may be required where the Supplier is acting as trustee of a trust.]\nTerms and Conditions\nThe parties agree:\nDefinitions and interpretation\nDefinitions\nIn this Agreement:\nAdvantage means any financial or other advantage, payment, gift, promise or transfer of anything of value\nAgreement means this agreement, including all of the parts and documents listed or referred to in clause\xa01.2.9.\nAgreement Details means the section of this Agreement headed ?Agreement Details?.\nAI System means a model, or a machine-based system that, for explicit or implicit objectives, infers, from the input it receives, how to generate outputs such as predictions, content, recommendations, or decisions that can influence physical or virtual environments.\xa0\nApplicable Corruption Law means all Laws relating to bribery, corruption, money laundering, fraud or similar activities within or outside of Australia.\xa0\nAuthority includes any Government Agency, governmental or semi-governmental or local government authority, administrative, regulatory or judicial body or tribunal, department, commission, public authority, agency, Minister, statutory corporation or instrumentality.\nBest Industry Practice means a standard of service or deliverable, in terms of quality, productivity, performance, cost and timeliness of delivery, that, when considered collectively, is equal to or better than the commonly accepted best practice being provided at the relevant time by a supplier of like or similar goods, services, deliverables and activities to the Goods and Services throughout the world.\nBusiness Day means a day other than a Saturday, Sunday or gazetted public holiday in New South Wales, Australia.\nBusiness Hours means the hours between 9:00am and 5:00pm on any Business Day.\nChange in Control means, in respect of an entity, the occurrence of any circumstances or events following which the entity, who was not so controlled before, is controlled by another person, alone or together with any Related Body Corporate, and: \nincludes, in respect of the entity, a change of a direct holding of at least fifteen percent of the voting shares in that entity or a holding company of that entity; however\nexcludes an internal solvent corporate reorganisation occurring exclusively within the group of companies comprised of the Supplier and its Related Bodies Corporate.\nClaim means any allegation, cause of action, liability, claim, proceeding, suit or demand of any nature, whatsoever arising, and whether present or future, fixed or unascertained, actual or contingent and whether at Law, under statute or otherwise.\nCommencement Date has the meaning given to that term in the Agreement Details.\nCommissioner means the relevant ?Information Commissioner? or ?Privacy Commissioner? (or similar authorities) under applicable Laws.\nConfidential Information means, in relation to a party, all information related to or connected with that party and its affairs, including IP Rights, financial information, the existence or terms of any contract (including this Agreement), Personal Information, ?health information? (as defined in the Privacy Laws), and any other commercially or scientifically valuable information of whatever description and in whatever form (whether written or oral, visible or invisible, or received before or after the date of this Agreement), including (in the case of the Principal) the Principal Data and Principal IP, but not including any information that:\n(a)\tis in the public domain other than as a result of a breach of any obligation of confidentiality (for clarity, a combination of information will not be taken to be in the public domain merely because it contains some information which is in the public domain); or\n(b)\tthe Recipient independently knew other than as a result of a breach of any obligation of confidentiality.\nConflict of Interest means the Supplier or its Personnel engaging in any activity or having any interest which conflicts with, or is likely to or may  be perceived to conflict with, the Supplier?s ability to fairly and independently perform this Agreement.\nConsequential Loss means any loss, not arising naturally, that is according to the usual course of things, from the relevant breach act or omission, whether or not such loss may reasonably be supposed to have been in the contemplation of the parties, at the time they entered the agreement, as the probable result of the relevant breach.\nCorrectly Rendered Invoice means an invoice which:\nincludes the details of the relevant Goods and Services (including any Deliverables) provided; \nspecifies a correctly calculated amount due for payment under this Agreement;\nis a tax invoice under the GST Law; \nincludes a subcontractor?s statement regarding workers' compensation, payroll tax and remuneration in the form specified at https://www.revenue.nsw.gov.au/help-centre/resources-library/forms/payroll/opt011.pdf (or such other site or form as advised by the Principal from time to time); and\ncomplies with any additional criteria notified by the Principal from time to time.\nCorrupt Conduct has the meaning in the Independent Commission Against Corruption Act 1988 (NSW).\nData Breach has the meaning given to that term in clause\xa014.1.\nDelay has the meaning given to that term in clause\xa03.5.1.\nDeliverable means the deliverables (if any) set out or referred to in the Statement of Work and any materials arising out of or brought into existence as part of the Services, including any Goods, software or documentation.\nDocumentation means the documentation set out in the Statement of Work and all other documentation necessary to enable the Principal to make full and proper use of the Goods and Services. \nDue Date means any date or timeframe by which the Supplier is required to perform its obligations under this Agreement (including for the supply of Goods or provision of the Services and other milestone set out in the Statement of Work). \nExtension Term has the meaning given to that term in the Agreement Details. \nFees means the fees and charges set out, referred to in or calculated in accordance with the Statement of Work.\nForce Majeure Event means any act of God, fire, flood, earthquake, national emergency (including terrorist acts), riot, civil disorder, war or any other act or omission of a third party (other than, in the case of the Supplier, its Personnel) that is beyond the reasonable control of the affected party, but in each case only to the extent that the affected party is without fault in causing or contributing to or compounding the effects of the relevant event and the event or its effects could not have been prevented with reasonable precautions.\nGIPA Act means Government Information (Public Access) Act 2009 (NSW).\nGoods means the goods (if any) set out or referred to in the Statement of Work.\nGovernment Agency means any of the following:\na government sector agency (within the meaning of the Government Sector Employment Act 2013 (NSW));\na New South Wales Government agency;\nany other public authority that is constituted by or under an Act or that exercises public functions for or on behalf of the State of New South Wales (other than a State owned corporation); or\nany State owned corporation prescribed by regulations under the Public Works and Procurement Act 1912 (NSW).\nGST has the meaning given to that term in the GST Law.\nGST Law means A New Tax System (Goods and Services Tax) Act 1999 (Cth).\nICAC means the Independent Commission Against Corruption.\nICIP Schedule means the ?ICIP Schedule? set out in Schedule 2.\nInitial Term has the meaning given to that term in the Agreement Details. \nIndigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property or ICIP means the cultural heritage, traditional knowledge and traditional cultural expressions belonging to Aboriginal Persons as referred to in Article 31 of the United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples. Cultural heritage, traditional knowledge and traditional cultural expression comprises all objects, sites and knowledge, the nature or use of which has been transmitted or continues to be transmitted from generation to generation, and which is regarded as pertaining to particular Aboriginal Persons or their territory. ICIP includes:\ncultural knowledge (knowledge of Country and what healthy Country looks like, ecological knowledge, scientific, agricultural, technical knowledge, ritual knowledge);\ntraditional cultural expression (traditional land management practices, stories of Country, art, architecture, designs and symbols and literature);\nlanguages (including oral or written language words);\nperformances (ceremonies, dance and song);\ncultural objects (including, but not limited to arts, crafts, ceramics, jewellery, weapons, tools, visual arts, photographs, textiles, contemporary art practices);\nhuman remains and tissues;\nplaces, sacred and historically significant sites and burial grounds;\nthe secret and sacred material and information (including sacred/historically significant sites and burial grounds); and\ndocumentation of Aboriginal Persons? heritage in all forms of media such as films, sound recordings, photographs, books, reports, records, digital databases and includes documentation taken by others.\nInsurance has the meaning given to that term in the Agreement Details.\nIP Rights includes all present and future rights anywhere in the world conferred by Law or in relation to copyright, inventions, plant varieties, trademarks, designs, patents and all other rights resulting from intellectual activity in the industrial, scientific, commercial, literary or artistic fields, including trade secrets and know-how (whether registered or unregistered, and including applications for any of the foregoing) and including all rights in applications to register these rights, all renewals and extensions of these rights and all rights in the nature of these rights.\nLaws means any legally binding law, legislation, statute, act, regulation, subordinate legislation, rule, by-law, order, proclamation, decree, ordinance, directive or code which is enacted, issued or promulgated from time to time in any relevant jurisdiction (including the Commonwealth or any State or Territory government) and any applicable common law and rule or principle of equity. In the context of clause 11, Laws also includes the rules of a relevant stock-exchange. \nLimitation Amount has the meaning given to that term in clause\xa019.2.\nLoss includes Claims, actions, proceedings, losses, damages, liabilities, costs and expenses (including legal expenses on a full indemnity basis).\nMachine Learning Tool means a kind of AI System which builds systems that can learn from data and improve their performance over time without being explicitly programmed.\nMinimum Limitation Amount has the meaning given to that term in clause\xa019.2.\nMoral Rights means a person's moral rights as defined in the Copyright Act 1968 (Cth) and any other similar rights existing under any other Laws.\nNew IP means any IP Rights that are created by the Supplier or its Personnel, or which arise, under or in connection with the Supplier?s or its Personnel?s performance of this Agreement, including any newly created IP Rights in the Goods and Services. New IP excludes Supplier IP and Principal IP.\nPersonal Information has the meaning given to that term in the Privacy Laws.\nPersonnel means a party?s directors, officers, employees, agents, contractors and subcontractors (provided that neither party is the Personnel of the other party) and, in the case of the Supplier, includes any persons carrying out the Supplier?s obligations on the Supplier?s behalf.\nPID Act means the Public Interest Disclosures Act 2022 (NSW).\nPrincipal Data means all data (including metadata) and information relating to the operations, facilities, customers, clients, Personnel, assets and programs of the Principal, including Personal Information, in whatever form that information may exist and whether created, captured, collected, entered into, stored in, generated by, controlled, managed, retrieved, transferred, transmitted, printed, processed or produced as part of the provision of the Goods or performance of the Services.\nPrincipal IP means any IP Rights owned or licensed by the Principal (other than any Supplier IP), including Principal Data, and any development, modification, enhancement, improvement or adaptation of such materials.\nPrincipal Policies means any NSW Government policies, guidelines and codes of conduct notified to the Supplier by the Principal from time to time, including any Principal policies, guidelines or codes of conduct set out or referred to in the Statement of Work.\nPrincipal?s Representative means the person specified in the Agreement Details as the Principal?s representative (or any replacement person notified in writing to the Supplier from time to time).\nPrivacy Laws means the Privacy and Personal Information Protection Act 1998 (NSW), the Privacy Act 1988 (Cth), the Health Records and Information Privacy Act 2002 (NSW) and any other applicable legislation with similar objectives.\nProhibited AI System means any AI System which has been prohibited by the Principal or any other NSW or Commonwealth government body and includes any Protective Security Directions under the PSPF. A Prohibited AI System includes the applications listed from time to time in the Restricted Applications List issued by the Department of Customer Services which is available at DCS-2025-01 Cyber Security NSW Directive - Restricted Applications List.\nPublic Official means a person representing or employed by an Authority, government department or enterprise.\nRelated Body Corporate has the meaning given to that term in the Corporations Act 2001 (Cth).\nRequest for Tender means the relevant request for tender or proposal (however named) or other procurement process regarding the subject matter of and issued by the Principal prior to the parties entry into this Agreement, including any and all conditions, annexures, schedules, attachments, addenda, clarifications and other similar things.\nSecurity Standards means any security standards that the Supplier and its systems must be compliant with, as set out in the Statement of Work.\nService Levels means the relevant service levels set out in the Statement of Work.\nServices means the services set out or referred to in the Statement of Work, including the provision of any Deliverables, together with all related or incidental services, responsibilities and materials not specifically described in the Statement of Work but which are required to properly perform the Services and provide the Deliverables.\nSites means, if applicable, the Principal location(s) where the Goods and Services are to be provided.\nSpecifications means: \nany specifications, performance criteria, functionality or other requirements for the Goods or Services set out or referred to in the Statement of Work; and\nany specifications generally published or made publicly available by the Supplier which specifically relate to the Goods or Services,\nand in the event of any conflict or inconsistency between paragraphs\xa0(a) and (b) above, paragraph (a) prevails to the extent of such conflict or inconsistency.\nSpecified Personnel means the Supplier?s Representative and the Supplier?s other Personnel nominated as ?Specified Personnel? (or similar) in the Statement of Work.\nStatement of Work means the statement of work set out or referred to in Schedule 1.\nSupplier IP means any IP Rights owned or licensed by the Supplier prior to the Commencement Date (including any third party?s IP Rights that are licenced to the Supplier), or that are obtained by the Supplier during the Term separately from and other than in connection with this Agreement.\nSupplier?s Representative means the person specified in the Agreement Details as the Supplier?s representative.\nTax means any sales tax, value added tax, duty, withholding tax, levy, impost or other charge or duty levied by any government in Australia or elsewhere, which arises out of or in connection with the Supplier?s performance of its obligations under this Agreement, but excludes GST.\nTender Response means:\nthe Supplier?s tender, proposal or other response (however named) to the Request for Tender;\nany written response by or on behalf of the Supplier to a request from the Principal for clarification or further information given before the Commencement Date; and\nany written statement made by or on behalf of the Supplier to the Principal before the Commencement Date in relation to its proposed provision of the Goods and Services.\nTerm means the Initial Term and any Extension Term(s), unless this Agreement is terminated earlier in accordance with its terms, in which case the Term ends on the date of termination of this Agreement. \nTerms and Conditions means the terms and conditions set out in this section of the Agreement headed ?Terms and Conditions?.\nWHS Legislation means legislation relating to health and safety, including the Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (NSW) and the Work Health and Safety Regulation 2017 (NSW).\nWilful Misconduct means an act or omission of a party, deliberately performed or engaged in, which the relevant party knew (or ought to have known or predicted on due and reasonable consideration), would have a reasonable possibility of damaging, having a materially adverse effect on, or prejudicing, the other party.\nInterpretation\nIn this Agreement, unless expressed to the contrary:\nheadings are for convenience only and do not affect the interpretation of this Agreement;\nwords denoting the singular include the plural and vice versa;\nthe words ?such as?, ?including?, ?particularly? and similar expressions are not used as, nor are intended to be interpreted as, words of limitation;\nan obligation or liability assumed by, or a right conferred on, two or more persons binds or benefits them jointly and severally; \nwords that are gender neutral or gender specific include each gender;\na reference to:\na person includes a natural person, partnership, joint venture, government agency, association, corporation or other body corporate;\na thing (including a chose in action or other right) includes a part of that thing;\na party includes its successors and permitted assigns;\na document includes all amendments or supplements to that document;\nwriting includes writing in digital form;\na clause, term, party, schedule or attachment is a reference to a clause or term of, or party, schedule or attachment to the relevant part of this Agreement in which that reference is located;\na reference to a statute or other Law is a reference to that statute or other Law as amended, consolidated or replaced; \na monetary amount is to Australian dollars or such other currency specified in the Agreement; and\ntime is to the local time in Sydney, New South Wales; \na reference to any Authority, institute, association or body is:\nif that Authority, institute, association or body is reconstituted, renamed or replaced or if the powers or functions of that Authority, institute, association or body are transferred to another organisation, deemed to refer to the reconstituted, renamed or replaced organisation or the organisation to which the powers or functions are transferred, as the case may be; and\nif that Authority, institute, association or body ceases to exist, deemed to refer to the organisation which serves substantially the same purposes or object as that Authority, institute, association or body;\nno rule of construction applies to the disadvantage of a party because that party was responsible for the preparation of any part of this Agreement; and\nif there is any conflict or inconsistency between the provisions of this Agreement, the provisions listed earlier below prevail to the extent of such conflict or inconsistency, and the provisions or documents listed later below are to be read down or if necessary severed to the extent necessary to resolve the conflict or inconsistency:\nthe Agreement Details;\nthese Terms and Conditions;\nthe Statement of Work (i.e. Schedule 1);\nany documents attached to the Statement of Work or incorporated by reference;\nany other Schedules; and\nany other document attached to this Agreement or incorporated by reference.\nTerm\nInitial Term\nThis Agreement commences on the Commencement Date and continues for the Initial Term, unless terminated earlier in accordance with its terms.\nExtension Term(s)\nWhere an Extension Term has been specified in the Agreement Details, the Principal may, in its sole discretion, extend the Term for a period not exceeding the relevant Extension Term (up to, if any, the maximum number of extensions specified in the Agreement Details), by giving written notice to the Supplier at least 15 Business Days before the end of the then current Term. \nGoods and Services\nProvision of Goods and Services\nThe Supplier must provide the Goods and Services to the Principal in accordance with this Agreement, including the Statement of Work and the relevant Specifications for those Goods and Services. \nStandard of performance\nThe Supplier must provide the Goods and perform the Services (including in relation to the provision of any Deliverables) and perform its other obligations under this Agreement:\nusing a sufficient number of suitably trained, qualified, skilled and experienced Personnel who hold all necessary or prudent certifications, licences and consents;\nin accordance with Best Industry Practice, all applicable Laws, the Principal Policies and the Principal?s reasonable directions from time to time;\nin a manner which ensures the delivery of the Goods and Services by the relevant Due Date; \nat such places and between such hours as are set out in the relevant Statement of Work, or as otherwise reasonably requested by the Principal from time to time; and\nusing its own systems, which the Supplier must ensure are compatible with the Principal?s systems. \nNon-compliance\nAt any time in the 12 months after the Supplier provides the relevant Goods or performs the relevant Services (including the provision of any relevant Deliverables), the Principal may notify the Supplier if the relevant Goods or Services do not meet any of the requirements of clauses 3.1, \xa03.2 and clause 4.1. The Supplier must promptly (at the Principal?s option) reperform, rectify or replace the relevant Goods or Services so that they comply with the requirements of this Agreement.\nService standards and Service Levels\nThe Services must be provided by the Supplier so as to meet or exceed the required levels of performance set out in the Statement of Work, including any Service Levels. \nWhere the Statement of Work provides for service credits or price reductions (or similar) in the event that the Supplier fails to meet the required levels of performance, the Supplier must either (depending on which is earlier):\ncredit such amounts against the next invoice issued by the Supplier to the Principal; or\npay such amounts to the Principal within thirty (30) days of the end of the relevant period to which such service credits or price reductions (or similar) relate.\nThe parties agree that any service credits or price reductions (or similar) provided for by the Statement of Work are a genuine pre-estimate of the minimum damage which the Principal would suffer by reason of the Supplier?s failure to perform its obligations in accordance with the terms and conditions of this Agreement. The parties agree that the purpose of the service credits or price reductions (or similar) is to incentivise the Supplier and that they are not intended as a penalty for non-performance or to quantify the full extent of the Principal?s Losses in relation to any failure.\nThe Principal?s rights and remedies under this clause\xa03.4 are in addition to and not in replacement of any other rights or remedies available to the Principal under any other terms and conditions of this Agreement or at common law or in equity.\nDelays\nThe Supplier must:\nanticipate and identify potential failures to meet any Due Date or other timeframe required under this Agreement (Delay) (including, to the extent known or able to be reasonably anticipated, any Delays that may arise due to the Principal or its Personnel);\nimmediately notify the Principal upon becoming aware of any Delays; and\ntake all necessary steps within its reasonable control to avoid or mitigate any Delays.\nManagement of this Agreement \nThe Supplier must ensure that the Supplier?s Representative:\nliaises with and reports to the Principal?s Representative; \nconfers with the Principal?s Representative to discuss the performance of this Agreement, and any actions needed to remedy any non-compliance; and\nattends meetings and briefings with the Personnel of the Principal,\nfrom time to time, including as reasonably required by the Principal.\nThe Supplier must provide to the Principal the reports specified in the Statement of Work, in the timeframe and format specified in the Statement of Work, and such reports as otherwise requested by the Principal from time to time. \nModern Slavery\nThe Supplier must at all times comply with the modern slavery requirements in Schedule 3 (Modern Slavery) in connection with this Agreement.\nThe Principal may require the Supplier to undertake a modern slavery self-assessment using the form of self-assessment required by the Principal from time to time, as required by the Principal. The Supplier must comply with any further modern slavery requirements required by the Principal as a result of any such assessment.\nPublic Interest Disclosures and GIPA\nIf the Supplier is engaged to provide services on behalf of the Principal under this Agreement, including an engagement to exercise a function of the Principal, the Supplier must comply with the requirements in Schedule 4 (Public Interest and GIPA Disclosures) in connection with this Agreement. \nUse of AI Systems\nExcept as specified in the Agreement Details, the Supplier must not, in connection with the performance of the Goods and Services:\nuse; or\ninput or upload Principal Data or information about Principal Data into,\nany AI System (including any Machine Learning Tool).\nThe Supplier must not, in connection with the performance of the Goods and Services, use any Prohibited AI Systems.\nIf the Supplier does use any AI System in accordance with clause 3.9.1, the Supplier:\nunless specified in the Agreement Details, must ensure the AI System does not collect and retain (in any form and for any period) any Principal Data; \nmust ensure the AI System is not open or otherwise accessible or able to be used by anyone other than Principal and the Supplier?s Personnel that are directly involved in the performance of the Goods and Services;\nmust check and verify the outputs generated by the AI System;\ncorrect any errors, omissions, inaccuracies, defects or other shortcomings in Deliverables generated by or delivered using any AI System before they are delivered to the Principal;\nwill be liable for any errors, omissions, inaccuracies or other shortcomings in any results or outputs generated using that AI System;\nfor abundant clarity, is not excused from compliance with clauses 11 12, 13, and 14 of this Agreement; and\nmust comply with any requirements outlined in the Agreement Details with respect to the AI System.\nWarranties\nWarranties\nThe Supplier represents, warrants and undertakes to the Principal that:\nall information which it provides to the Principal, whether prior to, on or after the Commencement Date, is true and correct in every respect and is not misleading or deceptive; \nit has and will maintain all approvals, licences, consents, and permissions, including from any Authority, necessary for the performance of its obligations under this Agreement; \non and from the date that the Goods and Services are delivered to the Principal in accordance with the terms of this Agreement,  the Goods and Services meet the Specifications, are of merchantable quality, are fit for the purpose for which they are provided and free from defects in materials and workmanship; \non and from the date that title to the Goods passes to the Principal, the Goods will be unused and free from any charge or encumbrance; and\nthe provision of the Goods and Services, and their use as contemplated by this Agreement, will not infringe the rights (including IP Rights and Moral Rights) of any third party.\nManufacturer warranties\nThe Supplier must take all reasonable steps (including as may be requested in writing by the Principal from time to time) to ensure that the Principal obtains the full benefit of any additional warranties that may be provided by the manufacturer or supplier of any Goods or Services provided by the Supplier under this Agreement. The Supplier will not do, or omit to do, anything that will or might jeopardise or affect the Principal?s entitlements under any such warranties.\nPersonal Property Securities Act\nTo the extent the Personal Property Securities Act 2009 (Cth) applies to any Goods or Services supplied by the Supplier to the Principal, the Supplier represents, warrants and undertakes that the supply of the Goods or Services to the Principal:\ndoes not breach any security agreement the Supplier has with a third party; and\nis within the ordinary course of the Supplier?s business. \nPersonnel\nPersonnel generally\nThe Supplier must:\nensure that all of its Personnel comply with the Supplier?s relevant obligations under this Agreement;\nensure that the Specified Personnel are involved in providing the relevant Services at all times or as otherwise required by this Agreement;\nnot remove or redeploy the Specified Personnel unless:\nthe Specified Personnel ceases to be one of the Supplier?s Personnel; or\nthe Supplier obtains the Principal?s prior written consent to such redeployment.\nRemoval of Personnel\nIf any of the Supplier?s Personnel are: \nunable or not suitable to undertake the work assigned to them (in the reasonable opinion of the Principal); or\notherwise removed or redeployed by the Supplier (subject to clause\xa05.1.3), \nthen the Supplier must promptly provide replacement Personnel that are acceptable to the Principal. If requested by the Principal, the Supplier must provide the resumes of such Personnel to the Principal, and allow the Principal to interview such Personnel.\nAccess to Sites\nIf any of the Supplier?s Personnel access any Sites to perform this Agreement, the Supplier must:\nensure that all such Personnel comply with the reasonable requirements and directions of the Principal and any Principal Policies;\nminimise disruption and inconvenience to the Principal or other people using the Sites and otherwise not do anything that is, or may be, dangerous, annoying, or offensive, or that may interfere with the Principal or other people using the Sites; \ntake full responsibility for disposal of all waste and pay all fees, charges and levies associated with the assessment, classification, transport, tracking and disposal of waste in connection with this Agreement;\non completion of the Supplier's Activities, remove all rubbish, materials and equipment of the Supplier from each Site; and\nnot pollute, contaminate or otherwise damage the environment and keep the Site clean, tidy and free of refuse;\nimmediately notify the Principal in writing upon becoming aware of any damage to property caused by the Principal, its Personnel or any other person; and\nonly access or use the Sites at the times specified by the Principal (or if no such times are specified, only during Business Hours and on reasonable prior written notice to the Principal) and for the purpose of carrying out obligations under this Agreement.\nWork, health and safety\nWithout limiting the Supplier's obligations under any other provision of this Agreement, the Supplier must:\ncomply with the WHS Legislation (including any obligation under the WHS Legislation to consult, co-operate and coordinate activities with all other persons who have a work health and safety duty in relation to the same matter);\nif requested by the Principal?s Representative or required by the WHS Legislation, demonstrate compliance with the WHS Legislation, including providing evidence of any approvals, prescribed qualifications or experience, or any other information relevant to work health and safety matters;\nnotify the Principal?s Representative promptly (and in any event within 12 hours of such matter arising) of all work health, safety and rehabilitation matters arising out of, or in any way in connection with, the provision of the Goods and Services or the performance of any of the Supplier?s other obligations under this Agreement;\ninsofar as the Supplier, in providing the Goods and Services or otherwise performing its obligations under this Agreement, is under any duty imposed by the WHS Legislation, do everything necessary to comply with any such duty; \nensure that it does not do anything or fail to do anything that would cause the Principal to be in breach of the WHS Legislation; and\ncomply with any additional work health and safety requirements specified in the Statement of Work or as otherwise reasonably required by the Principal from time to time.\nSubcontracting\nThe Supplier must not sub-contract a material part of its obligations under this Agreement without the prior written approval of the Principal. The Principal may give or withhold its approval in its absolute discretion and may impose conditions on its approval.\nThe Supplier is responsible for all acts and omissions of its Personnel (including subcontractors) as if they were those of the Supplier and the Supplier indemnifies the Principal against all costs, expenses and liabilities incurred by the Customer in connection with the acts or omissions of any Personnel.\nRequest for Tender and Tender Response\nTender process\nIf directed by the Principal, the Supplier must comply with the Request for Tender and the Tender Response to the extent that any matter or thing addressed in the Request for Tender or Tender Response is not provided for in this Agreement. \nWhere the Request for Tender or the Tender Response is capable of assisting in ascertaining the meaning of a particular provision of this Agreement, the Principal may rely on the Request for Tender and/or the Tender Response to:\nconfirm that the meaning of the provision is the ordinary meaning conveyed by the text of the provision taking into account its context in this Agreement and the purpose or object underlying this Agreement; or\ndetermine the meaning of a provision of this Agreement when the provision is ambiguous or obscure.\nConflicts\nTo the extent that there is any conflict or inconsistency between this Agreement and the Request for Tender or the Tender Response, the conflict or inconsistency will be resolved by giving priority to this Agreement.\nTo the extent that there is any conflict or inconsistency between the Request for Tender and Tender Response, the conflict or inconsistency will be resolved by giving priority to the Request for Tender, except to the extent that the Tender Response expressly provides otherwise in relation to particular section of the Request for Tender (for clarity, any wording in the Tender Response which provides that the entire Tender Response or a substantial part of the Tender Response prevails over the Request for Tender will not apply to this Agreement).\nInsurance\nInsurance\nThe Supplier must have and maintain the insurances set out in the Agreement Details.\nRequirements\nThe insurance referred to in clause\xa07.1 must be with an insurance office or underwriter that is well established, reputable and financially solvent. Without limiting the previous sentence, such insurances must be entered into with an insurer that is authorised and licensed to operate in Australia, and which has a security rating of A- or better by AM Best (or an equivalent rating organisation) at the date when cover is commenced.\nProof of compliance\nThe Supplier must, on request by the Principal, promptly provide certificates of currency certifying that it has insurance as required by this clause\xa07. If the Principal determines that the Supplier is not in compliance with this clause\xa07, the Principal may obtain appropriate insurance policies for and on behalf of the Supplier and pass the cost of such policies on to the Supplier.\nPrice and payment\nPayment\nIn consideration for the Supplier providing the Goods and Services in accordance with this Agreement, the Principal will pay each Correctly Rendered Invoice within 30 days of receipt. The making of a payment is not an acknowledgment that the Goods or Services have been provided or accepted in accordance with this Agreement.\nInvoices\nThe Supplier may issue Correctly Rendered Invoices to the Principal in accordance with (and at the times set out in) the Statement of Work (including in any payment plan). For clarity, if the Statement of Work:\nsets out that the relevant Fees are subject to the completion of any Goods or Services (or any other milestone), then the Supplier may only invoice the Principal for such Fees once the relevant Goods, Services, or milestone is completed in accordance with the requirements of this Agreement; or\ndoes not state when the Supplier may invoice the Principal for the relevant Fees, then the Supplier may invoice the Principal monthly in arrears on the last Business Day of each month.\nExpenses\nUnless otherwise agreed in writing by the Principal, the Supplier must not charge the Principal for any fees, charges or expenses in addition to the Fees (including any costs in relation to travel and accommodation, couriers, telecommunications or printing).\nDisputed invoices\nIf the Principal disputes any invoiced amount, it may withhold the relevant amount until the parties resolve the dispute, provided that the Principal must pay any amounts that are not disputed (subject to the Supplier reissuing a Correctly Rendered Invoice for the undisputed amounts).\nSet-off by the Principal\nAny debt due from the Supplier to the Principal or any other claim to money which the Principal may have against the Supplier under this or any other contract between the parties may be set-off in full against the Fees owing under this Agreement. The Supplier may not make a set-off against the Principal for any reason.\nTaxes\nSubject to clause\xa08.6.2, the Fees are inclusive of, and the Supplier is responsible for paying, all Taxes levied or imposed in connection with the Goods and Services and the performance of the Supplier?s obligations under this Agreement. \nUnless expressed otherwise, all consideration payable under this Agreement in relation to any supply is exclusive of GST. \nIf GST is payable in respect of any supply made by a supplier under this Agreement, then (subject to receipt of a tax invoice) the recipient will pay to the supplier an amount equal to the GST payable on the supply at the same time and in the same manner as the consideration for the supply is to be provided under this Agreement.\nWhere the Principal is required by any applicable Law to withhold any amounts from the payments made by it to the Supplier under this Agreement, the Principal:\nmay withhold such amounts and will not be required to gross-up its payments to the Supplier for any amounts withheld; however\nwill provide the Supplier with a certificate of withholding or such other reasonable evidence of such withholding, to facilitate the Supplier?s claims or deductions with the relevant taxing authority.\nWords and expressions that are not defined in this Agreement but which have a defined meaning in the GST Law have the same meaning as in the GST Law. \nIntellectual Property\nPrincipal IP\nAs between the parties, the Principal is the owner of the Principal IP and all right, title and interest (including IP Rights) in the Principal IP. \nSubject to the Supplier complying with its obligations under this Agreement, the Principal grants to the Supplier a non-exclusive, revocable, non-transferable, royalty free licence during the Term to use, reproduce, modify and adapt the Principal IP for the sole purpose of, and only to the extent necessary to provide or perform (as applicable), the Goods and Services.\nNew IP\nSubject to clause\xa09.3.1 and unless otherwise specified in the Statement of Work, the Principal owns the New IP. The Supplier hereby absolutely assigns and transfers (and will procure that its Personnel assign and transfer) to the Principal (with full title guarantee) all existing and future IP Rights throughout the world in the New IP, including all statutory and common law rights attaching thereto.\nIf and to the extent that the Statement of Work specifies that the Supplier owns any New IP, clause\xa09.2.1 does not apply to such New IP, and such New IP is licensed by the Supplier to the Principal on the same terms as Supplier IP.\nSupplier IP\nAs between the parties, the Supplier is the owner of the Supplier IP and all right, title and interest (including IP Rights) in the Supplier IP. \nThe Supplier grants (and must ensure that any relevant third parties grant) the Principal a worldwide, perpetual, irrevocable, non-exclusive, royalty-free, transferable licence to use, reproduce, sublicense, modify and adapt any Supplier IP made available to the Principal under or in connection with this Agreement (including any Supplier IP that is incorporated into the Goods or Services, or that the Principal otherwise requires a licence to in order to enjoy the benefit of this Agreement):\nto the extent necessary to obtain the full benefit of and make full use of the Goods and Services; and\notherwise for the Principal?s operations or the exercise of its functions.\nMoral Rights\nTo the extent permitted by applicable Law, the Supplier must procure that any individual who contributed to the Supplier IP or New IP unconditionally and irrevocably:\nconsents to any act or omission of the Principal or its Personnel that would otherwise infringe their Moral Rights in relation to the Supplier IP or New IP, whether occurring before or after their consent is given; and \nwhere applicable, waives all Moral Rights in relation to the Supplier IP or New IP.\nWarranty \nThe Supplier warrants that:\nit owns or has the necessary IP Rights to grant the rights outlined in this clause 9; and\nthe Principal will not infringe the IP Rights of any third party in using the Deliverables.\nThird Party IP Rights\nIf the Deliverables incorporate IP Rights of any third party, the Supplier must procure for the Principal a licence on terms no less favourable than the licence granted by the Supplier in relation to Supplier IP or such other licence rights agreed in writing by the parties.\nIndigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property (ICIP)\nThis clause 9.7.1 applies if the Agreement Details specify that ICIP will be collected or used in the delivery or performance of the Services and/or incorporated as part of the Goods or Deliverables, then the Supplier must at all times comply with the ICIP Schedule in connection with this Agreement. \nExcept as otherwise set out in the ICIP Schedule (if applicable):\nThe parties agree that the ownership of ICIP remains with the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities, custodians or traditional owners of the ICIP; and\nthe Supplier is required to ensure that it has the authority or the permission from the relevant Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities, custodians or traditional owners to use the ICIP and will ensure that the Principal has the right to use such ICIP to the extent that it is incorporated in the Deliverables or otherwise permitted under this Agreement.\nDelivery, Title and Risk\nDelivery\nThe Supplier must, unless otherwise directed by the Principal;\nload and transport the Goods for delivery in accordance with the Statement of Work and applicable industry standards;\nensure that all Goods are packaged in accordance with the Statement of Work and applicable industry standards;\ndeliver the Goods and Services and Documentation at the time, date and place specified in the Statement of Work and, if no time and date is specified, deliver the Goods as soon as practicable; and\ncomply with an other requirements specified by the Principal in this Agreement or otherwise.\nTitle\nUnless otherwise specified in the Statement of Work, title to each Good (excluding IP Rights which are addressed in clause\xa09) passes to the Principal upon the earlier of:\ndelivery to the Principal in accordance with this Agreement; or\npayment for the relevant Good. \nThe Supplier represents, warrants and undertakes to the Principal that, at the time title to a Good passes to the Principal pursuant to clause\xa010.2.1, the Good is free from any lien, security interest, mortgage or other encumbrance. \nRisk\nUntil risk passes to the Principal in accordance with clause\xa010.3.2, the Supplier bears the risk of, and must indemnify the Principal against, any Loss or damage to the Goods (including any unfixed goods and materials intended for incorporation into the Goods but not yet incorporated).\nUnless otherwise specified in the Statement of Work, risk in each Good will pass to the Principal upon delivery to the Principal in accordance with the Agreement.\nIf any Good is collected or returned (for example, for repair), risk in that Good will revert to the Supplier when the Good is collected by, or on behalf of, the Supplier or when the Good is delivered to a carrier for return to the Supplier.\nConfidentiality\nConfidential Information\nWhere either party (Recipient) receives or otherwise possesses Confidential Information of the other party (Discloser), the Recipient must: \nkeep such information confidential;\nin the case of the Supplier or its Personnel, only use it where required to perform its obligations under this Agreement; and\nnot disclose it to anyone other than:\nwith the prior consent of the Discloser and on the condition that the subsequent recipient is bound by the same or substantively equivalent confidentiality requirements as specified in this Agreement; \nwhere required by the GIPA Act (or any other similar Laws) which may require the Principal to publish or disclose certain information concerning this Agreement;\nwhere required by any other Laws, provided that the Recipient gives the Discloser reasonable notice of any such legal requirement or order to enable the Discloser to seek a protective order or other appropriate remedy (unless it would be in violation of a court order or other legal requirement); \nin the case of the Principal, to any Government Agency or responsible Minister for a Government Agency; or\nto its Personnel and directors, officers, lawyers, accountants, insurers, financiers and other professional advisers where the disclosure is in connection with advising on, reporting on, or facilitating the party?s exercise of its rights or performance of its obligations under this Agreement on the condition that the subsequent recipient is bound by the same or substantively equivalent confidentiality requirements as specified in this Agreement. \nPublicity\nThe Supplier must not issue any press release or make any other public statement regarding this Agreement or the Goods and Services without the prior written consent of the Principal, except as required by Law. \nPermitted disclosures\nThis clause 11 does not preclude the Principal from disclosing any information (including Confidential Information) of the Supplier to the extent that this Agreement otherwise permits the disclosure of such information. \nPrivacy\nPersonal Information\nIf the Supplier or its Personnel obtains access to, or collects, uses, holds, controls, manages or otherwise processes, any Personal Information in connection with this Agreement (regardless of whether or not that Personal Information forms part of the Principal Data), the Supplier must:\ncomply with all Privacy Laws, as though it were a person subject to those Privacy Laws; \nonly use that Personal Information for the sole purpose of providing the Goods and Services and otherwise performing its obligations under this Agreement;\nnot disclose the Personal Information to any other person without the Principal?s prior written consent, which may be given in respect of classes or categories of subcontractors or types of subcontracted activities and made subject to any applicable conditions; \nnot transfer the Personal Information outside New South Wales, Australia or access it, or allow it to be accessed, from outside New South Wales, Australia without the Principal?s prior written consent (which may be withheld in the Principal?s sole discretion);\nprotect the Personal Information from loss, theft, damage, or unauthorised access, use, disclosure, modification and other misuse and in accordance with the security requirements under this Agreement; and\nnotify the Principal as soon as reasonably possible if the Supplier is approached by any Commissioner or other Authority concerning any Personal Information.\nDisclosure\nWhere the Supplier is required by Law to produce or disclose any information or to develop or provide any response or explanation to an Authority in relation to any incident (including any privacy breach) concerning the handling, management, safekeeping or protection of any Personal Information in connection with this Agreement, it must (to the extent such action is not prohibited by Law), provide written notice to the Principal as soon as reasonably possible (and in any case within 24 hours of becoming aware of the requirement) of the nature and content of the information to be produced or disclosed and, prior to providing a response to the Authority or disclosing any Personal Information, engage in reasonable and good faith consultation with the Principal regarding its proposed response or explanation.\nData Breaches involving Personal Information\nUnless required by Law, the Supplier or its Personnel must not notify or contact any affected individuals in relation to any actual or suspected Data Breaches involving Personal Information without the prior written consent of the Principal.\nPrincipal Data and Security\nGenerally\nThe Supplier must implement, maintain and enforce safety and security procedures and safeguards against the corruption, misuse, interference, destruction, loss or unauthorised access, use, modification, or disclosure of the Principal Data in the Supplier?s possession or control, including in accordance with:\nthe Principal Policies;\nBest Industry Practice;\nall applicable Laws; and\nany other requirements set out or referred to in this Agreement.\nSecurity Standards and requirements\nThe Supplier must operate an effective system of security management that conforms to the latest issue of the Security Standards, as certified by an independent accredited third party, and provide a copy of its compliance certificate(s) for the Security Standards to the Principal on request.\nThe Supplier must comply with any other secrecy and security requirements of the Principal notified by the Principal to the Supplier from time to time.\nPrincipal Data\nPrincipal Data is and remains the property of the Principal at all times, including after completion of the Agreement. Except as required by Law, the Supplier must:\nnot use Principal Data for any purpose other than as directly necessary to perform its obligations under this Agreement;\nnot sell, commercially exploit, let for hire, assign rights in or otherwise dispose of any Principal Data;\nnot make any Principal Data available to any third party without the Principal?s prior written consent;\nnot transfer, store, host process, access or view Principal Data outside of New South Wales, unless otherwise approved in writing by the Principal;\ncomply with all applicable Laws and any procedures specified by the Principal concerning the Principal Data; and\nprohibit and prevent any person who does not have the appropriate level of security clearance from gaining access to Principal Data.\nData Breaches\nNotification of Data Breach\nIf the Supplier becomes aware of an actual or potential:\nunauthorised (whether under this Agreement or otherwise) or unlawful use of, loss of, theft of, damage to, access to, alteration of, or disclosure of Principal Data within the Supplier's or its Personnel's possession or control;\nbreach of clause\xa011 (Confidentiality), clause\xa012 (Privacy) or clause\xa013 (Principal Data and Security);\nnotifiable data breach under the Privacy Laws; or\nany other breach of security in relation to the Goods or Services or the IT systems of either the Supplier or the Principal, \n(in each case a Data Breach), the Supplier must immediately (but in any event within 24 hours) notify the Principal in writing of the Data Breach.\nSupplier?s obligations \nIf a Data Breach occurs, the Supplier must immediately:\ncomply with the Principal?s directions regarding the Data Breach (including by providing the Principal any information it requests regarding the Data Breach);\nallow the Principal to participate in the Supplier?s assessment of the Data Breach;\ntake all necessary steps to ensure that any potential Data Breach does not become an actual Data Breach;\nmake all reasonable attempts to remedy any Data Breach, mitigate any harm caused by the Data Breach and minimise its consequences;\nprovide the Principal with a written report detailing the cause of, and procedure for correcting, the Data Breach; and\ntake all reasonable steps to prevent any reoccurrence of such Data Breach, and provide the Principal with reasonable evidence that it will not reoccur. \nData breach involving Personal Information\nIf a Data Breach involves (or the Supplier has reasonable grounds to suspect that a Data Breach may involve) Personal Information, the Supplier must:\ncomply with clause\xa014.2; \nunless otherwise directed by the Principal, comply with the Principal?s published data breach policy and any data breach procedures and documentation specified in this Agreement, as well as any other Principal Policies relevant to the management, mitigation and response to a Data Breach; and\ncomply with any reasonable direction (including as to timeframes) from the Principal with respect to the Data Breach involving Personal Information (which may include, for example, activities to support the Principal?s response to the incident and compliance with the New South Wales mandatory notification of data breach scheme). \nNotifiable data breach\nIf either party determines that a Data Breach is or may be a notifiable data breach under the Privacy Laws, the Supplier must, if requested by the Principal, prepare a draft notice to the Commissioner within 48 hours for the Principal to review. The Principal may:\nrequire the Supplier to make any amendments to the draft notice; or\nelect to notify the Commissioner of the notifiable data breach itself.\nCommissioner involvement\nNotwithstanding clause 14.4, and subject to applicable Laws, the Principal reserves the right to notify and liaise with the Commissioner with respect to any Data Breach and the Supplier must not notify the Commissioner or provide the Commissioner with any information without the prior written approval of the Principal.\nConflict of interest and Corruption Prevention\nConflict of Interest\nThe Supplier represents, warrants and undertakes to the Principal that, to the best of its knowledge and belief after making due and reasonable enquiries, there is no Conflict of Interest in respect of itself or its Personnel which relates to the Supplier?s ability to perform its obligations under this Agreement. \nNotification of Conflict of Interest\nThe Supplier must immediately provide the Principal written notice upon becoming aware of the existence, or possibility, of an actual or perceived Conflict of Interest, in which case:\nthe Supplier must take all necessary action as may be reasonably required by the Principal to avoid or minimise any such Conflict of Interest; and\nthe Principal may either:\napprove (with conditions) the Supplier continuing to perform its obligations under this Agreement; or\nterminate this Agreement by notice in writing to the Supplier where in the Principal?s view the actual or perceived Conflict of Interest cannot be appropriately managed.\nCorruption Prevention\nThe Supplier must comply with all Applicable Corruption Laws and not offer, promise or give (directly or indirectly) any financial or other Advantage to any:\nperson with the intention of inducing them to breach a position of trust or duty of good faith or impartiality; or\nPublic Official intending to influence that official.\nThe Supplier warrants to the Principal and repeats the warranty on each day of the Term that:\nit has not been found by ICAC to have engaged in Corrupt Conduct; and\nin performing its obligations under this Agreement it will not employ or continue to employ any person found by ICAC to have engaged in Corrupt Conduct\nThe Supplier must ensure that:\ndoes not employ, or continue to employ, any person or otherwise engage any Personnel; and\nno subcontractor employs, or continues to employ, any person,\nin relation to the Goods and Services who has been found to have engaged in, or in the case of a company, any officer of which has been found to have engaged in, Corrupt Conduct by ICAC\nThe Principal may direct the Supplier to terminate a subcontract if any officer of the relevant subcontractor has been found to have engaged in Corrupt Conduct by ICAC and the Supplier must immediately comply with that direction.\nTermination\nTermination by the Principal for convenience\nThe Principal may immediately terminate this Agreement (or reduce the scope of the Goods or Services) for convenience at any time by giving written notice to the Supplier. \nTermination by the Principal for cause\nThe Principal may terminate this Agreement (or reduce the scope of the Goods or Services) immediately by written notice to the Supplier if:\nthe Supplier breaches any provision of this Agreement where that breach is not capable of remedy;\nthe Supplier breaches this Agreement and fails to remedy the breach within 20 Business Days of receiving notice from the Principal requiring it do so;\nthe Supplier fails to meet any Service Levels on 3 or more occasions in any 3 month period; \nthere is a finding (whether by an Authority, the Principal or any other person) of, or the Principal reasonably forms the view that there has been, serious wrongdoing or other misconduct involving the Supplier or any of its Personnel, which misconduct may include corrupt or unlawful behaviour by the Supplier or any other conduct that could reasonably be considered to adversely affect the reputation of the Principal or the State of New South Wales; \nthe Supplier becomes (or threatens to become) insolvent; or\nthe Supplier undergoes a Change in Control.\nTermination by the Supplier\nThe Supplier may terminate this Agreement immediately by written notice to the Principal if the Principal materially breaches this Agreement and fails to remedy the breach within 40 Business Days of a written notice from the Supplier requiring it do so.\nEffect of termination\nTermination of this Agreement for any reason is without prejudice to any rights which either party may have accrued before termination.\nAdjustment of the Fees on termination\nWhere this Agreement is terminated or the scope of the Goods and Services is reduced under clause\xa016.1, the Principal must pay the Supplier for Goods and Services provided up until the date of termination plus the direct costs reasonably and necessarily incurred by the Supplier as a result of such termination or reduction in scope (as applicable). The Supplier must provide evidence of any such costs under this clause to the Principal?s satisfaction prior to the payment of those costs by the Principal.\nWhere this Agreement is terminated or the scope of the Goods and Services is reduced under clause\xa016.2, the Principal must pay the Supplier for the Goods properly delivered and Services fully and properly performed (including the full and proper delivery of any Deliverables) by the Supplier as at the date of termination or reduction in scope (as applicable) after first deducting the Principal?s assessment (acting reasonably) of amounts  likely to be payable by the Supplier to the Principal and any other Loss suffered (or likely to be suffered) by the Principal due to the termination of the Agreement or reduction in scope or breach by the Supplier. \nWhere this Agreement is terminated under clause\xa016.3, the Principal will pay the Supplier for Goods and Services provided up until the date of termination.\nOther than as set out in clauses\xa016.5.1 to 16.5.3, the Supplier will have no Claim against the Principal for any Losses arising from the termination of this Agreement (or reduction in scope of the Goods and Services under clauses\xa016.1, 16.2 or 16.3. \nDispute resolution\nFor clarity, the processes described in clause\xa023 are independent of, may be undertaken contemporaneously with, and do not constrain or delay, a party exercising its rights under this clause\xa016. \nReturn and assistance\nUpon the expiry or termination of this Agreement the Supplier must:\nimmediately stop using Principal Data, Principal Personal Information and Principal Confidential Information; \nreturn and/or destroy (as directed by the Principal) any Principal IP, Principal Data, Principal Personal Information or Principal Confidential Information in its possession or control, and provide to the Principal all Goods and Deliverables (including any draft or incomplete Deliverables);\nImmediately deliver up all Deliverables completed or partially completed as at the date of termination or expiry (unless otherwise directed by the Principal) and\nprovide all assistance reasonably requested by the Principal in connection with transitioning of the provision of the Services to another supplier (which may include preparing and performing a disengagement plan approved by the Principal).\nSuspension\nPrincipal?s direction\nThe Principal may direct the Supplier in writing to:\nsuspend the performance or carrying out of; and/or\nafter a suspension has been instructed, re-commence the performance or carrying out of,\nall or part of the Goods and Services or the performance of the Supplier?s obligations under this Agreement, at any time. Any such suspension will be effective on and from the date specified in the Principal?s direction.\nSupplier?s compliance with direction\nThe Supplier must comply with a direction issued by the Principal under clause\xa017.1. \nSuspension for Supplier breach\nIf a suspension under this clause\xa017 is instructed by the Principal as a result of any breach by the Supplier, the Supplier's failure or delay in carrying out any of its obligations in accordance with this Agreement or because of any event of the kind described in clause\xa016.2, such suspension will be without any liability to the Principal and the Supplier will not be entitled to make any Claim against the Principal arising out of, or in connection with, the suspension.\nSuspension for other reasons\nIf a suspension is instructed by the Principal under clause\xa017.1 other than for the reasons described in clause\xa017.3, then:\nunless otherwise agreed by the parties, the Supplier will be entitled to invoice the Principal the direct, reasonable and substantiated costs (excluding any profit, profit component or overheads) necessarily incurred by the Supplier as a result of implementing the suspension as directed by the Principal, to the extent such costs could not have been reasonably mitigated or avoided;\nthe Supplier must take all reasonable steps to mitigate those costs incurred by it as a result of such suspension; and\nthe Supplier will not be entitled to make any Claim against the Principal arising out of or in connection with the suspension other than as described in clause\xa017.4.1.\nIndemnity\nSubject to clause\xa019.7, the Supplier must indemnify the Principal and its Personnel from and against any and all Losses they sustain, suffer or incur as a result of or in connection with: \nany breach by the Supplier or its Personnel of clauses\xa011 (Confidentiality), 12 (Privacy) or 13 (Principal Data and Security); \nany of the Supplier?s or its Personnel?s fraud, recklessness or Wilful Misconduct;\nany breach by the Supplier or its Personnel of any applicable Laws;\nany claim alleging that the Goods, Services, Deliverables or any other materials provided by or on behalf of the Supplier or its Personnel infringe the rights (including IP Rights) of any third party; \nany personal injury, illness or death caused by the Supplier or its Personnel; and\nany loss of or damage to property caused by the Supplier or its Personnel. \nLimit
