2026-09-29T04:31:07.277336Z [] {'source_reference_id': '853', 'source_id': 'buynsw', 'source_url': 'https://buy.nsw.gov.au/prcOpportunity/46709C00-67E9-4591-AE04602ACE4CEB51', 'title': 'The Provision of After-Hours Security Officer Services at NESA?s Clarence Street Office', 'issuing_agency': 'NSW Education Standards Authority (NESA)', 'category': 'Request for Quotation', 'status': None, 'publish_date': None, 'closing_date': '2026-10-23', 'value_amount': None, 'value_currency': None, 'value_notes': None, 'location': 'Sydney, NSW', 'description': 'The NSW Education Standards Authority (NESA) is seeking after-hours security officer services for its Clarence Street office in Sydney (Levels 3-10, including Level 8 Suite 1 and Level 10 Suite 3). Services are required continuously from 5:00 PM weekdays to 8:30 AM the next business day, and 24-hours on weekends and NSW public holidays. The scope includes providing licensed and trained security officers for monitoring premises, access points, and alarms; conducting regular patrols; responding to incidents; maintaining records; and performing lock-up procedures. One security officer is required per shift, with provisions for additional personnel as needed.\n\nThis Request for Quotation (RFQ) is open exclusively to suppliers currently approved under the NSW Whole of Government Integrated Security Services Contract 2007486. All security officers must hold a current NSW Security Master License, NSW Class 1A Security Officer license, and first aid certification, along with required Working with Children Checks and National Police Checks. Suppliers are responsible for providing uniforms and most equipment, while NESA supplies an on-site mobile phone for alarm notifications. On-site visits are not required during the RFQ process.\n\nThe contract term is from 1 November 2026 to 30 June 2028, with an option for a one-year extension. Quoted rates must be fixed for the first 12 months, in Australian dollars, and inclusive of GST, adhering to applicable maximum rates from the overarching contract. The tender (Tender 853) closes on 23 October 2026 at 3:00 PM. Quotations will remain valid for 12 months. Clarification requests must be submitted at least 3 business days prior to the closing date.', 'summary_headline': 'NESA seeks after-hours security officer services for its Clarence Street office.', 'contact_name': 'Michael Buraga', 'contact_email': 'Procurement@nesa.nsw.edu.au', 'contact_phone': '(02) 9367 8139', 'lodgment_address': 'electronically through the NSW Government Supplier Hub: https://buy.nsw.gov.au/suppliers', 'documents': [{'file_name': 'Tender-853-The-Provision-of-After-Hours-Security-Officer-Services-at-NESA?s-Clarence-Street-Office-RFQ-.pdf', 'file_type': 'pdf', 'storage_uri': 'gs://tenderai-dev-documents/tenders/buynsw/Tender 853/9e01fb4c85f782a5-Tender-853-The-Provision-of-After-Hours-Security-Officer-Services-at-NESA?s-Clarence-Street-Office-RFQ-.pdf', 'extracted_text': 'Page 1 of 18 \n \n \n \n \n \nREQUEST FOR QUOTATION \nUNDER \nNSW WHOLE OF GOVERNMENT INTEGRATED SECURITY \nSERVICES CONTRACT 2007486 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nRFQ CONDITIONS \nAND SCOPE \nOF WORK \nVersion 1 \n \n  The Provision of After-Hours Security Officer Services at      \n  NESA?s Clarence Street Office  \n  Tender 853 \n  Closing date: 23/10/2026 and Closing time: 3pm \n\nPage 2 of 18 \n \n  RFQ Contact Officer \nRespondents should refer requests for information or advice regarding this \nRFQ to: CONTACT OFFICER                          Michael Buraga \nCONTACT PHONE NUMBER                           (02) 9367 8139 \n \n     CONTACT EMAIL ADDRESS \n                   Procurement@nesa.nsw.edu.au \n \nCONTACT POSTAL ADDRESS                          Level 4, 117 Clarence Street, Sydney, NSW 2000 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n? Crown in right of the State of NSW through the Department Primary Industries and Regional Development 2023. \n \nThis publication is copyright and may incorporate material to which an individual maintains moral rights. Other than for the purposes \nof and subject to the conditions prescribed under the Copyright Act 1968, no part of it may, in any form or by any means, be \nreproduced, altered, manipulated, stored in a retrieval system or transmitted without prior written consent of the copyright owner or \nowner of moral rights. Any enquiries relating to consents and use of this publication, including by NSW Government agencies, must be \naddressed to NSW Public Works. \n \nWhile this document has been formulated with all due care, the State of New South Wales does not warrant or represent that the \ndocument is free from errors or omissions, or that it is exhaustive. The State of NSW disclaims, to the extent permitted by law, all \nwarranties, representations or endorsements, express or implied, with regard to this document including but not limited to, all implied \nwarranties of merchantability, fitness for a particular purpose, or non-infringement. The State of NSW further does not warrant or \naccept any liability in relation to the quality or accuracy of this document and no responsibility is accepted by the State of NSW for \nthe accuracy, currency, reliability and correctness of any information in this document provided by the client or third parties. \n \nAll references to NSW Public Works are taken to be references to the Department Primary Industries and Regional Development and \non behalf of the State of New South Wales. \n \n \n\nPage 3 of 18 \n \nContents \n \nRFQ OVERVIEW........................................................................................................................ 4 \n1. \nIntroduction ............................................................................................................................ 4 \n1.1. \nBackground/Objectives ......................................................................................................................... 4 \n1.2. \nThe Customer is responsible for the delivery of this RFQ process. ..................................................... 4 \n1.3. \nCustomer Agreement and Duration ..................................................................................................... 5 \nRFQ CONDITIONS ..................................................................................................................... 6 \n2. \nRFQ Preparation ..................................................................................................................... 6 \n2.1. \nRespondent to inform itself................................................................................................................... 6 \n3. \nRFQ Process ............................................................................................................................ 6 \n3.1. \nQuotation Lodgement ........................................................................................................................... 6 \n3.2. \nQuotation Validity Period ..................................................................................................................... 7 \n3.3. \nCorruption or Unethical Conduct ........................................................................................................ 7 \n3.4. \nNSW Procurement Policy Framework ................................................................................................... 7 \n3.5. \nCost of Respondent?s Participation in the RFQ ..................................................................................... 8 \n3.6. \nClarifications ......................................................................................................................................... 8 \n3.7. \nEvaluation Process ................................................................................................................................ 8 \n3.8. \nEvaluation Criteria ................................................................................................................................ 8 \n3.9. \nAcceptance or Rejection of Quotations ................................................................................................ 9 \n3.10. Late Quotations ................................................................................................................................... 9 \n3.11. Extension of the Closing Date and Time .............................................................................................. 9 \n3.12. Disclaimer ............................................................................................................................................ 9 \n3.13. Ownership ............................................................................................................................................ 9 \n3.14. Pricing ................................................................................................................................................... 9 \n3.15. Enquiries ............................................................................................................................................. 10 \n4. \nExchange of Information between Government Agencies .................................................. 10 \n5. \nDisclosure Information ....................................................................................................... 11 \n6. \nComplaints Procedures ....................................................................................................... 11 \nSCOPE OF WORKS ................................................................................................................ 12 \n1. \nIntroduction .......................................................................................................................... 12 \n2. \nScope .................................................................................................................................... 12 \n3. \nPerformance Management ................................................................................................ 17 \nATTACHMENTS ...................................................................................................................... 18 \n \n \n \n\nPage 4 of 18 \n \nRFQ OVERVIEW \n1. Introduction \n1.1. Background/Objectives \nThe purpose of this Request for Quotation (RFQ) is for the NSW Education Standards Authority \n(NESA) to seek quotations and assess the suitability of qualified suppliers to provide after-hours \nsecurity officer services. This RFQ is intended solely for the information of the party to whom it \nis addressed and no other person. Respondents should ensure that they read and understand \neach section of this RFQ. \n \nNESA is an independent statutory authority that reports to an independent Board and the NSW \nMinister for Education and Early Childhood Learning. NESA engages external security providers \nto deliver after-hours security officer services to support the safety and security of its staff, \nassets and premises outside standard business hours. After-hours services are required \ncontinuously from 5:00pm each weekday until 8:30am the following business day, including 24-\nhour coverage throughout the weekend from 5:00pm Friday until 8:30am Monday. Continuous \n24-hour security coverage is also required on all NSW public holidays applicable to the location \nof the premises. \n \nThe scope of this RFQ encompasses the engagement of a suitably qualified and experienced \nsupplier to provide after-hours security officer services. The required security services include: \n? \nProvision of appropriately licensed and trained security officers to monitor the premises, \naccess points, and security alarm systems. \n? \nConduct of regular patrols of internal and external areas of the premises. \n? \nPrompt response to incidents, emergencies, and security breaches. \n? \nAccurate and timely maintenance of incident reports, patrol logs, and other required \nrecords. \n? \nTimely reporting and escalation of incidents to relevant NESA stakeholders, as required. \nNESA seeks to establish a contract for the provision of reliable after-hours security officer services \nfor the period from 1 November 2026 to 30 June 2028. \nThe primary objectives to be achieved by the supplier in delivering the after-hours security officer \nservices are: \n? \nPossess the capability and capacity to deliver the services required by NESA. \n? \nEnsure that all security officers hold the appropriate licenses and have undergone suitable \npersonnel screening, including Working with Children Checks and National Police Checks, \nwhere required. \n? \nProvide a comprehensive service delivery plan that demonstrates compliance with NESA?s \nsecurity service requirements. \n? \nProvide appropriate equipment and uniforms to enable security officers to deliver the \nrequired security services to NESA. \n1.2. The Customer is responsible for the delivery of this RFQ process. \nFor the purposes of this RFQ the Principal of this Customer Agreement is the Customer \nHead/Secretary who will be a Customer under the NSW Whole of Government Integrated Security \nServices Contract 2007486.  References to ?Customer? in this RFQ means NSW Education \nStandards Authority. \n \nThe key outcome of this RFQ is to establish a competitive pricing framework for the identified \nServices which will deliver improved value to the NSW Education Standards Authority based on \n\nPage 5 of 18 \n \nservices, price and compliance with contract conditions. \n \nThis RFQ invites selected Service Providers appointed to the NSW Whole of Government \nIntegrated Security Services Contract 2007486 to supply the Services as listed in this RFQ. It is \nthe intention to appoint one contractor as the preferred contractor for the Services listed in this \nRFQ. \n1.3. Customer Agreement and Duration \nThere will be no binding agreement between the Principal and a Proponent in relation to the \nRFQ until a formal written Customer Agreement has been executed by the Principal and the \nsuccessful Proponent, or a formal Order has been issued by the Principal to the successful \nProponent. \n \nAgreement Term: \nFrom 1 November 2026 to 30 June 2028 \nExtension Option: \n1 year extension \nAnticipated Commencement Date: 1/11/2026 \n \n \n \n\nPage 6 of 18 \n \nRFQ CONDITIONS \n2. RFQ Preparation \n2.1.  Respondent to inform itself \nBefore submitting its quotation, a respondent must: \n1. \nExamine all information relevant to the risks and contingencies and other \ncircumstances having an effect on its Quotation; and \n2. \nSatisfy itself: \n(a) that the price is correct; and \n(b) that it is financially and practically viable for it to enter into and perform the \nCustomer Agreement to be established under NSW Whole of Government \nIntegrated Security Services Contract 2007486 via this Agreement (The Provision \nof After-Hours Security Officer Services at NESA?s Clarence Street Office / Tender \n538). \n3. RFQ Process \n3.1.  Quotation Lodgement \n3.1.1 Respondents are required to lodge their quotation electronically through the NSW \nGovernment Supplier Hub: https://buy.nsw.gov.au/suppliers (Login as a registered user, go to \nthe Tenders tile on your dashboard and follow the on-screen instructions to locate the \nopportunity and lodge the response). Quotations submitted electronically will be treated in \naccordance with the NSW Electronic Transactions Act 2000. \n3.1.2 The Respondent, by electronically submitting its quotation, is taken to have accepted the \nconditions shown herein and in the NSW Whole of Government Integrated Security \nServices Contract 2007486. \n3.1.3 The Customer may decline to consider for acceptance any quotations that cannot be \neffectively evaluated because they are incomplete or corrupted. \n3.1.4 A Respondent must observe the following format for submissions: \na. \nElectronically submitted quotations must be submitted in a file format, which can be \nread, formatted, displayed and printed by: \nMicrosoft Word *.doc, Microsoft Excel *.xls or Adobe Acrobat *.pdf  \nb. \nRespondents may opt to compress electronically submitted quotations in any format \nthat can be decompressed by WinZip. Respondents must not submit self-extracting \n(*exe) zip files; \nc. \nRespondents must not change existing text in the RFQ other than to insert/submit \nthe required information. \n3.1.5 Signatures are not required for quotations submitted to the email address. Respondents \nmust ensure that quotations are authorised by the person or persons who may do so on \nbehalf of the Respondent and appropriately identify the person and indicate the person?s \napproval of the information communicated. \n3.1.6 Electronically submitted quotations may be made corrupt or incomplete, for example by \ncomputer viruses. The Customer may decline to consider for acceptance any quotations \nthat cannot be effectively evaluated because they are incomplete or corrupt. Note that: \na. \nTo reduce the likelihood of viruses, Respondents must not include any macros, applets \nor executable code or files in the quotation; \n\nPage 7 of 18 \n \nb. \nRespondents should ensure that electronically submitted files are free from viruses \nby checking the files with an up-to-date virus checking program before \nsubmission; \n3.1.7 If a Respondent experiences any difficulty with the email or website address in submitting \na quotation or otherwise, it is encouraged to advise the Contact Officer. \n3.1.8 Quotations (including all supporting information, if any) must be submitted electronically \nin accordance with this RFQ and fully received by the Closing Date and Closing Time. \nRespondents must complete all of the Response document and must not amend any of the \nquestions provided. Prices, responses and other information provided in the quotation \nmust be in writing and in English. The quoted price must be in Australian dollars and \ninclusive of GST. \n3.1.9 Respondents should notify the Contact Officer in writing on or before the Closing Date and \nTime if they find any discrepancy, error or omission in this RFQ. \n3.2. Quotation Validity Period \n3.2.1 The quotation will remain open for acceptance by the Customer for a period of 12 months \nfrom the Closing Date and Time for quotations. \n3.3. Corruption or Unethical Conduct \n3.3.1 Respondents must comply with the requirements of the NSW Education Standards \nAuthority and NSW Supplier Code of Conduct, which is available at the link below and must \ndisclose any conflicts of interests in Response. \n3.3.2 If a respondent, or any of its officers, employees, agents or sub-contractors is found to have: \n(a) offered any inducement or reward to any public servant or employee, agent or \nsubcontractor of the Customer, Customer or the NSW Government in connection with \nthis RFQ or the submitted Quotation; \n(b) committed corrupt conduct in the meaning of the Independent Commission Against \nCorruption Act 1988; \n(c) a record or alleged record of unethical behaviour; or not complied with the \nrequirements \nof \nthe \nNSW \nSupplier \nCode \nof \nConduct \n(available \nat: \nhttps://buy.nsw.gov.au/policy-library/policies/supplier-code-of-conduct) \nthis may \nresult in the quotation not receiving further consideration. \n3.3.3 The Customer may, in its discretion, invite a relevant respondent to provide written \ncomments within a specified time before the Customer excludes the respondent on this \nbasis. \n3.3.4 If the Customer becomes aware of improper conflict of interests by a successful respondent \nafter an Agreement has been executed, then the Customer reserves the right to terminate \nthe General Conditions of Contract that has been made under it. \n3.4. NSW Procurement Policy Framework \n3.4.1 The \nNSW \nGovernment \nProcurement \nPolicy \nFramework \n(available \nat \nhttps://buy.nsw.gov.au/policy-library/policies/procurement-policy-framework) brings together \ninto one cohesive document the requirements for goods and services including \nconstruction procurement with the NSW Government. In submitting its quotation, the \nrespondent signifies agreement to comply with the Framework. \n3.4.2 Failure to comply with the Framework may be taken into account by the Customer when \nconsidering the respondent?s quotation or any subsequent quotation, and may result in the \nquotation being passed over. \n\nPage 8 of 18 \n \n3.5. Cost of Respondent?s Participation in the RFQ \n3.5.1 The respondent acknowledges that the Customer will not be liable to it for any expenses or \ncosts incurred by it as a result of its participation in this RFQ, including where the RFQ has \nbeen discontinued. \n3.6. Clarifications \n3.6.1 Respondents may, up to 3 business days prior to the Closing Date and Time, seek clarification \nof any aspect of this RFQ through the Contact Officer. Where a clarification is given to one \nRespondent this information will be provided to all other potential Respondents. \n3.7. Evaluation Process \n3.7.1 Respondent will be assessed against the evaluation criteria listed below, which are not indicated \nin order of any significance or equal weight. The evaluation criteria for this RFQ that do not \nrelate to price will account for 60% of the total evaluation score. The evaluation criteria for \nthis RFQ that relate to price will account for 40% of the total evaluation score. Information \nsupplied by the respondent in Response will contribute to the assessment against each \ncriterion. Any part of the response / information may be used to check with Referees for \nclarification. \n3.7.2 Respondents are advised to respond clearly to all the evaluation criteria listed in this RFQ. \nQuotations that do not include a fully completed Response, in particular those quotations which \ndo not contain sufficient information to permit a proper evaluation to be conducted, or \nelectronic quotations that cannot be effectively evaluated because the file has become \ncorrupt, may be excluded from the quotation process without further consideration at the \nCustomer?s discretion. \n3.8. Evaluation Criteria \n3.8.1 The evaluation criteria for this RFQ (which include but are not limited to) are: \n(a) Price \n(b) Non-Price Criteria \nThese criteria will be used to determine the quotation that represents the best value. \n3.8.2 The criteria listed here also apply to this RFQ:  \n? \nDemonstrated capability and capacity to deliver the services required.  \n? \nRelevant experience in delivering comparable services. \n? \nAll security officers assigned to deliver the services must hold a current NSW Security Master \nLicense. \n? \nEvidence that all security officers assigned to deliver the services, including relief security \nofficers, hold a current NSW Class 1A Security Officer license and current first aid \ncertification, and have completed relevant training in emergency response, access control, \npatrol procedures, incident reporting, escalation protocols and work health and safety. \n? \nProvide company status, including business size classification as small, medium or large, and \nidentification as an Aboriginal-owned business, where applicable. \n? \nDemonstrates the ability to provide a Service Delivery Plan detailing the approach to staffing \nand rostering, service continuity, site induction, lock-up procedures, patrols, access control, \nalarm and incident response, escalation, reporting, supervision and performance \nmonitoring. \n? \nProvide security equipment and uniforms that are functional, in good condition, \nprofessionally presented, and compliant with safety requirements.  \n\nPage 9 of 18 \n \n? \nEvidence that all security officers assigned to deliver the services, including relief personnel, \nhave completed a National Police Check and hold a current Working with Children Check \nwhere required by NESA, together with details of the respondent?s process for verifying and \nmonitoring these checks before personnel are deployed. \n? \nPrice  \n3.8.3 Please note that you must be on the NSW Whole of Government Integrated Security \nServices Contract 2007486 to be eligible to tender for this Contract. \n3.9. Acceptance or Rejection of Quotations \n3.9.1 The Customer is not bound to accept any Quote and may accept part or parts of a Quote. \nThe Customer may accept a number of quotes to ensure adequate services are available. \n3.9.2 The lowest priced Quote will not necessarily be accepted as the Customer is committed to \nseeking value for money. \n3.10.  Late Quotations \nIn accordance with the NSW Government Code of Tendering, late quotations will not be \naccepted, except where the integrity and competitiveness of the quotation process has not \nbeen compromised \n3.11. Extension of the Closing Date and Time \nThe Customer may, in its discretion, extend the Closing Date and Time. \n3.12. Disclaimer \nThe NSW Government is not committed contractually in any way to those organisations who \nare invited to Quote. The issue of this Request for Quote does not commit or otherwise \noblige the Government to proceed with any part or step of the process. \n3.13. Ownership \nAll information submitted in response to the Quote by any Respondents shall become the \nproperty of the Customer. \n3.14. Pricing \n3.14.1 Quoted rates shall be fixed rates for the first twelve months of the Customer Contract, shall    \nbe in Australian dollars and quoted as inclusive of GST. \n3.14.2 To the extent that the Services requested in this RFQ are covered by the Maximum Rates, \nthe Quotation must use the applicable Maximum Rates (or rates lower than the Maximum \nRates) to price the Services. \n3.14.3 To the extent that the requested Services are not covered by the Maximum Rates, the \nQuotation must use reasonable, market related rates or prices, which may include a \nreasonable amount for profit and overheads \n3.14.4 Respondents should bear in mind that the management fee as outlined in NSW Whole of \nGovernment Integrated Security Services Contract 2007486 is to be taken into account \nin pricing. This fee is payable by the respondent direct to NSW Public Works, Department \nof Primary Industries and Regional Development. \n3.14.5 Individual items not described separately in the Pricing Schedules and necessary for the \ncompletion of the works, or any part thereof, shall be deemed to be included in those \nitems listed in the Pricing Schedules. \n3.14.6 The Respondent shall satisfy itself as to the meaning of every item in the Pricing \nSchedules, and the prices included in these schedules shall be deemed to cover all the \nRespondent?s obligations in the undertaking, completion of the requirement. No claim \n \n\nPage 10 of 18 \n \nfor additional payment will be allowed for any error or misunderstanding by the \nRespondent in this respect. \n3.14.7 The Respondent shall be deemed to have: \n? \nExamined the RFQ and any other information made available in writing by the \nCustomer or Customer?s representative for the purpose of quoting; \n? \nExamined all further information relevant to the risks, contingencies, and other \ncircumstances having an effect on their Response which is obtainable by the making \nof reasonable inquires; and \n? \nSatisfied themselves as to the correctness and sufficiency of their Responses \nincluding quoted prices that shall be deemed to cover the cost of complying with all \nthe conditions of the RFQ and of all matters necessary for the due and proper \nperformance and completion of the work described in this Request for Quotation. \n3.15. Enquiries \n3.15.1 All enquiries in relation to this RFQ must be directed to the Contact Officer as follows: \nMichael Buraga \nFacilities Lead \nBusiness Operations \nLevel 4, 117 Clarence Street, Sydney, NSW, 2000 \n \n(02) 9367 8139 \nprocurement@nesa.nsw.edu.au \n3.15.2 Any information given to a respondent to clarify any aspect of this RFQ will also be given to \nall other respondents if in the Officer?s opinion the information would unfairly favour the \ninquiring respondent over other respondents. \n3.15.3 Questions/clarifications are to be forwarded formally via email latest 7 working days prior \nto the closing date of this RFQ and will be responded to by return email. \n3.15.4 The Respondent shall be deemed to have: \n? \nExamined the RFQ and any other information made available in writing by the \nCustomer or Customer?s representative for the purpose of quoting; \n? \nExamined all further information relevant to the risks, contingencies, and other \ncircumstances having an effect on their Response which is obtainable by the making \nof reasonable inquires; and \n? \nSatisfied themselves as to the correctness and sufficiency of their Responses \nincluding quoted prices that shall be deemed to cover the cost of complying with all \nthe conditions of the RFQ and of all matters necessary for the due and proper \nperformance and completion of the work described in this Request for Quotation. \n4. Exchange of Information between Government Agencies \n4.1.   \nBy lodging a quotation the respondent will authorise the Customer to make information \navailable, on request, to any NSW Government Customer. This includes information dealing with \nthe respondent?s performance on any prior contract that has been awarded. Such information \nmay be used by the recipient NSW Government Customer for assessment of the suitability for \npre-qualification, selective tender lists, expressions of interest or the award of a contract. \n \n \n \n\nPage 11 of 18 \n \n4.2.    \nThe provision of the information by the Customer to any other NSW Government Customer is \nagreed by the respondent to be a communication falling within section 30 of the Defamation \nAct 2005 (NSW), and the respondent shall have no claim against the Customer and the State of \nNew South Wales in respect of any matter arising out of the provision or receipt \nof such information, including any claim for loss to the respondent arising out of the \ncommunication. \n5. Disclosure Information \n5.1.   \nFollowing the Customer?s decision, all respondents will be notified in writing of the outcome of \ntheir quotations. \n5.2.   \nDetails of this quotation and the outcome of the quotation process will be disclosed in \naccordance with the Government Information (Public Access) Act (NSW) and the Premier?s \nMemorandum 2007-01. \n6. Complaints Procedures \n6.1.   \n In case of complaints and disputes, both the Customer and the Service Provider?s representative \nmust use their best efforts to resolve the matter at a local level. \nIf the complaint cannot be resolved locally between the Customer and the Service Provider, \nthe Customer can follow the issue resolution process which is available by contacting NSW \nPublic Works. \n \n \n\nPage 12 of 18 \n \nSCOPE OF WORKS \n1. Introduction \nThe purpose of this RFQ is to obtain quotations from appropriately qualified and experienced suppliers for \nthe provision of after-hours security officer services across the NSW Education Standards Authority?s (NESA) \npremises. \nNESA requires a reliable and professional security service to support the safety and security of its staff, \nvisitors, assets, and premises outside standard business hours. The services will be required continuously \nfrom 5:00pm each weekday until 8:30am the following business day, including 24-hour coverage on the \nweekends. Continuous 24-hour coverage will also be required on NSW public holidays applicable to the \nlocation of the premises. \nThe required services include maintaining an after-hours security presence, monitoring premises and access \npoints, completing lock-up procedures, conducting scheduled and randomised patrols, responding to alarms \nand security incidents, and escalating matters to NESA, building management or emergency services as \nrequired. Security officers assigned to deliver the services will also be responsible for maintaining accurate \npatrol logs, occurrence records, and incident reports. \nThe successful supplier must provide appropriately licensed, trained and screened security officers, \nincluding suitable arrangements for relief coverage and service continuity. Security officers assigned to \ndeliver the services must be equipped and uniformed to perform the required duties professionally and \nmust comply with NESA?s security procedures, site-specific Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs) and all \napplicable legislative and regulatory requirements. \nServices must be delivered consistently, with minimal disruption to NESA?s business operations. The \nresulting contract is intended to cover the period from 1 November 2026 to 30 June 2028, subject to the \nterms and conditions set out in this RFQ. \nSubmissions will be assessed against the requirements and evaluation criteria contained in this \ndocument, including the respondent?s capability and capacity, relevant experience, licensing, \nservice delivery approach, personnel arrangements, equipment, uniforms, and required screening \nchecks. \n2. Scope \nService Overview \nNESA requires a suitably qualified security provider to deliver reliable after-hours security services across \nits premises. The services are intended to protect NESA?s staff, visitors, information, assets and occupied \nareas, while supporting a safe and secure working environment outside standard business hours. \nThe successful supplier will provide appropriately licensed, trained and vetted security officers on \na daily basis during the hours specified by NESA. Services must be delivered in accordance with \nrelevant legislation, NSW Government requirements, NESA policies and procedures, and any site-\nspecific SOPs. \n \nAfter-Hours Security Services  \n \nThe security officer assigned to deliver the services will maintain a visible presence and monitor \nactivities within NESA?s premises throughout the rostered shift. The security officer will be \nresponsible for managing after-hours access, responding to enquiries, and identifying and \nreporting any unusual, unsafe or unauthorised activities. \n \nAt the conclusion of business operations, the officer will complete NESA?s approved lock-up procedures. \nThis will include checking nominated work areas, meeting rooms, kitchens, access points and other areas \n\nPage 13 of 18 \n \nunder NESA?s control; confirming that staff and visitors have departed where required; and ensuring \ndoors and other designated access points are appropriately secured. \n \nThe security officer will also monitor lift lobbies, entry and exit points, and other nominated areas \nrelevant to the security of NESA?s premises. Matters relating to common property or base-building \nservices must be reported to building management or another nominated contact in accordance \nwith the agreed escalation process. \n \nAdditional Security Personnel \n \nNESA may require additional security officers from time to time to support events, increased site activity, \nemergencies, heightened security risks or other operational requirements. Additional personnel may also \nbe requested in response to a matter raised by building management. \n \nIf required, the supplier must be able to provide suitably qualified security officer(s) who have a satisfactory \nNational Police Check with no recorded criminal history and hold a current Working with Children Check \ncertificate within the requested timeframe. Any additional services, including the required hours and \napplicable charges, must be authorised by NESA before they commence, except where immediate action is \nrequired to respond to an emergency or protect people or property. \n \nPatrols and Monitoring  \n \nThe security officer assigned to deliver the services will conduct scheduled and randomised patrols of the \ninternal and external areas identified by NESA. Patrol times should vary where practicable so that the \nsecurity pattern is not readily predictable.  \n \nDuring patrols, the security officer will check for evidence of unauthorised access, suspicious activity, \nunsecured doors, safety hazards, property damage, water leaks, unusual odours, lighting faults and other \nsecurity, safety or facilities-related issues. Fire exits and egress paths within NESA-controlled areas must \nalso be checked for obvious obstruction or interference. The security officer must use a patrol log to \nrecord the details of each patrol undertaken during the shift.  \n \nAny issue identified during a patrol must be addressed where it is safe and within the officer?s authority \nto do so. Otherwise, it must be secured where possible and promptly reported through the agreed \nescalation process.  \n \nAlarm and Incident Response  \n \nThe security officer assigned to deliver the services must respond promptly to alarms, security incidents, \nemergencies and irregular activity affecting NESA?s premises. Responses must be undertaken in \naccordance with the applicable SOPs, emergency procedures and instructions from NESA. \n \nDepending on the nature of the event, the security officer may be required to assess the immediate \nsituation, protect the affected area, assist staff or visitors, contact emergency services, notify building \nmanagement and escalate the matter to NESA?s nominated representatives. \n \nSecurity officer(s) must not place themselves or others at unreasonable risk and must remain within the \nduties and authority of their role. \n \nReporting and Records  \n \nThe supplier must maintain accurate records of the services delivered. This will include daily occurrence \nrecords, patrol logs, incident reports, alarm response records and any other documentation reasonably \nrequired by NESA. \n \nSerious or time-critical incidents must be reported immediately to the relevant NESA contact and, where \n\nPage 14 of 18 \n \napplicable, building management or emergency services. Written incident reports must be provided \nwithin the timeframe specified by NESA and include the time and location of the incident, persons \ninvolved, actions taken, notifications made and any recommended follow-up action. \n \nAll records must be clear, factual and handled in accordance with NESA?s privacy, confidentiality and \ninformation security requirements. \n \nSecurity Personnel  \n \nThe supplier must ensure that all security officers supplied to NESA hold all licences and certifications \nrequired to perform the services, including a current NSW Class 1A Security Officer licence and current \nfirst aid certification. The supplier must also ensure that all security officers have completed all required \npersonnel screening and probity checks, including Working with Children Checks and National Police \nChecks, where required by NESA or applicable legislation. \n \nThe Supplier must ensure that all security officers supplied to NESA have completed relevant and \nappropriate training in incident and emergency response, access control, patrol procedures, customer \nservice, report writing, escalation processes, and work health and safety. \n \nThe Supplier must ensure that all security officers supplied to NESA present professionally, wear an \napproved uniform and appropriate identification, and use equipment that is suitable for the duties being \nperformed. \n \nNESA expects the supplier to provide consistent personnel wherever reasonably practicable to support \nsite familiarity and service continuity. Relief personnel must be appropriately briefed and competent to \nundertake the required duties before commencing work at a NESA site. \n \nSite Procedures and Service Management \n \nBefore commencing service delivery, the supplier must work with NESA to review, develop and maintain \nsite-specific SOPs covering routine duties, lock-up arrangements, patrols, access control, alarm response, \nincident escalation, emergency contracts and reporting requirements. \n \nThe supplier will be responsible for supervising its personnel, monitoring service quality and addressing \nperformance issues promptly. It must ensure continuity of service during staff absences and other \nforeseeable disruptions, with replacement personnel meeting the same licensing, screening and \ncompetency requirements. \n \nServices must be delivered with minimal disruption to NESA?s operations and in a manner that reflects \nthe professional standards expected within a NSW Government workplace. \n \nSites and Areas Covered \n \nThe services will be provided at the NESA premises below. Unless otherwise directed by NESA, security \nofficers are responsible only for NESA-controlled areas and the nominated access points, lift lobbies and \nadjoining areas required to perform the services. \n \nMain Office: 117 Clarence Street, Sydney, NSW 2000. Levels 3-10 (Level 8 Suite 1 and Level 10 Suite 3) \n \nNESA may amend the areas requiring coverage during the contract period following changes to its \noccupancy, operational requirements or identified security risks. Any change affecting the agreed staffing \nlevels, service hours or price must be authorised by NESA in writing before implementation. \n \nService Hours and Staffing \n \nAfter-hours security services are required from 5:00pm each weekday until 8:30am the following \nbusiness day, including continuous 24-hour coverage on weekends. Continuous 24-hour coverage is also \n\nPage 15 of 18 \n \nrequired on all NSW public holidays applicable to the location of the premises. \n \nThe supplier must provide one security officer on site throughout each rostered shift. If required, NESA \nmay request an additional security officer. The supplier is responsible for arranging any relief coverage \nnecessary to meet this requirement at no additional cost to NESA. \n \nThe supplier must ensure that security officer(s) arrive on site at the scheduled commencement time of \neach shift. Security officer(s) must not leave the site before the scheduled finish time of their shift, or \nbefore an effective handover has occurred, unless authorised by NESA. \n \nIf any issues arrive, the supplier must immediately notify NESA and take prompt action to resolve the \nissue. \n \nShift Commencement and Handover \n \nThe supplier must ensure that, at the commencement of each shift, the incoming security officer reviews \nthe daily occurrence record, outstanding incidents, access arrangements, expected visitors and \ncontractors, alarm notifications, temporary instructions and any other matters relevant to the shift. \n \nThe supplier must ensure that, where one security officer replaces another, the outgoing and incoming \nsecurity officers complete a documented handover. The handover must identify any unresolved \nincidents, outstanding facilities or security issues, keys and equipment transferred, persons remaining on \nsite, and any instructions requiring action during the incoming shift. \n \nThe supplier must notify NESA?s nominated representative immediately if an officer is expected to arrive \nlate, cannot attend or must leave the premises unexpectedly. The supplier must arrange an appropriately \nlicensed, screened and site-inducted replacement immediately. \n \nKeys, Access Cards and Security Information \n \nThe supplier must ensure that all security officers supplied to NESA maintain secure custody of all keys, \naccess cards, alarm information, security codes and other security-related items provided by NESA. These \nitems must be recorded, stored securely and handed over at each shift change, where applicable. The \nsupplier must ensure that keys, access cards and security information are not copied, photographed, \nshared, removed from the premises or used for any purpose unrelated to the services. Any loss, damage, \nsuspected compromise or unauthorised disclosure must be reported to NESA immediately. \n \nThe supplier must ensure that all NESA-issued property is returned upon request, when a security officer \nis removed from the site, or at the conclusion of the contract. \n \nSupplier-Provided Equipment \n \nUnless expressly identified as being provided by NESA, the supplier must provide and maintain all \nequipment required for its security officers supplied to NESA to perform the services safely and \neffectively. This may include: \n? \nProfessional and clearly identifiable uniforms; \n? \nPhotographic identification; \n? \nReliable communication equipment; \n? \nTorches and replacement batteries or charging equipment; \n? \nAppropriate personal protective equipment; \n? \nNotebooks or electronic reporting devices; and \n? \nEquipment or systems used to record and verify patrols. \n\nPage 16 of 18 \n \nAll supplier-provided equipment must be fit for purpose, maintained in working order and replaced \npromptly if it becomes faulty, damaged or unavailable. The cost of supplying, maintaining and \nreplacing this equipment must be included in the quoted rates unless otherwise specified. \nNESA will provide an on-site mobile phone for receiving SMS alarm notifications and communicating \nwith nominated contacts. The supplier must ensure that security officers supplied to NESA check that \nthe phone is operational, adequately charged and in their possession at the beginning of each shift. \nAny fault, loss or damage must be reported immediately to NESA?s nominated representative. \nUniform and Presentation Standards \nThe supplier must ensure that security officers supplied to NESA wear the supplier?s approved \nuniform and photographic identification while on duty. Uniforms must be clean and professional, \nclearly identify the wearer as a security officer, and be appropriate for the premises and the duties \nbeing performed. \nThe supplier must ensure that security officers supplied to NESA maintain a professional standard of \nconduct and presentation when interacting with NESA staff, visitors, contractors, building \nmanagement and emergency services. \nBase-Building and Common-Area Boundaries \nThe supplier?s routine patrol and monitoring responsibilities are limited to NESA-controlled areas and \nany additional areas expressly nominated by NESA. \nUnless specifically included in the Site and Service Schedule or site-specific SOPs, the services do not \ninclude responsibility for managing or routinely patrolling base-building plant rooms, rooftops, \nloading docks, car parks, external building perimeters or other common areas controlled by building \nmanagement. \nThe supplier must ensure that, where a security officer supplied to NESA observes an incident, hazard \nor fault affecting a base-building or common area, the officer reports the matter to building \nmanagement and NESA in accordance with the agreed escalation process. Reporting such a matter \ndoes not transfer responsibility for the management, repair or control of the relevant area to the \nsupplier. \nAttendance, Absences and Service Continuity \nThe supplier must have a contingency plan in place to ensure uninterrupted and appropriate \ncoverage by licensed, screened and trained security officers throughout the contract period.  \nThe supplier must not deploy a security officer who has not completed NESA?s required induction or \nwho do not meet the licensing, screening and competency requirements of the RFQ. Any changes to \nregular security officers must be communicated to NESA in advance wherever practicable. \nA failure to attend, late attendance or uncovered shift must be reported immediately by the supplier \nto NESA?s nominated representative. The supplier must provide a replacement officer immediately \nafter becoming aware of the absence. \nMobilisation and Site Induction \nBefore commencing the services, the supplier must complete a mobilisation process in consultation \nwith NESA. This must include: \n? \nConfirming the regular security officers and relief security officers assigned to the contract; \n? \nProviding evidence of required licences, qualifications and screening checks for the security \nofficers supplied to NESA; \n? \nProviding the roster and escalation contacts; \n? \nCompleting site inductions. It is the supplier?s responsibility to ensure that the required NESA \nsite inductions are provided to all security officers supplied to NESA; \n\nPage 17 of 18 \n \n? \nReviewing NESA?s emergency and security procedures; \n? \nConfirming arrangements for keys, access cards, alarms and equipment; \n? \nReviewing and finalising the site-specific SOP?s; and \n? \nConfirming reporting, patrol logs, invoicing and contract-management arrangements. \nThe supplier must ensure that all security officers supplied to NESA complete the required NESA site \ninduction. No security officer may commence unsupervised duties at a NESA site until the required \ninduction has been completed and recorded. \nConfidentiality and Information Security \nSecurity officers supplied to NESA by the supplier may become aware of confidential information \nconcerning NESA?s operations, staff, visitors, security arrangements and premises. The supplier must \nensure that this information is accessed and used only where necessary to perform the services. \nThe supplier must ensure that security information, photographs, incident details, access \narrangements and information about NESA personnel must not be disclosed to any unauthorised \nperson or published through social media or any other channel. Any actual or suspected loss, misuse \nor unauthorised disclosure must be reported to NESA immediately.  \nAdditional Personnel and Variations \nRequests for additional security personnel must identify the location, required duties, number of \nofficers and expected service hours. The supplier must confirm availability, applicable rates and \nmobilisation time before the additional services commence. \nThe supplier must ensure that, except in an emergency requiring immediate action to protect people \nor property, any additional services and associated charges receive written authorisation from NESA.  \nThe supplier must also ensure that any request or operational direction from Building Management \nthat may result in an additional charge is referred to NESA for approval before the service is provided.  \nUnless specified otherwise by the Customer, the Statement of Requirements for the Whole of \nGovernment Integrated Security Services Contract 2007486 will apply to the provision of services. \n3. Performance Management \n3.1.   \nThe Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) identified below are in addition to KPIs in clauses 51.1 \nand 54 of Part C of the Statement of Requirements. \n? All required patrols and lock-up procedures must be completed during each shift. Compliance will be \nassessed through patrol records, lock-up records, and daily occurrence logs. \n? A complete and documented handover must occur at every shift change. Compliance will be assessed \nthrough handover records and daily occurrence logs. \n? Urgent operational matters must be acknowledged within 15 minutes, with the immediate action \ntaken or proposed course of action communicated to NESA within 30 minutes. Compliance will be \nassessed through emails, telephone records, incident reports, and escalation records. \n? Where a rostered security officer is unable to attend or must leave the premises unexpectedly, the \nsupplier must provide an appropriately licensed, screened and site-inducted replacement officer \nwithin 60 minutes of becoming aware of the absence. Compliance will be assessed through rosters, \nattendance records and notifications provided to NESA. \n? Where NESA requests additional security officers, the supplier must acknowledge the request and \nconfirm availability, applicable rates and anticipated mobilisation time within the timeframe agreed \nwith NESA. Compliance will be assessed through service requests and the supplier?s recorded \nresponses \n\nPage 18 of 18 \n \nATTACHMENTS \n? \nThe draft Customer Agreement for Security Services document (for use under the NSW Whole of \nGovernment Integrated Security Services Contract 2007486) is provided as a separate attachment. \n? \nThe Quotation Submission Form is attached as a separate document. The form must be completed as \npart of the respondent?s submission. \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'Quotation-Submission-Form.docx', 'file_type': 'docx', 'storage_uri': 'gs://tenderai-dev-documents/tenders/buynsw/Tender 853/c322c90147cb2310-Quotation-Submission-Form.docx', 'extracted_text': 'QUOTATION SUBMISSION FORM \nRequest For Quotation ? Tender 853: Provision of After-Hours Security Officer Services at NESA?s Clarence Street Office\nREPONSE\nRespondents must complete all sections of this Response Form.\nNo formatting changes are to be made.  \nRespondents may append reasonable supporting documentation where deemed necessary\nCompany Information\nDetailed Requirements\nIn response to the requirements specified by NESA in Section 2.\xa0Scope, of the\xa0Scope of Works\xa0section of the Request for Quotation under the NSW Whole of Government Integrated Security Services Contract 2007486 ? Tender 853:\xa0Provision of After-Hours Security Officer Services at NESA?s Clarence Street Office, Respondents must submit a completed version of the following table to NESA:\nInstructions for completing this table are as follows:\nEach section of Section 2. Scope, of the Scope of Works must be allocated a discrete row in the table, and be clearly identified in the ?Reference? column.\nState ?Yes? or ?No? whether you comply to that section\nProvide a detailed response to the section, demonstrating how you meet the requirement \nCorporate Governance\nValue-add Services\nPlease provide details of any value add services you are able to offer NESA\nPricing\nComplete Section 2 ? Pricing Structure\nCompliance with Terms of Draft Contract\nThe draft Customer Agreement for Security Services template (for use under the NSW Whole of Government Integrated Security Services Contract 2007486) is provided as a separate attachment.\nRespondents must provide a statement of acceptance of these terms and conditions and if not able to do so must then provide a full Schedule of Departures and Qualifications to these terms and conditions.\nAny such Schedule of Departures and Qualifications must:\n?\tProvide a clear and unambiguous reference to the source document (section, clause number etc.).\n?\tGive reasons for failing to agree to any specific terms and conditions.\nParticular attention is drawn to our requirement for Insurances and Indemnities.\n'}, {'file_name': 'The-draft-Customer-Agreement-for-Security-Services-document.pdf', 'file_type': 'pdf', 'storage_uri': 'gs://tenderai-dev-documents/tenders/buynsw/Tender 853/f520a2b5a31399e0-The-draft-Customer-Agreement-for-Security-Services-document.pdf', 'extracted_text': '?\n?\n?\nCustomer Agreement for Security \nServices  \n \n(for use under the NSW Whole of Government Integrated \nSecurity Services Contract 2007486) \n \n?????????????\n?\n????\n?\n?????????????????????\n?\n?\n?\n\n?\n?????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????\n?\n?\nContents \n??????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????? \n??????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????? \n?????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????? \n??????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????? \n? \n??????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????????